window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['jaesche/kemp'] = {"jaesche-s01-001":["Preface. [Jäsche]",[]],"jaesche-s01-002":["It is now a year and a half since Kant charged me with preparing his Logic for the press, as he had delivered it to his hearers in public lectures, and with presenting it to the public in the form of a compendious handbook. For this purpose I received from him the manuscript in his own hand which he had used in his lectures. He expressed at the same time a special and honourable confidence in me: that, being acquainted with the principles of his system in general, I should here also readily enter into his course of thought, and that I should not distort or falsify his thoughts, but should set them forth with the requisite clearness and precision and at the same time in their proper order. I have accepted this honourable commission, and have sought to carry it out as well as I was able, in accordance with the wish and expectation of the venerable sage, my much revered teacher and friend. In so doing, everything that concerns the exposition, the dress and the working out, the presentation and arrangement of the thoughts, is in part to be set down to my account. It is therefore naturally incumbent upon me to render the readers of this new work of Kant some account on this matter. Here, then, are one or two more detailed explanations on this point.",[]],"jaesche-s01-003":["Since the year 1765 Professor Kant had without interruption taken Meier’s textbook (Georg Friedrich Meier’s *Auszug aus der Vernunftlehre*, Halle, Gebauer, 1752) as the guide on which to base his lectures on logic. His reasons for doing so he set out in a programme which he published in 1765 to announce his lectures. The copy of this compendium which he used in his lectures is, like all the other textbooks he employed for the same purpose, interleaved with paper. His general remarks and elucidations, as well as the more special ones which relate directly to the text of the compendium in its several sections, are found partly on the interleaved paper and partly on the blank margins of the textbook itself. What is here and there set down in writing in these scattered remarks and elucidations together makes up the store of materials which Kant laid up for his lectures. From time to time he enlarged it with new ideas, and in respect of various particular topics he revised and improved it again and again. It therefore contains at least the essentials of all that the celebrated commentator on Meier’s textbook was accustomed to communicate on logic to his hearers in his lectures, which were delivered in a free manner, and which he had thought worth recording.",[]],"jaesche-s01-004":["As regards the presentation and arrangement of the matter in this work, I believed that I should best carry out the ideas and principles of the great man if, in respect of the economy and the division of the whole, I adhered to his express declaration. According to it, nothing further may be admitted into the treatise on logic proper, and especially into its Doctrine of Elements, than the theory of the three essential chief functions of thought: concepts, judgments and inferences. Everything, therefore, which treats merely of knowledge in general and of its logical perfections, and which in Meier’s textbook precedes the doctrine of concepts and takes up almost half of the whole, must on this view still be reckoned to the Introduction. “Previously,” Kant remarks at the very opening of the eighth section, in which his author expounds the doctrine of concepts, “Previously we treated of knowledge in general, as a propaedeutic to logic; now follows logic itself.”",[]],"jaesche-s01-005":["In accordance with this express indication I have therefore transferred into the Introduction everything that occurs before the section mentioned; and for this reason the Introduction has become much more extensive than it is usually found to be in other handbooks of logic. The consequence of this was that the Doctrine of Method, as the other chief part of the treatise, had to turn out the shorter, the more topics had already been dealt with in the Introduction, such as the doctrine of proofs and the like, which are now indeed rightly drawn by our more recent logicians into the province of the Doctrine of Method. It would have been a repetition as unnecessary as it would have been unseemly to mention these topics once more here in their proper place, merely in order to make complete what is incomplete and to put everything where it belongs. I have, however, done the latter in respect of the doctrine of definitions and of the logical division of concepts, which in Meier’s compendium already belongs to the eighth section, namely, to the Doctrine of Elements concerning concepts; and this order Kant also left unaltered in his lectures.",[]],"jaesche-s01-006":["It goes without saying that the great reformer of philosophy would have worked out logic, and indeed, as regards its economy and outer form, this part of theoretical philosophy in particular, according to his architectonic plan, the essential outlines of which are drawn in the *Critique of Pure Reason*, had it pleased him to do so. He would have done so had his task permitted him to think of working out a logic himself: the task, namely, of a scientific grounding of the entire system of philosophy proper, the philosophy of what is really true and certain, a task far more important and more difficult, which he alone could first carry out, and he alone in its originality. But this work he could very well leave to others, who, with insight and unprejudiced judgment, could make use of his architectonic ideas for a truly purposive and well-ordered treatment of this science. This was to be expected from several thorough and unprejudiced thinkers among our German philosophers; and this expectation has not deceived Kant and the friends of his philosophy. Several of the more recent textbooks of logic are, more or less, as regards the economy and disposition of the whole, to be regarded as a fruit of these Kantian ideas on logic. This science has thereby really gained. It has become neither richer, nor, properly speaking, more solid in its content or more grounded in itself; but it has been purified, partly of all elements foreign to it, partly of many useless subtleties and mere dialectical playthings, and it has become more systematic and yet, with all the scientific rigour of its method, at the same time simpler. Of this even the most cursory comparison of the older textbooks of logic with the newer ones, worked out on Kantian principles, must convince anyone who has but correct and clear concepts of the peculiar character and the rightful limits of logic. For however much many of the older handbooks of this science may be distinguished by scientific rigour of method, by clearness, precision and exactness in their definitions, and by cogency and evidence in their proofs, there is yet hardly one among them in which the boundaries of the various domains belonging to general logic in its wider extent, the merely propaedeutic, the dogmatic and the technical, the pure and the empirical, do not so run into and through one another that the one cannot be definitely distinguished from the other.",[]],"jaesche-s01-007":["Herr Jakob does indeed remark in the preface to the first edition of his Logic: “Wolff grasped the idea of a general logic admirably; and had this great man hit upon expounding pure logic entirely by itself, he would certainly, by virtue of his systematic mind, have given us a masterpiece which would have made all future works of this kind useless.” But he did not in fact carry out this idea, nor has any of his successors carried it out, however great and well-founded in general the merit may be which the Wolffian school has earned in regard to what is properly logical, the formal perfection of our philosophical knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s01-008":["Apart, however, from what could still be done, and had to be done, for the perfecting of logic in respect of its outer form, through the necessary separation of pure and merely formal propositions from empirical and real or metaphysical ones, Kant’s judgment is not doubtful when it is a question of appraising and determining the inner content of this science as a science. He repeatedly declared himself definitely and expressly on this point: that logic is to be regarded as a separate science, subsisting by itself and grounded in itself, and that consequently, from its origin and first elaboration by Aristotle down to our own times, it has properly been able to gain nothing in scientific grounding. In accordance with this assertion Kant therefore thought neither of grounding the logical principles of identity and contradiction themselves through a higher principle, nor of a deduction of the logical forms of judgments. He recognised and treated the principle of contradiction as a proposition which has its evidence in itself and needs no derivation from a higher principle. He restricted only the use, the validity, of this principle. He banished it from the domain of metaphysics, in which dogmatism sought to make it count, and confined it to the merely logical use of reason, as valid for this use alone.",[]],"jaesche-s01-009":["Whether, however, the logical principle of identity and contradiction is in itself and absolutely neither capable of nor in need of any further deduction, is indeed another question. It leads to the momentous question whether there is in general an absolutely first principle of all knowledge and science, whether such a principle is possible and can be found.",[]],"jaesche-s01-010":["The *Wissenschaftslehre* believes that it has discovered such a principle in the pure, absolute I, and has thereby completely grounded the whole of philosophical knowledge, not merely as to its form but also as to its content. On the presupposition of the possibility and apodeictic validity of this absolutely single and unconditioned principle, it therefore proceeds quite consistently when it does not allow the logical principles of identity and contradiction, the propositions A = A and –A = –A /, to hold as unconditioned, but declares them to be merely subaltern propositions, which can and must first be proved and determined through it and its supreme proposition: I am. (See *Grundlage der Wissenschaftslehre*, p. 13 etc.) In an equally consistent manner Schelling also, in his *System of Transcendental Idealism*, declares himself against presupposing the logical principles as unconditioned, that is, as not to be derived from any higher ones. For logic in general, he holds, can arise only through abstraction from determinate propositions, and, in so far as it arises in a scientific manner, only through abstraction from the supreme principles of knowledge; and consequently it already presupposes these highest principles of knowledge, and with them the *Wissenschaftslehre* itself. But since, on the other hand, these highest principles of knowledge, considered as principles, just as necessarily already presuppose the logical form, there arises from this that circle which cannot indeed be resolved for science, but can yet be explained. It is explained through the recognition of a first principle of philosophy which is first at once in form and in content (formal and material), in which both, form and content, mutually condition and ground each other. In this principle would then lie the point at which the subjective and the objective, identical and synthetic knowledge, would be one and the same.",[]],"jaesche-s01-011":["On the presupposition of such a dignity as must undoubtedly belong to such a principle, logic, like every other science, would accordingly have to be subordinated to the *Wissenschaftslehre* and its principles.",[]],"jaesche-s01-012":["However this may be, so much is settled: in any case logic remains unaltered within its own domain as regards what is essential. The transcendental question, whether the logical propositions are capable of, and in need of, a derivation from a higher absolute principle, can have as little influence upon logic itself, and upon the validity and evidence of its laws, as the transcendental problem, How are synthetic judgments *a priori* possible in mathematics?, has upon pure mathematics in respect of its scientific content. Just as the mathematician as mathematician, so also the logician as logician can pursue his course calmly and securely within the domain of his science in defining and proving, without needing to concern himself with the transcendental question, lying outside his sphere, which belongs to the transcendental philosopher and the teacher of the *Wissenschaftslehre*: How is pure mathematics, or pure logic, possible as science?",[]],"jaesche-s01-013":["Given this general recognition of the correctness of general logic, the dispute between the sceptics and the dogmatists over the ultimate grounds of philosophical knowledge has therefore never been conducted in the domain of logic, whose rules every reasonable sceptic acknowledged as valid no less than the dogmatist, but always in the domain of metaphysics. And how could it be otherwise? The highest problem of philosophy proper concerns, after all, not subjective but objective knowledge, not identical but synthetic knowledge. Here, therefore, logic as such is entirely out of play. It could not have occurred either to the Critique or to the *Wissenschaftslehre*, nor can it ever occur to any philosophy which knows how to distinguish definitely the transcendental standpoint from the merely logical, to seek the ultimate grounds of real philosophical knowledge within the domain of mere logic, and to try to pick a real object out of a proposition of logic considered merely as such.",[]],"jaesche-s01-014":["Whoever has definitely grasped, and never again loses sight of, the vast difference between logic proper (general logic), as a merely formal science, the science of mere thinking considered as thinking, and transcendental philosophy, the one material or real pure science of reason, the science of knowledge proper, will therefore easily judge what is to be thought of the recent attempt which Herr Bardili has lately undertaken (in his *Grundriss der ersten Logik*). He seeks to establish a *prius* for logic itself, in the expectation of finding by way of this inquiry “a real object, either posited through it (mere logic), or else none whatever positable; the key to the essence of nature either given through it, or else no logic and no philosophy whatever possible.” In truth it is not to be seen in what possible way Herr Bardili could find a real object from the *prius* of logic which he has set up, the principle of the absolute possibility of thinking, according to which we can repeat One as One and the Selfsame in the Many (not the manifold) an infinite number of times. This supposedly newly discovered *prius* of logic is plainly neither more nor less than the old, long-recognised principle of identity, which lies within the domain of logic and is placed at the head of this science: What I think, I think; and just this and nothing else I can now think repeated to infinity. — Who indeed, in the logical principle of identity rightly understood, will think of a manifold, and not of a mere many, which certainly neither arises nor can arise otherwise than through the mere repetition of one and the selfsame thinking, the mere repeated positing of an A = A = A, and so on to infinity? It is therefore hardly likely that, on the path which Herr Bardili has taken, and by the heuristic method he has employed for this purpose, that should be found which concerns philosophising reason: the starting-point and end-point from which it can set out in its inquiries and to which it can return again. The chief and most significant objections which Herr Bardili raises against Kant and his method of philosophising could therefore strike not so much Kant the logician as rather Kant the transcendental philosopher and metaphysician. We can there|fore",[]],"jaesche-s01-015":["here leave them all aside in their proper place.",[]],"jaesche-s01-016":["Finally, I would further remark here that I shall work up and publish Kant’s Metaphysics in the same manner, as soon as leisure permits; the manuscript for it is likewise already in my hands. Königsberg, 20th September 1800.",[]],"jaesche-s01-017":["Gottlob Benjamin Jäsche,",[]],"jaesche-s01-018":["Doctor and *Privatdocent* in Philosophy",[]],"jaesche-s01-019":["at the University of Königsberg,",[]],"jaesche-s01-020":["Member of the Learned Society",[]],"jaesche-s01-021":["of Frankfurt on the Oder.",[]],"jaesche-s02-001":["Introduction.",[]],"jaesche-s02-002":["I. Concept of Logic.",[]],"jaesche-s02-003":["Everything in nature, in the lifeless as well as in the living world, takes place according to rules, although we do not always know these rules. — Water falls according to laws of gravity, and in animals the motion of walking also takes place according to rules. The fish in the water, the bird in the air, moves according to rules. The whole of nature in general is, properly speaking, nothing but a connection of appearances according to rules; and there is nowhere any absence of rule. When we think we find such a thing, we can in that case only say that the rules are unknown to us.",[]],"jaesche-s02-004":["The exercise of our powers also takes place according to certain rules which we follow, at first without being conscious of them, until we gradually come to know them through trials and a lengthy use of our powers. Indeed, in the end we become so familiar with them that it costs us much effort to think them *in abstracto*. Thus, for example, universal grammar is the form of a language in general. But one speaks even without knowing grammar; and he who speaks without knowing it does really have a grammar, and speaks according to rules, of which, however, he is not conscious.",[]],"jaesche-s02-005":["Now just as all our powers taken together, so the understanding in particular is bound in its actions to rules which we can investigate. Indeed, the understanding is to be regarded as the source and the faculty of thinking rules in general. For as sensibility is the faculty of intuitions, so the understanding is the faculty of thinking, that is, of bringing the representations of the senses under rules. It is therefore eager to seek rules, and satisfied when it has found them. Since the understanding is the source of rules, the question therefore arises: according to what rules does it itself proceed?",[]],"jaesche-s02-006":["For there can be no doubt whatever that we cannot think, or use our understanding, otherwise than according to certain rules. These rules, however, we can in turn think by themselves, that is, we can think them apart from their application, or *in abstracto*. What, then, are these rules?",[]],"jaesche-s02-007":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-008":["All rules according to which the understanding proceeds are either necessary or contingent. The former are those without which no use of the understanding whatever would be possible; the latter, those without which a certain determinate use of the understanding would not take place. The contingent rules, which depend upon a determinate object of knowledge, are as manifold as these objects themselves. Thus there is, for example, a use of the understanding in mathematics, in metaphysics, in morals, and so on. The rules of this particular, determinate use of the understanding in the sciences mentioned are contingent, because it is contingent whether I think this or that object to which these particular rules relate.",[]],"jaesche-s02-009":["If, however, we set aside all knowledge which we must borrow merely from objects, and reflect solely upon the use of the understanding in general, we discover those rules of it which are absolutely necessary in every respect and without regard to any particular objects of thought, because without them we should not think at all. These rules can therefore be discerned *a priori*, that is, independently of all experience, because, without distinction of objects, they contain merely the conditions of the use of the understanding in general, whether that use be pure or empirical. And from this it follows at the same time that the universal and necessary rules of thought in general can concern solely its form, and in no way its matter. Accordingly the science which contains these universal and necessary rules is merely a science of the form of the knowledge of our understanding, or of thinking. We can therefore form for ourselves an idea of the possibility of such a science, just as of a universal grammar, which contains nothing beyond the mere form of language in general, without the words, which belong to the matter of language.",[]],"jaesche-s02-010":["This science of the necessary laws of the understanding and of reason in general, or, what is the same thing, of the mere form of thinking in general, we entitle logic.",[]],"jaesche-s02-011":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-012":["As a science which bears upon all thinking in general, without regard to objects as the matter of thinking, logic is",[]],"jaesche-s02-013":["1) to be regarded as the foundation of all other sciences and as the propaedeutic of all use of the understanding. But just because it abstracts entirely from all objects, it can",[]],"jaesche-s02-014":["2) be no organon of the sciences.",[]],"jaesche-s02-015":["By an organon we understand, namely, an instruction how a certain [mode of] knowledge is to be brought about. This, however, requires that I already know the object of the knowledge which is to be produced according to certain rules. An organon of the sciences is therefore not mere logic, since it presupposes exact acquaintance with the sciences, their objects and their sources. Thus mathematics, for example, is an excellent organon, as a science which contains the ground of the extension of our knowledge in respect of a certain use of reason. Logic, on the other hand, since as the universal propaedeutic of all use of the understanding and of reason in general it may not enter into the sciences and anticipate their matter, is only a universal art of reason (*canonica Epicuri*), the art of making modes of knowledge in general conform to the form of the understanding. It is therefore to be entitled an organon only in this sense, an organon which serves, admittedly, not for the extension but merely for the appraisal and correction of our knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s02-016":["3) As a science of the necessary laws of thinking, however, logic is a canon. These laws are those without which no use of the understanding and of reason whatever takes place, and they are consequently the conditions under which alone the understanding can and ought to agree with itself: the necessary laws and conditions of its correct use. As a canon of the understanding and of reason, logic may therefore borrow no principles either from any science or from any experience; it must contain nothing but laws *a priori*, which are necessary and bear upon the understanding in general.",[]],"jaesche-s02-017":["Some logicians, it is true, presuppose psychological principles in logic. But to bring such principles into logic is as absurd as to derive morals from life. Were we to take the principles from psychology, that is, from observations about our understanding, we should merely see how thinking goes on, and how it is under the manifold subjective hindrances and conditions; and this would therefore lead to the knowledge of merely contingent laws. In logic, however, the question is not of contingent but of necessary rules; not how we think, but how we ought to think. The rules of logic must therefore be derived not from the contingent but from the necessary use of the understanding, which one finds in oneself without any psychology. In logic we wish to know not how the understanding is and thinks, and how it has hitherto proceeded in thinking, but how it ought to proceed in thinking. Logic is to teach us the correct use of the understanding, that is, the use which agrees with itself.",[]],"jaesche-s02-018":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-019":["From the definition of logic here given the remaining essential properties of this science can now also be derived, namely, that it is",[]],"jaesche-s02-020":["4) a science of reason not merely as to form but as to matter, since its rules are not derived from experience and since it has at the same time reason for its object. Logic is therefore a self-knowledge of the understanding and of reason, not, however, as regards their capacities in respect of objects, but solely as to form. In logic I shall not ask: What does the understanding know, and how much can it know, or how far does its knowledge extend? For that would be self-knowledge in respect of its material use, and so belongs to metaphysics. In logic the only question is: How will the understanding know itself?",[]],"jaesche-s02-021":["As a science rational both as to matter and as to form, logic is finally also",[]],"jaesche-s02-022":["5) a doctrine, or demonstrated theory. For since it is occupied not with the common use of the understanding and of reason, which as such is merely empirical, but solely with the universal and necessary laws of thinking in general, it rests upon principles *a priori*, from which all its rules can be derived and proved, as rules to which all knowledge of reason would have to conform.",[]],"jaesche-s02-023":["In that logic is to be regarded as a science *a priori*, or as a doctrine for a canon of the use of the understanding and of reason, it is essentially distinguished from aesthetic. Aesthetic, as mere critique of taste, has no canon (law), but only a norm (a pattern or guiding line merely for appraisal), which consists in universal agreement. Aesthetic, that is to say, contains the rules of the agreement of knowledge with the laws of sensibility; logic, on the other hand, the rules of the agreement of knowledge with the laws of the understanding and of reason. The former has only empirical principles, and so can never be science or doctrine, if by doctrine we understand a dogmatic instruction from principles *a priori*, in which we discern everything through the understanding, without other lessons obtained from experience, and which gives us rules the following of which procures the required perfection.",[]],"jaesche-s02-024":["Many, especially orators and poets, have tried to reason subtly about taste, but they have never been able to pronounce a decisive judgment upon it. The philosopher Baumgarten in Frankfurt had drawn up the plan of an aesthetic as a science. But Home has more correctly entitled aesthetic critique, since it gives no rules *a priori* which sufficiently determine the judgment, as logic does, but derives its rules *a posteriori*, and only by comparison makes more general the empirical laws according to which we know the less perfect and the more perfect (the beautiful).",[]],"jaesche-s02-025":["Logic is therefore more than mere critique; it is a canon which afterwards serves for critique, that is, as the principle for appraising all use of the understanding in general, though only its correctness in respect of mere form, since it is no organon, any more than is universal grammar.",[]],"jaesche-s02-026":["As the propaedeutic of all use of the understanding in general, general logic is at the same time also distinguished, from another side, from transcendental logic. In the latter the object itself is represented as an object of the mere understanding; general logic, on the other hand, bears upon all objects in general.",[]],"jaesche-s02-027":["If we now gather together all the essential marks which belong to the complete determination of the concept of logic, we shall have to set up the following concept of it:",[]],"jaesche-s02-028":["Logic is a science of reason not merely as to form but as to matter; a science *a priori* of the necessary laws of thinking, not, however, in respect of particular objects, but of all objects in general; — a science, therefore, of the correct use of the understanding and of reason in general, not subjectively, that is, not according to empirical (psychological) principles, how the understanding thinks, but objectively, that is, according to principles *a priori*, how it ought to think.",[]],"jaesche-s02-029":["[Introduction.] II. Chief Divisions of Logic.",[]],"jaesche-s02-030":["— Exposition. — Utility of this Science. — Outline of a History of it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-031":["Logic is divided",[]],"jaesche-s02-032":["1) into Analytic and Dialectic.",[]],"jaesche-s02-033":["Analytic discovers, by analysis, all the actions of reason which we perform in thinking in general. It is therefore an analytic of the form of understanding and of reason, and is also rightly entitled the logic of truth, because it contains the necessary rules of all (formal) truth, without which our knowledge, irrespective of its objects, is untrue even in itself. It is therefore also nothing more than a canon for *dijudication* (of the formal correctness of our knowledge).",[]],"jaesche-s02-034":["Were one to use this merely theoretical and universal doctrine as a practical art, that is, as an organon, it would become dialectic: a logic of illusion (*ars sophistica, disputatoria*), which arises from a mere misuse of analytic, in so far as, according to the mere logical form, the illusion of true knowledge is artificially produced, although the marks of such knowledge must be derived from agreement with objects, and so from content.",[]],"jaesche-s02-035":["In former times dialectic was studied with great diligence. This art propounded false principles under the illusion of truth, and sought in accordance with them to maintain things in semblance. Among the Greeks the dialecticians were the advocates and orators, who could lead the people wherever they wished, because the people allow themselves to be deceived by illusion. Dialectic was therefore at that time the art of illusion. In logic, too, it was for a time expounded under the name of the art of disputation; and so long as this was so, all logic and philosophy was the cultivation, by certain garrulous minds, of the art of artificially producing every kind of illusion. But nothing can be more unworthy of a philosopher than the cultivation of such an art. In this meaning, therefore, it must be entirely dropped, and in its place a critique of this illusion must rather be introduced into logic.",[]],"jaesche-s02-036":["We should accordingly have two parts of logic: analytic, which would set forth the formal criteria of truth, and dialectic, which would contain the marks and rules by which we could know that something does not agree with the formal criteria of truth, although it seems to agree with them. Dialectic in this meaning would therefore have its proper use as a cathartic of the understanding.",[]],"jaesche-s02-037":["It is customary, further, to divide logic",[]],"jaesche-s02-038":["2) into natural or popular logic and artificial or scientific logic (*logica naturalis, log. scholastica s. artificialis*).",[]],"jaesche-s02-039":["But this division is inadmissible. For natural logic, or the logic of common reason (*sensus communis*), is properly no logic, but an anthropological science, which has only empirical principles, since it treats of the rules of the natural use of the understanding and of reason, which are known only *in concreto*, and so without consciousness of them *in abstracto*. — Artificial or scientific logic alone therefore deserves this name, as a science of the necessary and universal rules of thinking, which can and must be known *a priori*, independently of the natural use of the understanding and of reason *in concreto*, although they can at first be found only through observation of that natural use.",[]],"jaesche-s02-040":["3) Yet another division of logic is that into theoretical and practical logic. But this division also is incorrect.",[]],"jaesche-s02-041":["General logic, which, as a mere canon, abstracts from all objects, can have no practical part. That would be a *contradictio in adjecto*, since a practical logic presupposes acquaintance with a certain kind of objects to which it is applied. We can therefore entitle every science a practical logic; for in every science we must have a form of thinking. General logic, considered as practical, can therefore be nothing more than a technique of learning in general, an organon of scholastic method.",[]],"jaesche-s02-042":["In accordance with this division logic would therefore have a dogmatic and a technical part. The first could be entitled the Doctrine of Elements, the other the Doctrine of Method. The practical or technical part of logic would be a logical art in respect of arrangement and of logical technical terms and distinctions, in order thereby to facilitate the understanding in its action.",[]],"jaesche-s02-043":["In both parts, however, the technical as well as the dogmatic, not the least regard might be paid either to objects or to the subject of thinking. In the latter respect logic could be divided",[]],"jaesche-s02-044":["4) into pure and applied logic.",[]],"jaesche-s02-045":["In pure logic we separate the understanding from the other powers of the mind, and consider what it does by itself alone. Applied logic considers the understanding in so far as it is intermingled with the other powers of the mind. These influence its actions and give it a wrong direction, so that it does not proceed according to the laws which it itself quite well recognises to be the right ones. Applied logic ought not, properly speaking, to be entitled logic. It is a psychology, in which we consider how our thinking is wont to proceed, not how it ought to proceed. It does indeed in the end tell us what we ought to do in order to make a right use of the understanding under various subjective hindrances and limitations. We can also learn from it what furthers the right use of the understanding, that is, the aids to that use, or the remedies for logical faults and errors. But it is not, for all that, a propaedeutic. For psychology, from which everything in applied logic has to be taken, is a part of the philosophical sciences, and logic is meant to be the propaedeutic to these sciences.",[]],"jaesche-s02-046":["It is said, indeed, that technique, that is, the manner of building a science, ought to be expounded in applied logic. This, however, is futile, and is even harmful. One then begins to build before one has the materials. One may perhaps supply the form, but the content is lacking. Technique must be expounded in connection with each science [by itself].",[]],"jaesche-s02-047":["Finally, as regards",[]],"jaesche-s02-048":["5) the division of logic into the logic of the common and the logic of the speculative understanding, we observe that this science cannot be so divided at all.",[]],"jaesche-s02-049":["It cannot be a science of the speculative understanding. For as a logic of speculative knowledge, or of the speculative employment of reason, it would be an *organon* of other sciences, and not a mere propaedeutic, such as must concern every possible employment of understanding and of reason.",[]],"jaesche-s02-050":["Just as little can logic be a product of the common understanding. For the common understanding is the faculty of discerning the rules of knowledge *in concreto*; whereas logic is meant to be a science of the rules of thinking *in abstracto*.",[]],"jaesche-s02-051":["We may, however, take the universal human understanding as the object of logic; and in so far logic will abstract from the special rules of speculative reason, and will thus be distinguished from the logic of the speculative understanding.",[]],"jaesche-s02-052":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-053":["As regards the exposition [Vortrag] of logic, it may be either scholastic or popular.",[]],"jaesche-s02-054":["It is scholastic in so far as it is suited to the desire for knowledge, the capacities and the culture of those who wish to treat the knowledge of logical rules as a science. It is popular when it condescends to the capacities and needs of those who do not wish to study logic as a science, but only to use it in order to enlighten their understanding. In scholastic exposition the rules must be presented in their universality, or *in abstracto*; in popular exposition, on the other hand, in the particular, or *in concreto*. Scholastic exposition is the foundation of popular exposition. For only he can expound something in a popular manner who could also expound it more thoroughly.",[]],"jaesche-s02-055":["We here distinguish, further, exposition from method. By method is to be understood the manner in which a certain object, to the knowledge of which the method is to be applied, is to be completely known. It must be derived from the nature of the science itself; and since it is an order of thinking which is thereby determined and necessary, it does not admit of alteration. Exposition signifies only the manner of communicating one's thoughts to others, in order to make a doctrine intelligible.",[]],"jaesche-s02-056":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-057":["From what we have so far said regarding the nature and the end of logic, the value of this science and the utility of its study can now be estimated by a correct and determinate standard.",[]],"jaesche-s02-058":["Logic, then, is not indeed a universal art of discovery, nor an *organon* of truth. It is not an algebra, by whose aid hidden truths might be discovered.",[]],"jaesche-s02-059":["It is, however, useful and indispensable as a criticism of knowledge, that is, for passing judgment upon common as well as upon speculative reason, not in order to teach reason, but only in order to make it correct and consistent with itself. For the logical principle of truth is the agreement of the understanding with its own universal laws.",[]],"jaesche-s02-060":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-061":["Finally, as regards the history of logic, we shall cite only the following.",[]],"jaesche-s02-062":["Present-day logic derives from Aristotle's Analytic. This philosopher may be regarded as the father of logic. He expounded it as an *organon*, and divided it into analytic and dialectic. His mode of teaching is very scholastic, and is directed to the development of the most universal concepts which underlie logic. From this, however, we derive no benefit, since almost everything comes down to mere subtleties, except that the names of various acts of the understanding have been drawn from it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-063":["Logic, moreover, has not gained much in content since Aristotle's time, nor by its nature can it do so. But it may well gain in respect of exactness, determinateness and distinctness. There are only a few sciences which can attain to a permanent state in which they undergo no further change. Logic belongs to these, and so does metaphysics. Aristotle had omitted no moment of the understanding; we are in this only more exact, more methodical and more orderly.",[]],"jaesche-s02-064":["It was indeed believed of Lambert's Organon that it would greatly enlarge logic. But it contains nothing more than subtler divisions, which, like all correct subtleties, may sharpen the understanding, but are of no essential use.",[]],"jaesche-s02-065":["Among more recent philosophers there are two who have set universal logic in motion: Leibniz and Wolff.",[]],"jaesche-s02-066":["Malebranche and Locke have not treated of logic proper, since they deal also with the content of knowledge and the origin of concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s02-067":["The universal logic of Wolff is the best we have. Some have combined it with the Aristotelian logic, as, for instance, Reusch.",[]],"jaesche-s02-068":["Baumgarten, a man of great merit in this field, condensed the Wolffian logic; and Meier in turn wrote a commentary upon Baumgarten.",[]],"jaesche-s02-069":["Among the more recent logicians is also Crusius, who, however, did not consider what the nature of logic really is. For his logic contains metaphysical principles, and in so far oversteps the limits of this science. It sets up, moreover, a criterion of truth which cannot be a criterion, and in so far gives free rein to every kind of extravagance [Schwärmerei].",[]],"jaesche-s02-070":["In the present age there has been no logician of particular fame; nor do we need any new discoveries for logic, since it contains merely the form of thinking.",[]],"jaesche-s02-071":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-072":["[Introduction.] III. Concept of Philosophy in General.",[]],"jaesche-s02-073":["— Philosophy considered according to the scholastic concept and according to the cosmical concept [Weltbegriff]. — Essential requirements and ends of philosophising. — The most universal and highest problems of this science.",[]],"jaesche-s02-074":["It is sometimes difficult to explain what is meant by a science. But a science gains in precision through the fixing of its determinate concept; and in this way many errors are, for certain reasons, avoided, which otherwise creep in when we are not yet able to distinguish the science from the sciences allied to it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-075":["Before attempting to give a definition of philosophy, however, we must first examine the character of the various modes of knowledge themselves; and since philosophical modes of knowledge belong to the modes of knowledge through reason, we must in particular explain what is to be understood by the latter.",[]],"jaesche-s02-076":["Modes of knowledge through reason are opposed to historical modes of knowledge. The former are modes of knowledge from principles (*ex principiis*); the latter, modes of knowledge from data (*ex datis*). But a mode of knowledge may have arisen from reason and yet be historical. When, for instance, a mere man of letters learns the products of another's reason, his knowledge of such products of reason is merely historical.",[]],"jaesche-s02-077":["For modes of knowledge may be distinguished",[]],"jaesche-s02-078":["1) according to their objective origin, that is, according to the sources from which alone a mode of knowledge is possible. In this respect all modes of knowledge are either rational or empirical;",[]],"jaesche-s02-079":["2) according to their subjective origin, that is, according to the manner in which a mode of knowledge can be acquired by men. Considered from this latter point of view, modes of knowledge are either rational or historical, however they may in themselves have arisen. Thus something may objectively be a mode of knowledge through reason which subjectively is yet only historical.",[]],"jaesche-s02-080":["In the case of some rational modes of knowledge it is harmful to know them merely historically; in the case of others it is a matter of indifference. The mariner, for instance, knows the rules of navigation historically from his tables, and that is sufficient for him. But if the jurist knows jurisprudence merely historically, he is wholly spoiled for being a genuine judge, and still more for being a legislator.",[]],"jaesche-s02-081":["From the distinction just stated between objectively and subjectively rational modes of knowledge it is now also evident that one may in a certain respect learn philosophy without being able to philosophise. He, therefore, who would really become a philosopher must practise himself in making a free use of his reason, and not a merely imitative and, so to speak, mechanical use.",[]],"jaesche-s02-082":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-083":["We have defined modes of knowledge through reason as modes of knowledge from principles; and it follows that they must be *a priori*. But there are two kinds of knowledge which are both *a priori* and yet have many notable differences, namely, mathematics and philosophy.",[]],"jaesche-s02-084":["It is commonly maintained that mathematics and philosophy are distinguished from one another in respect of their object, the former treating of quantity, the latter of quality. All this is false. The difference between these sciences cannot rest on the object. For philosophy is concerned with everything, and therefore also with *quanta*; and so in part is mathematics, in so far as everything has a magnitude. It is solely the different mode of knowledge through reason, or of the employment of reason, in mathematics and in philosophy that constitutes the specific difference between these two sciences. Philosophy is knowledge through reason from mere concepts; mathematics, on the other hand, is knowledge through reason from the construction of concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s02-085":["We construct concepts when we exhibit them in intuition *a priori*, without experience; or, [in other words,] when we exhibit in intuition the object which corresponds to our concept of it. The mathematician can never make use of his reason according to mere concepts, nor the philosopher of his through the construction of concepts. In mathematics reason is employed *in concreto*; the intuition, however, is not empirical: here we make something *a priori* the object of intuition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-086":["In this, then, as we see, mathematics has an advantage over philosophy, that the modes of knowledge of the former are intuitive, those of the latter only discursive. The reason why in mathematics we consider magnitudes more [than qualities] lies in this, that magnitudes can be constructed in intuition *a priori*, whereas qualities do not allow of being exhibited in intuition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-087":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-088":["Philosophy, then, is the system of philosophical modes of knowledge, or of modes of knowledge through reason from concepts. This is the scholastic concept of this science. According to the cosmical concept [Weltbegriff], it is the science of the ultimate ends of human reason. This lofty concept confers dignity, that is, an absolute worth, upon philosophy. And in|deed",[]],"jaesche-s02-089":["it is philosophy alone which has inner worth, and which first gives worth to all other modes of knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s02-090":["We do always ask in the end: to what purpose does philosophising serve, and to what purpose its final end, philosophy itself, considered as a science according to the scholastic concept?",[]],"jaesche-s02-091":["In this scholastic meaning of the word, philosophy is directed only to skill; in relation to the cosmical concept, on the other hand, to usefulness. In the former respect it is thus a doctrine of skill; in the latter, a doctrine of wisdom, the lawgiver of reason. And the philosopher is in so far not an artificer of reason [Vernunftkünstler], but a lawgiver.",[]],"jaesche-s02-092":["The artificer of reason, or, as Socrates calls him, the *philodox*, strives merely for speculative knowledge, without regard to how much that knowledge contributes to the ultimate end of human reason. He gives rules for the employment of reason in the service of all kinds of arbitrary ends. The practical philosopher, the teacher of wisdom through doctrine and example, is the philosopher proper. For philosophy is the idea of a perfect wisdom, which shows us the ultimate ends of human reason.",[]],"jaesche-s02-093":["To philosophy according to the scholastic concept two things belong:",[]],"jaesche-s02-094":["first, a sufficient store of modes of knowledge through reason; secondly, a systematic connection of these modes of knowledge, that is, their combination in the idea of a whole.",[]],"jaesche-s02-095":["Not only does philosophy allow of such a strictly systematic connection; it is even the only science which has systematic connection in the most proper sense, and which gives systematic unity to all other sciences.",[]],"jaesche-s02-096":["As regards philosophy according to the cosmical concept (*in sensu cosmico*), it may also be entitled a science of the highest maxim of the employment of our reason, if by maxim we understand the inner principle of choice among different ends.",[]],"jaesche-s02-097":["For philosophy in the latter meaning is indeed the science of the relation of all knowledge and of every employment of reason to the final end of human reason. To this end, as the supreme end, all other ends are subordinated, and in it they must be united into unity.",[]],"jaesche-s02-098":["The field of philosophy in this cosmopolitan [weltbürgerlich] meaning may be reduced to the following questions: 1) What can I know? 2) What ought I to do? 3) What may I hope? 4) What is man?",[]],"jaesche-s02-099":["The first question is answered by metaphysics, the second by morals, the third by religion, and the fourth by anthropology. Fundamentally, however, all this might be reckoned as anthropology, since the first three questions relate to the last.",[]],"jaesche-s02-100":["The philosopher must therefore be able to determine 1) the sources of human knowledge [Wissen], 2) the extent of the possible and useful employment of all knowledge, and finally 3) the limits of reason.",[]],"jaesche-s02-101":["The last is the most necessary, but also the most difficult; the *philodox*, however, does not concern himself with it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-102":["Two things chiefly are required of a philosopher: 1) culture of talent and of skill, in order to use them for all kinds of ends; 2) readiness in the use of all means to any ends whatsoever. The two must be united. For without knowledge [Kenntnisse] one will never become a philosopher; but neither will knowledge alone ever constitute the philosopher, unless there be added a purposive combination of all modes of knowledge and skills into unity, and an insight into their agreement with the highest ends of human reason.",[]],"jaesche-s02-103":["No one can call himself a philosopher who cannot philosophise. But philosophising can be learned only through practice and through one's own use of reason.",[]],"jaesche-s02-104":["How, indeed, should philosophy, properly speaking, be learned? Every philosophical thinker builds his own work, so to speak, upon the ruins of another's; but no work has ever been achieved which has been stable in all its parts. We cannot, therefore, learn philosophy, for the very reason that it is not yet given. But even supposing that a philosophy actually existed, no one who learned it would be able to say of himself that he was a philosopher; for his knowledge of it would still always be only subjectively historical.",[]],"jaesche-s02-105":["In mathematics the case is otherwise. This science can in a certain measure be learned; for the proofs are here so evident that everyone can be convinced by them. Owing to its evidence it can also be, as it were, preserved as a certain and stable doctrine.",[]],"jaesche-s02-106":["He, on the other hand, who would learn to philosophise may regard all systems of philosophy only as a history of the employment of reason, and as objects for the exercise of his philosophical talent.",[]],"jaesche-s02-107":["The true philosopher must therefore, as a self-thinker, make a free and independent use of his reason, not a slavishly imitative one. Nor, again, must he make a dialectical use of it, that is, a use which aims only at giving modes of knowledge a semblance of truth and wisdom. That is the business of the mere sophist, and is altogether incompatible with the dignity of the philosopher, as one who knows and teaches wisdom.",[]],"jaesche-s02-108":["For science has an inner, true worth only as an organ of wisdom. As such, however, it is also indispensable to wisdom; so that we may well maintain that wisdom without science is but the silhouette of a perfection to which we shall never attain.",[]],"jaesche-s02-109":["He who hates science, but loves wisdom all the more, is entitled a misologist. Misology commonly springs from an emptiness of scientific knowledge and from a certain kind of vanity bound up with it. Sometimes, however, those also fall into the fault of misology who at first pursued the sciences with great diligence and success, but in the end found no satisfaction in all their knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s02-110":["Philosophy is the only science which knows how to procure for us this inner satisfaction; for it closes, as it were, the scientific circle, and only through it do the sciences then acquire order and connection.",[]],"jaesche-s02-111":["For the purpose of practice in thinking for oneself, or philosophising, we shall therefore have to look more to the method of our employment of reason than to the propositions themselves at which we have arrived by means of it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-112":["[Introduction.] IV. Brief Outline of a History of Philosophy.",[]],"jaesche-s02-113":["There is some difficulty in determining the boundary where the common employment of the understanding ends and the speculative begins, or where common knowledge through reason becomes philosophy.",[]],"jaesche-s02-114":["There is, however, a fairly sure mark of distinction here, namely, the following.",[]],"jaesche-s02-115":["Knowledge of the universal *in abstracto* is speculative knowledge; knowledge of the universal *in concreto* is common knowledge. Philosophical knowledge is speculative knowledge of reason; it therefore begins where the common employment of reason starts to make attempts at knowledge of the universal *in abstracto*.",[]],"jaesche-s02-116":["From this determination of the difference between the common and the speculative employment of reason we can now judge from which people the beginning of philosophising must be dated. Among all peoples, then, the Greeks were the first to begin to philosophise. For they first attempted to cultivate modes of knowledge through reason not by the guiding thread of images, but *in abstracto*; whereas other peoples always sought to make concepts intelligible to themselves only through images *in concreto*. Thus there are still today peoples, such as the Chinese and some Indians, who do indeed treat of things derived merely from reason, such as God, the immortality of the soul and the like, but who yet do not seek to investigate the nature of these objects according to concepts and rules *in abstracto*. They make no separation here between the employment of reason *in concreto* and its employment *in abstracto*. Among the Persians and Arabs there is indeed some speculative employment of reason; but the rules for it they borrowed from Aristotle, and so, after all, from the Greeks. In Zoroaster's Zendavesta not the slightest trace of philosophy is to be found. The same holds also of the vaunted Egyptian wisdom, which in comparison with Greek philosophy was mere child's play.",[]],"jaesche-s02-117":["As in philosophy, so also in respect of mathematics the Greeks were the first to cultivate this part of knowledge through reason according to a speculative, scientific method, demonstrating every theorem from elements.",[]],"jaesche-s02-118":["When and where among the Greeks the philosophical spirit first arose cannot, however, properly be determined.",[]],"jaesche-s02-119":["The first who introduced the employment of speculative reason, and from whom the first steps of the human understanding towards scientific culture have also been derived, is Thales, the founder of the Ionian sect. He bore the surname of Physicist, although he was also a mathematician; just as, in general, mathematics has always preceded philosophy.",[]],"jaesche-s02-120":["The first philosophers, moreover, clothed everything in images. For poetry, which is nothing but a clothing of thoughts in images, is older than prose. At first, therefore, men had to make use of figurative language and the poetic style even in dealing with things which are solely objects of pure reason. Pherecydes is said to have been the first writer of prose.",[]],"jaesche-s02-121":["The Ionians were followed by the Eleatics. The principle of the Eleatic philosophy and of its founder Xenophanes was this: in the senses there is deception and illusion; in the understanding alone lies the source of truth.",[]],"jaesche-s02-122":["Among the philosophers of this school Zeno distinguished himself as a man of great understanding and acuteness, and as a subtle dialectician.",[]],"jaesche-s02-123":["Dialectic signified at first the art of the pure employment of the understanding in respect of abstract concepts separated from all sensibility. Hence the many eulogies of this art among the ancients. Later, those philosophers who wholly rejected the testimony of the senses were, in maintaining this position, inevitably led into many subtleties; and dialectic then degenerated into the art of maintaining and contesting any proposition whatsoever. It thus became a mere exercise for the sophists, who wished to reason [raisonniren] about everything, and who devoted themselves to giving illusion the colour of truth and to making black white. For this reason the name sophist, by which had formerly been meant a man able to speak rationally and with insight on all matters, now became so hated and despised, and the name philosopher was introduced in its stead.",[]],"jaesche-s02-124":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-125":["About the time of the Ionian school there arose in Magna Graecia a man of rare genius, who not only likewise founded a school, but at the same time devised and carried out a project the like of which had never before existed. This man was Pythagoras, born at Samos. He founded, namely, a society of philosophers united among themselves in a league by the law of silence. His hearers he divided into two classes: the acusmatics (ἀκουσμαθικοι), who had merely to listen, and the acroamatics (ἀκροαμαθικοι), who were also permitted to ask questions.",[]],"jaesche-s02-126":["Among his doctrines there were some exoteric ones, which he expounded to the whole people. The rest were secret and esoteric, intended only for the members of his league; some of these he received into his most intimate friendship and wholly separated from the others. As the vehicle of his secret doctrines he employed physics and theology, that is, the doctrine of the visible and of the invisible. He had also various symbols, which were presumably nothing but certain signs that served the Pythagoreans for communicating with one another.",[]],"jaesche-s02-127":["The purpose of his league seems to have been none other than to purify religion of the delusions of the people, to moderate tyranny, and to introduce greater lawfulness into states. This league, however, which the tyrants began to fear, was destroyed shortly before the death of Pythagoras; and this philosophical society was dissolved, partly through the execution, partly through the flight and banishment, of the greater part of its members. The few who still remained were novices. And since these knew little of the doctrines peculiar to Pythagoras, nothing certain and determinate can be said about them. Later, many doctrines were ascribed to Pythagoras, who was, moreover, a very mathematical mind; but these are certainly mere fabrications.",[]],"jaesche-s02-128":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-129":["The most important epoch of Greek philosophy begins, finally, with Socrates. For it was he who gave to the philosophical spirit and to all speculative minds an entirely new, practical direction. He is also almost the only one among all men whose conduct comes nearest to the idea of a sage.",[]],"jaesche-s02-130":["Among his pupils the most famous is Plato, who occupied himself more with the practical doctrines of Socrates; and among the pupils of Plato, Aristotle, who raised speculative philosophy once more to a higher level.",[]],"jaesche-s02-131":["Plato and Aristotle were followed by the Epicureans and the Stoics, who were the most avowed enemies of one another. The former placed the highest good in a joyful heart, which they called pleasure [Wollust]; the latter found it solely in loftiness and strength of soul, in which one can dispense with all the amenities of life.",[]],"jaesche-s02-132":["The Stoics, moreover, were dialectical in speculative philosophy and dogmatic in moral philosophy. In their practical principles, through which they sowed the seed of the most sublime dispositions that have ever existed, they showed an extraordinary degree of dignity. The founder of the Stoic school is Zeno of Citium. The most famous men of this school among the Greek philosophers are Cleanthes and Chrysippus.",[]],"jaesche-s02-133":["The Epicurean school was never able to attain the repute which the Stoic enjoyed. But whatever may be said of the Epicureans, this much is certain: they showed the greatest moderation in enjoyment, and were the best natural philosophers among all the thinkers of Greece.",[]],"jaesche-s02-134":["We may further remark here that the principal Greek schools bore special names. Thus the school of Plato was entitled the Academy; that of Aristotle, the Lyceum; the school of the Stoics, the *Porticus* (στοα), a covered walk, from which the name Stoic derives; the school of Epicurus, *horti*, because Epicurus taught in gardens.",[]],"jaesche-s02-135":["Plato's Academy was followed by three further academies, founded by his pupils. The first was founded by Speusippus, the second by Arcesilaus, and the third by Carneades.",[]],"jaesche-s02-136":["These academies inclined towards scepticism. Speusippus and Arcesilaus both attuned their way of thinking to scepticism, and Carneades carried it still further. For this reason the sceptics, those subtle, dialectical philosophers, are also entitled Academics. The Academics thus followed the first great doubter, Pyrrho, and his successors. Their teacher Plato had himself given occasion for this, since he expounded many of his doctrines in dialogue form, so that reasons *pro* and *contra* were adduced without his deciding the matter himself, although he was otherwise very dogmatic.",[]],"jaesche-s02-137":["If we begin the epoch of scepticism with Pyrrho, we obtain a whole school of sceptics who differed essentially from the dogmatists in their way of thinking and method of philosophising. For they made it the first maxim of every philosophising employment of reason to withhold one's judgment even in face of the strongest semblance of truth; and they set up the principle that philosophy consists in the equilibrium of judging, and teaches us to uncover false illusion. Of these sceptics, however, nothing further has come down to us than the two works of Sextus Empiricus, in which he has collected all the doubts.",[]],"jaesche-s02-138":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-139":["When philosophy later passed from the Greeks to the Romans, it was not extended; for the Romans always remained only pupils.",[]],"jaesche-s02-140":["Cicero was in speculative philosophy a pupil of Plato, in morals a Stoic. To the Stoic sect belonged, as the most famous, Epictetus, Antoninus the Philosopher and Seneca. There were no natural philosophers among the Romans, except Pliny the Younger, who has left a description of nature.",[]],"jaesche-s02-141":["At length culture vanished among the Romans also, and barbarism arose, until in the sixth and seventh centuries the Arabs began to apply themselves to the sciences and to bring Aristotle once more into repute. Now, accordingly, the sciences rose again in the West, and in particular the authority of Aristotle, who was, however, followed in a slavish manner. In the eleventh and twelfth centuries the Scholastics appeared. They expounded Aristotle and carried his subtleties to infinity. Men occupied themselves with nothing but sheer abstractions. This scholastic method of pseudo-philosophising [After-Philosophiren] was displaced at the time of the Reformation; and there were now eclectics in philosophy, that is, independent thinkers who professed allegiance to no school, but sought truth and accepted it wherever they found it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-142":["Philosophy owes its improvement in modern times, however, partly to the increased study of nature, partly to the combination of mathematics with natural science. The order which the study of these sciences brought into thinking extended also to the particular branches and parts of philosophy proper. The first and greatest investigator of nature in modern times was Bacon of Verulam. In his investigations he took the path of experience, and drew attention to the importance and indispensability of observations and experiments for the discovery of truth. It is, however, difficult to say whence the improvement of speculative philosophy properly derives. No small merit in this regard was earned by Descartes, who contributed much to giving distinctness to thinking through the criterion of truth which he set up, and which he placed in the clearness and evidence of knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s02-143":["Among the greatest and most meritorious reformers of philosophy in our times, however, Leibniz and Locke are to be reckoned. The latter sought to analyse the human understanding, and to show which powers of the soul and which of their operations belong to this or that mode of knowledge. But he did not complete the work of his investigation; his procedure, too, is dogmatic, although he did this service, that men began to study the nature of the soul better and more thoroughly.",[]],"jaesche-s02-144":["As regards the special dogmatic method of philosophising peculiar to Leibniz and Wolff, it was very faulty. There is also so much that is deceptive in it that it is indeed necessary to suspend the whole procedure, and to set in motion in its stead another, the method of critical philosophising. This consists in investigating the procedure of reason itself, in analysing the entire human faculty of knowledge, and in examining how far its limits may extend.",[]],"jaesche-s02-145":["In our age natural philosophy is in a most flourishing state, and among the investigators of nature there are great names, Newton, for instance. Of recent philosophers, on the other hand, no distinguished and abiding names can properly be cited at present, since here everything is, as it were, in flux. What one builds, another pulls down.",[]],"jaesche-s02-146":["In moral philosophy we have not advanced beyond the ancients. As regards metaphysics, it seems as though we had been brought to a standstill in the investigation of metaphysical truths. There is now evident a kind of indifferentism towards this science, since men seem to take pride in speaking contemptuously of metaphysical inquiries as mere idle subtleties. And yet metaphysics is philosophy proper, true philosophy!",[]],"jaesche-s02-147":["Our age is the age of criticism, and we must wait and see what will come of the critical endeavours of our time in respect of philosophy, and of metaphysics in particular.",[]],"jaesche-s02-148":["[Introduction.] V. Knowledge in General.",[]],"jaesche-s02-149":["— Intuitive and discursive knowledge; intuition and concept, and in particular their difference. — Logical and aesthetic perfection of knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s02-150":["All our knowledge has a twofold relation: first, a relation to the object; secondly, a relation to the subject. In the former respect it is related to representation; in the latter, to consciousness, the universal condition of all knowledge in general. (Consciousness is, properly speaking, a representation that another representation is in me.)",[]],"jaesche-s02-151":["In every mode of knowledge we must distinguish matter, that is, the object, and form, that is, the manner in which we know the object. If, for instance, a savage sees from a distance a house whose use he does not know, he has indeed before him in representation the very same object as another who knows it determinately as a dwelling designed for human beings. But in respect of form this knowledge of one and the same object differs in the two. In the one it is mere intuition; in the other it is intuition and concept together.",[]],"jaesche-s02-152":["The difference in the form of knowledge rests on a condition which accompanies all knowing, namely, on consciousness. If I am conscious of the representation, it is clear; if I am not conscious of it, it is obscure.",[]],"jaesche-s02-153":["Since consciousness is the essential condition of all logical form in our modes of knowledge, logic can and may concern itself only with clear representations, and not with obscure ones. In logic we do not consider how representations arise, but solely how they agree with logical form. Logic, indeed, cannot deal at all with mere representations and their possibility; that it leaves to metaphysics. It is itself concerned only with the rules of thinking in concepts, judgments and inferences, since it is through these that all thinking takes place. Something, admittedly, precedes the stage at which a representation becomes a concept, and this too we shall indicate in its proper place. But we shall not inquire how representations arise. Logic does, it is true, deal also with knowing [*Erkennen*], since thinking already takes place in knowing. But representation is not yet knowledge; knowledge always presupposes representation. And representation cannot be explained at all; for we should always have to explain what representation is by means of yet another representation.",[]],"jaesche-s02-154":["All clear representations, to which alone the logical rules can be applied, may now be distinguished in respect of distinctness and indistinctness. If we are conscious of the representation as a whole, but not of the manifold contained in it, the representation is indistinct. To illustrate the matter, let us first take an example in intuition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-155":["We see a country house in the distance. If we are conscious that the object intuited is a house, we must necessarily also have a representation of the various parts of this house, the windows, doors, etc. For if we did not see the parts, we should not see the house itself. But we are not conscious of this representation of the manifold of its parts, and our representation of the object in question is therefore an indistinct representation.",[]],"jaesche-s02-156":["If, further, we want an example of indistinctness in concepts, the concept of beauty may serve. Everyone has a clear concept of beauty. But various marks [*Merkmale*] occur in this concept, among others that the beautiful must be something which (1) falls within the senses and (2) pleases universally. If we are unable to set out separately the manifold of these and other marks of the beautiful, our concept of it is still indistinct.",[]],"jaesche-s02-157":["The followers of Wolff entitle an indistinct representation a confused one. This expression is not appropriate, since the opposite of confusion is not distinctness but order. Distinctness is indeed an effect of order, and indistinctness an effect of confusion; every confused mode of knowledge is therefore also indistinct. But the proposition does not hold conversely: not all indistinct knowledge is confused. For in modes of knowledge in which there is no manifold there is no order, but neither is there any confusion.",[]],"jaesche-s02-158":["This is the case with all simple representations. They never become distinct, not because there is confusion in them, but because no manifold is to be found in them. They must therefore be entitled indistinct, but not confused.",[]],"jaesche-s02-159":["Even in composite representations, in which a manifold of marks can be distinguished, the indistinctness often arises not from confusion but from weakness of consciousness. Something may be distinct in respect of form, that is, I may be conscious of the manifold in the representation; and yet in respect of matter the distinctness may diminish as the degree of consciousness becomes less, although all the order is present. This is the case with abstract representations.",[]],"jaesche-s02-160":["Distinctness itself may be of two kinds:",[]],"jaesche-s02-161":["First, sensible. This consists in consciousness of the manifold in intuition. I see, for instance, the Milky Way as a whitish band; the rays of light from the individual stars in it must necessarily have entered my eye. But the representation of it was merely clear, and becomes distinct only through the telescope, since I now see the individual stars contained in that milky band.",[]],"jaesche-s02-162":["Secondly, intellectual: distinctness in concepts, or distinctness of the understanding. This rests on the analysis [*Zergliederung*] of the concept in respect of the manifold contained in it. Thus, for example, the concept of virtue contains as marks (1) the concept of freedom, (2) the concept of adherence to rules (of duty), (3) the concept of overcoming the power of the inclinations, in so far as they conflict with those rules. When we thus resolve the concept of virtue into its several constituents, we make it distinct to ourselves through this very analysis. But in making it distinct we add nothing to a concept; we only explain it. In being made distinct, therefore, concepts are improved not in respect of their matter, but only in respect of their form.",[]],"jaesche-s02-163":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-164":["If we reflect upon our modes of knowledge in respect of the two fundamentally different faculties of sensibility and under|standing,",[]],"jaesche-s02-165":["from which they arise, we here come upon the distinction between intuitions and concepts. Considered in this respect, all our modes of knowledge are either intuitions or concepts. The former have their source in sensibility, the faculty of intuitions; the latter in the understanding, the faculty of concepts. This is the logical distinction between understanding and sensibility, according to which sensibility yields nothing but intuitions, the understanding nothing but concepts. The two fundamental faculties can indeed be considered from another side also, and defined in another manner, namely, sensibility as a faculty of receptivity, the understanding as a faculty of spontaneity. But this mode of definition is not logical but metaphysical. Sensibility is also usually entitled the lower faculty, and the understanding the higher, on the ground that sensibility supplies the mere material for thought, while the understanding disposes of this material and brings it under rules or concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s02-166":["On this distinction between intuitive and discursive modes of knowledge, or between intuitions and concepts, rests the difference between the aesthetic and the logical perfection of knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s02-167":["A mode of knowledge may be perfect either according to laws of sensibility or according to laws of the understanding; in the former case it is aesthetically perfect, in the latter logically perfect. The two, aesthetic and logical perfection, are thus of different kinds: the former relates to sensibility, the latter to the understanding. The logical perfection of knowledge rests on its agreement with the object, and so on universally valid laws; it can therefore be judged according to norms *a priori*. Aesthetic perfection consists in the agreement of knowledge with the subject, and is grounded in the special sensibility of man. In aesthetic perfection, therefore, there are no objectively and universally valid laws in relation to which it could be judged *a priori*, in a manner universally valid for all thinking beings whatsoever. But in so far as there are also universal laws of sensibility which, though not objectively valid for all thinking beings, are yet subjectively valid for the whole of mankind, an aesthetic perfection can also be thought that contains the ground of a subjectively universal satisfaction [*Wohlgefallen*]. This is beauty: that which pleases the senses in intuition, and which can be the object of a universal satisfaction precisely because the laws of intuition are universal laws of sensibility.",[]],"jaesche-s02-168":["It is through this agreement with the universal laws of sensibility that the beautiful proper, the self-subsistent beautiful, whose essence consists in mere form, is distinguished in kind from the agreeable. The agreeable pleases solely in sensation, through charm [*Reiz*] or emotion [*Rührung*], and can therefore be the ground only of a merely private satisfaction.",[]],"jaesche-s02-169":["It is this essential aesthetic perfection, too, which of all [aesthetic perfections] is compatible with logical perfection and can best be combined with it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-170":["Considered from this side, aesthetic perfection, in respect of the essentially beautiful, can be advantageous to logical perfection. In another respect, however, it is also detrimental to it, namely, in so far as in aesthetic perfection we look only to the non-essentially beautiful, the charming or moving, which pleases the senses in mere sensation and relates not to the mere form but to the matter of sensibility. For charm and emotion are what can most corrupt the logical perfection of our modes of knowledge and of our judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s02-171":["In general there always remains, no doubt, a kind of conflict between the aesthetic and the logical perfection of our knowledge, which cannot be entirely removed. The understanding wishes to be instructed, sensibility to be enlivened; the former desires insight, the latter comprehensibility [*Faßlichkeit*]. If modes of knowledge are to instruct, they must to that extent be thorough; if they are at the same time to entertain, they must also be beautiful. A discourse that is beautiful but shallow can please only sensibility, not the understanding; one that is thorough but dry can please only the understanding, not sensibility as well.",[]],"jaesche-s02-172":["Since, however, the needs of human nature and the end of popularity in knowledge require that we seek to unite the two perfections, we must take care to supply aesthetic perfection to those modes of knowledge which are at all capable of it, and to make a scholastically correct, logically perfect knowledge popu|lar through aesthetic form",[]],"jaesche-s02-173":["In this endeavour to combine aesthetic with logical perfection in our modes of knowledge, we must not lose sight of the following rules: (1) that logical perfection is the basis of all other perfections, and may therefore not be wholly subordinated or sacrificed to any other; (2) that we should look chiefly to formal aesthetic perfection, the agreement of a mode of knowledge with the laws of intuition, since it is precisely in this that the essentially beautiful consists, which can best be united with logical perfection; (3) that we must be very cautious with charm and emotion, through which a mode of knowledge acts upon sensation and acquires an interest for it. For in this way attention can so easily be drawn away from the object to the subject, and from this a very detrimental influence upon the logical perfection of knowledge must evidently result.",[]],"jaesche-s02-174":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-175":["In order to make the essential differences between the logical and the aesthetic perfection of knowledge more recognisable, not merely in general but from several particular sides, we shall compare the two with one another in respect of the four chief moments of quantity, quality, relation and modality, upon which the estimation of the perfection of knowledge depends.",[]],"jaesche-s02-176":["A mode of knowledge is perfect (1) in respect of quantity, if it is universal; (2) in respect of quality, if it is distinct; (3) in respect of relation, if it is true; and finally (4) in respect of modality, if it is certain.",[]],"jaesche-s02-177":["Considered from these points of view, a mode of knowledge will thus be logically perfect: in respect of quantity, if it has objective universality (universality of the concept or of the rule); in respect of quality, if it has objective distinctness (distinctness in the concept); in respect of relation, if it has objective truth; and finally in respect of modality, if it has objective certainty.",[]],"jaesche-s02-178":["To these logical perfections there now correspond, in relation to those four chief moments, the following aesthetic perfections, namely:",[]],"jaesche-s02-179":["(1) aesthetic universality. This consists in the applicability of a mode of knowledge to a multitude of objects which serve as examples to which it can be applied, and through which it becomes at the same time serviceable for the end of popularity;",[]],"jaesche-s02-180":["(2) aesthetic distinctness. This is distinctness in intuition, in which a concept thought in abstraction is exhibited or illustrated *in concreto* by means of examples;",[]],"jaesche-s02-181":["(3) aesthetic truth. [This is] a merely subjective truth, which consists only in the agreement of knowledge with the subject and with the laws of sensible illusion [*Sinnen-Schein*], and is consequently nothing more than a universal illusion;",[]],"jaesche-s02-182":["(4) aesthetic certainty. This rests on what is necessary according to the testimony of the senses, that is, on what is confirmed by sensation and experience.",[]],"jaesche-s02-183":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-184":["In the perfections just named there are always two elements which in their harmonious union constitute perfection in general, namely, manifoldness and unity. For the understanding the unity lies in the concept; for the senses, in intuition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-185":["Mere manifoldness without unity cannot satisfy us. Truth is therefore the chief of all perfections, since it is the ground of unity through the relation of our knowledge to the object. Even in aesthetic perfection truth always remains the *conditio sine qua non*, the foremost negative condition, without which nothing can please taste universally. No one, therefore, may hope to make progress in the fine arts and letters [*schöne Wissenschaften*] unless he has laid logical perfection at the foundation of his knowledge. And it is indeed in the greatest possible union of logical with aesthetic perfection in general, in respect of such knowledge as is meant at once to instruct and to entertain, that the character and the art of genius display themselves.",[]],"jaesche-s02-186":["[Introduction.] VI. Particular Logical Perfections of Knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s02-187":["A) Logical perfection of knowledge in respect of quantity. — Magnitude. — Extensive and intensive magnitude. — Extent and thoroughness, or importance and fruitfulness, of knowledge. — Determination of the horizon of our modes of knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s02-188":["The magnitude of knowledge may be taken in a twofold sense, either as extensive or as intensive magnitude. The former relates to the extent [*Umfang*] of knowledge, and so consists in its multitude and manifoldness; the latter relates to its content [*Gehalt*], which concerns the manifold validity [*Vielgültigkeit*], or the logical importance and fruitfulness, of a mode of knowledge, in so far as it is regarded as the ground of many and great consequences (*non multa sed multum*).",[]],"jaesche-s02-189":["In extending our modes of knowledge, or in perfecting them in respect of their extensive magnitude, it is well to make an estimate of how far a mode of knowledge accords with our ends and capacities. This consideration concerns the determination of the horizon of our modes of knowledge, by which is to be understood the adequacy of the magnitude of our knowledge as a whole to the capacities and ends of the subject.",[]],"jaesche-s02-190":["The horizon may be determined",[]],"jaesche-s02-191":["(1) logically, according to our faculty or cognitive powers, in relation to the interest of the understanding. Here we have to judge how far we can advance in our modes of knowledge, how far we must go in them, and how far certain modes of knowledge serve, from a logical point of view, as means to this or that principal knowledge which is our end;",[]],"jaesche-s02-192":["(2) aesthetically, according to taste, in relation to the interest of feeling. He who determines his horizon aesthetically seeks to adapt science to the taste of the public, that is, to make it popular, or in general to acquire only such modes of knowledge as can be universally communicated and in which the class of the unlearned also finds pleasure and interest;",[]],"jaesche-s02-193":["(3) practically, according to utility, in relation to the interest",[]],"jaesche-s02-194":["of the will. The practical horizon, in so far as it is determined according to the influence which a mode of knowledge has upon our morality, is pragmatic, and of the greatest importance.",[]],"jaesche-s02-195":["The horizon thus concerns the estimation and determination of what man can know, what he may know, and what he ought to know.",[]],"jaesche-s02-196":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-197":["As regards in particular the horizon determined theoretically or logically (and it is of this alone that we can here speak), we can consider it either from the objective or from the subjective point of view.",[]],"jaesche-s02-198":["In respect of objects the horizon is either historical or rational. The former is much wider than the latter; it is indeed immeasurably great, for our historical knowledge has no limits. The rational horizon, on the other hand, can be fixed. It can be determined, for example, to what kind of objects mathematical knowledge cannot be extended; and likewise, with regard to philosophical knowledge of reason, how far reason can here advance *a priori* without any experience.",[]],"jaesche-s02-199":["In relation to the subject the horizon is either the universal and absolute horizon, or a particular and conditioned one (private horizon).",[]],"jaesche-s02-200":["By the absolute and universal horizon is to be understood the congruence of the limits of human knowledge with the limits of the whole of human perfection in general. The question here, then, is: What can man as man know at all?",[]],"jaesche-s02-201":["The determination of the private horizon depends on various empirical and special considerations, for example of age, sex, station, mode of life, and the like. Every particular class of men thus has its particular horizon in relation to its special cognitive powers, ends and standpoints; and every mind [*Kopf*] has its own horizon, according to the individuality of its powers and of its standpoint. Lastly, we can also think a horizon of sound reason and a horizon of science, the latter of which still requires principles in order to determine by them what we can and cannot know.",[]],"jaesche-s02-202":["What we cannot know is above our horizon; what we may not know, or need not know, is outside our horizon. This latter, however, can hold only relatively, in relation to this or that particular private end, to the attainment of which certain modes of knowledge not only contribute nothing but might even be a hindrance. For no knowledge is absolutely and in every respect useless and unserviceable, although we cannot always discern its utility. It is therefore a reproach as unwise as it is unjust that shallow minds direct against great men who labour at the sciences with painstaking industry, when they ask: What is the use of that? This question ought not even to be raised when one proposes to occupy oneself with the sciences. Supposing a science could give information only about some possible object, it would on that account alone be useful enough. Every logically perfect knowledge always has some possible use, which, though as yet unknown to us, may perhaps be discovered by posterity. Had men in cultivating the sciences always looked only to material gain, to their utility, we should have no arithmetic and geometry. Our understanding, moreover, is so constituted that it finds satisfaction in mere insight, and more even than in the utility that arises from it. Plato already remarked this. Man here feels his own excellence; he feels what it means to have understanding. Men who do not feel this must envy the beasts. The inner worth which modes of knowledge possess through logical perfection is not to be compared with their outer worth, their worth in application.",[]],"jaesche-s02-203":["What lies outside our horizon, in so far as, being dispensable to us, we may in view of our purposes not know it, and likewise what lies below our horizon, in so far as, being harmful to us, we ought not to know it, are both to be understood only in a relative, and by no means in an absolute, sense.",[]],"jaesche-s02-204":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-205":["In regard to the extension and demarcation of our knowledge, the following rules are to be recommended:",[]],"jaesche-s02-206":["One must determine one's horizon",[]],"jaesche-s02-207":["(1) early, certainly, but yet only when one is able to determine it for oneself, which does not usually happen before the twentieth year;",[]],"jaesche-s02-208":["(2) and must not change it lightly and often (not fall from one thing to another);",[]],"jaesche-s02-209":["(3) must not measure the horizon of others by one's own, nor hold that to be useless which is of no use to oneself. It would be presumptuous to try to determine the horizon of others, since one does not know sufficiently either their capacities or their purposes;",[]],"jaesche-s02-210":["(4) must neither extend it too far nor restrict it too much. For he who wishes to know too much ends by knowing nothing; and he who, conversely, believes that certain things do not concern him often deceives himself, as when, for example, the philosopher believes that he can dispense with history.",[]],"jaesche-s02-211":["One should also seek",[]],"jaesche-s02-212":["(5) to determine in advance the absolute horizon of the whole human race (in respect of past and future time), and so also, in particular,",[]],"jaesche-s02-213":["(6) to determine the place which our science occupies in the horizon of knowledge as a whole. For this purpose the universal encyclopaedia serves as a universal map (*mappe-monde*) of the sciences.",[]],"jaesche-s02-214":["(7) In determining one's particular horizon itself, one should examine carefully for what part of knowledge one has the greatest capacity and liking, what is more or less necessary in respect of certain duties, and what cannot coexist with the necessary duties; and finally",[]],"jaesche-s02-215":["(8) one should always seek rather to extend one's horizon than to narrow it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-216":["In general, what d'Alembert fears from the extension of knowledge is not to be feared. For it is not the burden that oppresses us; what constricts us is the volume of space available for our knowledge. Criticism of reason, of history and of historical writings, and a universal spirit directed to human knowledge *en gros* and not merely *en détail*, will always reduce the extent without diminishing anything of the content. Only the dross falls away from the metal, or the baser vehicle, the husk, which was necessary until then. With the extension of natural history, mathematics, etc., new methods will be invented which abridge the old and make the multitude of books dispensable. It will be on the invention of such new methods and principles that our ability will depend to find everything for ourselves at will by their aid, without burdening the memory. He therefore deserves well of history, as a genius, who comprehends it under ideas which can always endure.",[]],"jaesche-s02-217":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-218":["Opposed to the logical perfection of knowledge in respect of its extent is ignorance. [It is] a negative imperfection, or imperfection of lack, which on account of the limits of the understanding remains inseparable from our knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s02-219":["We can consider ignorance from an objective and from a subjective point of view.",[]],"jaesche-s02-220":["(1) Taken objectively, ignorance is either material or formal. The former consists in a lack of historical, the latter in a lack of rational, modes of knowledge. One ought not to be wholly ignorant in any field; but one may well restrict one's historical knowledge in order to devote oneself the more to the rational, or conversely.",[]],"jaesche-s02-221":["(2) In the subjective sense, ignorance is either learned, scientific, or common. He who clearly discerns the limits of knowledge, and so the field of ignorance, from where it begins, is ignorant in a technical [*kunstmäßig*] or learned way: for example, the philosopher who discerns and proves how little, for lack of the requisite *data*, can be known regarding the structure of gold. He, on the other hand, who is ignorant without discerning the grounds of the limits of knowledge, and without concerning himself about them, is ignorant in a common, unscientific way. Such a man does not even know that he knows nothing. For one can never represent one's ignorance otherwise than through science, just as a blind man cannot represent darkness to himself until he has gained his sight.",[]],"jaesche-s02-222":["Knowledge of one's ignorance thus presupposes science, and at the same time makes one modest, whereas imagined knowledge puffs one up. Thus Socrates' not-knowing was a praiseworthy ignorance, properly, by his own confession, a knowing of not-knowing. Those, therefore, who possess a great deal of knowledge and yet for all that are astonished at the amount of what they do not know, cannot fairly be charged with ignorance.",[]],"jaesche-s02-223":["Ignorance is in general blameless (*inculpabilis*) in things the knowledge of which lies above our horizon; and it may be permissible (though again only in a relative sense) in respect of the speculative use of our cognitive faculties, in so far as the objects here lie, if not above, yet outside our horizon. But it is disgraceful in things which it is very necessary, and also easy, for us to know.",[]],"jaesche-s02-224":["There is a difference between not knowing something and ignoring something, that is, taking no notice of it. It is well to ignore much that it is not good for us to know. From both of these, again, abstracting is to be distinguished. One abstracts from a mode of knowledge when one ignores its application, whereby one obtains it *in abstracto* and can then better consider it in general as a principle. Such abstracting from what, in the knowledge of a thing, does not belong to our purpose is useful and praiseworthy.",[]],"jaesche-s02-225":["Teachers of reason [*Vernunftlehrer*] are commonly ignorant historically.",[]],"jaesche-s02-226":["Historical knowledge without determinate limits is polyhistory; this puffs one up. Polymathy is directed to the knowledge of reason. Both together, historical as well as rational knowledge extended without determinate limits, may be entitled pansophy. To historical knowledge belongs the science of the instruments of learning, philology, which comprises a critical knowledge of books and languages (literature and linguistics).",[]],"jaesche-s02-227":["Mere polyhistory is a Cyclopean learning which lacks one eye, the eye of philosophy; and a Cyclops of a mathematician, historian, describer of nature, philologist and linguist is a scholar who is great in all these departments, but holds all philosophy to be dispensable on their account.",[]],"jaesche-s02-228":["A part of philology is constituted by the *humaniora*, by which is understood the knowledge of the ancients, which promotes the union of science with taste, smooths away roughness, and promotes the communicability and urbanity in which humanity consists.",[]],"jaesche-s02-229":["The *humaniora* thus concern instruction in what serves the cultivation of taste, in accordance with the models of the ancients. To this belong, for example, eloquence, poetry, wide reading in the classical authors, and the like. All this humanistic knowledge may be reckoned to the practical part of philology, the part aiming directly at the formation of taste. If, however, we further distinguish the mere philologist from the humanist, they would differ in this, that the former seeks among the ancients the instruments of learning, the latter the instruments of the formation of taste.",[]],"jaesche-s02-230":["The *belletrist* or *bel esprit* is a humanist after contemporary models in living languages. He is therefore no scholar, for only dead languages are now learned languages, but a mere dilettante in the knowledge of taste as fashion dictates, with no need of the ancients. One might call him the ape of the humanist. The polyhistor must, as philologist, be linguist and literary scholar, and, as humanist, be a classicist and an interpreter of the classics. As philologist he is cultivated, as humanist civilised.",[]],"jaesche-s02-231":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-232":["In respect of the sciences there are two degenerations of the prevailing taste: pedantry and *galanterie*. The one pursues the sciences merely for the schools, and thereby restricts them in respect of their use; the other pursues them merely for social intercourse or the world, and thereby limits them in respect of their content.",[]],"jaesche-s02-233":["The pedant is either, as a scholar, opposed to the man of the world, and is to that extent the conceited scholar without knowledge of the world, that is, without knowledge of how to bring his science home to men; or he is indeed to be regarded as a man of skill in general, but only in formalities, not in essence and purpose. In the latter sense he is a stickler for formalities. Restricted in respect of the kernel of things, he looks only to the dress and the shell. He is the unsuccessful imitation or caricature of the methodical mind. Pedantry may therefore also be entitled hair-splitting punctiliousness and useless exactness (micrology) in formalities. And such formality of scholastic method outside the schools is to be met with not only among scholars and in matters of learning, but also among other classes and in other things. Ceremonial at courts and in social intercourse, what is it but a chase after formalities and hair-splitting? In the military it is not altogether so, although it appears to be. But in conversation, in dress, in diet, in religion, much pedantry often prevails.",[]],"jaesche-s02-234":["Exactness in formalities that is suited to its end is thoroughness (scholastically correct, scholastic perfection). Pedantry is thus an affected thoroughness; just as *galanterie*, as a mere courtesan suing for the applause of taste, is nothing but an affected popularity. For *galanterie* is concerned only to win the reader's favour, and therefore not to offend him even by a difficult word.",[]],"jaesche-s02-235":["To avoid pedantry, extensive knowledge is required, not only in the sciences themselves but also in respect of their use. Only the true scholar, therefore, can free himself from pedantry, which is always the property of a limited mind.",[]],"jaesche-s02-236":["In endeavouring to secure for our knowledge the perfection of scholastic thoroughness and at the same time of popularity, without falling into the faults just mentioned of an affected thoroughness or an affected popularity, we must look first of all to the scholastic perfection of our knowledge, the scholastically correct form of thoroughness. Only then should we consider how to make the knowledge methodically learned in the schools truly popular, that is, so easily and universally communicable to others that thoroughness is not displaced by popularity. For scholastic perfection must not be sacrificed for the sake of popular perfection, to please the people; without it all science would be nothing but a toy and trifling.",[]],"jaesche-s02-237":["In order to learn true popularity, however, one must read the ancients, for example Cicero's philosophical writings, the poets Horace, Virgil, etc., and among the moderns Hume, Shaftesbury and others: men who all had much intercourse with the refined world, without which one cannot be popular. For true popularity requires much practical knowledge of the world and of men, knowledge of the concepts, the taste and the inclinations of men, to which constant regard must be had in the exposition and even in the choice of fitting expressions suited to popularity. Such a descent (condescen|sion)",[]],"jaesche-s02-238":["to the capacity of comprehension of the public and to customary expressions, in which scholastic perfection is not set aside, but only the clothing of the thoughts is so arranged that the scaffolding, the scholastically correct and technical part of that perfection, is not allowed to show (just as one draws lines in pencil, writes upon them, and afterwards rubs them out): this truly popular perfection of knowledge is in fact a great and rare perfection, which shows much insight into the science. Besides many other merits it has this further one, that it can furnish a proof of complete insight into a matter. For the merely scholastic testing of a mode of knowledge still leaves the doubt whether the testing may not be one-sided, and whether the knowledge itself really has a worth granted it by all men. The school has its prejudices, just as common understanding has; each here corrects the other. It is therefore important to test a mode of knowledge upon men whose understanding is attached to no school.",[]],"jaesche-s02-239":["This perfection of knowledge, whereby it qualifies itself for easy and universal communication, might also be entitled the external extension, or extensive magnitude, of a mode of knowledge, in so far as it is outwardly spread among many men.",[]],"jaesche-s02-240":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-241":["Since there are so many and so manifold modes of knowledge, one will do well to make oneself a plan according to which one so orders the sciences that they best accord with one's ends and contribute to their furtherance. All modes of knowledge stand to one another in a certain natural connection. If, in striving to extend our knowledge, we pay no regard to this connection, all our much knowing will result in nothing but mere rhapsody. But if we make one principal science our end, and regard all other modes of knowledge only as means of attaining it, we bring into our knowledge a certain systematic character. And in order to proceed according to such a well-ordered and purposive plan in extending our knowledge, we must therefore seek to become acquainted with that connection of the modes of knowledge with one another. Guidance for this is given by the architectonic of the sciences, which is a system according to ideas, in which the sciences are considered in respect of their kinship and systematic connection in a whole of the knowledge that is of interest to mankind.",[]],"jaesche-s02-242":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-243":["As regards, in particular, the intensive magnitude of knowledge, that is, its content [Gehalt], or its manifold validity and importance, which, as we have remarked above, is essentially different from its extensive magnitude, the mere range of it, we shall make only the following few observations upon this point:",[]],"jaesche-s02-244":["1) A mode of knowledge which is directed to what is great, that is, to the whole in the employment of the understanding, is to be distinguished from subtlety in small matters (micrology).",[]],"jaesche-s02-245":["2) Every mode of knowledge which furthers logical perfection in respect of form is to be entitled logically important: for instance, every mathematical proposition, every clearly understood law of nature, every correct philosophical explanation [Erklärung]. Practical importance cannot be foreseen; we must wait for it to appear.",[]],"jaesche-s02-246":["3) Importance must not be confused with difficulty [Schwere]. A mode of knowledge may be difficult without being important, and conversely. Difficulty, therefore, decides neither for nor against the worth and importance of a mode of knowledge. Importance rests upon the magnitude or multiplicity of its consequences. The more consequences, or the greater the consequences, that a mode of knowledge has, and the more use can be made of it, the more important it is. A mode of knowledge without important consequences is entitled a mere subtle brooding [Grübelei]; such, for instance, was the scholastic philosophy.",[]],"jaesche-s02-247":["[Introduction.] VII. B) Logical Perfection of Knowledge in respect of Relation.",[]],"jaesche-s02-248":["— Truth. — Material and formal or logical truth. — Criteria of logical truth. — Falsity and error. — Illusion [Schein] as the source of error. — Means of avoiding errors.",[]],"jaesche-s02-249":["A chief perfection of knowledge, indeed the essential and inseparable condition of all its perfection, is truth. Truth, it is said, consists in the agreement of knowledge with its object. According to this merely nominal definition [Worterklärung], my knowledge, in order to count as true, must agree with the object. Now I can compare the object with my knowledge only by knowing it. My knowledge, therefore, is required to confirm itself; and this is far from sufficient for truth. For since the object is outside me and the knowledge is in me, all that I can ever judge is whether my knowledge of the object agrees with my knowledge of the object. Such a circle in explanation was entitled by the ancients *diallelus*. And in fact this fault was always laid to the charge of the logicians by the sceptics. They observed that this definition of truth is like the case of a man who makes a statement in court and appeals to a witness whom no one knows, but who seeks to establish his own credibility by asserting that the man who called him as witness is an honest man. The accusation was certainly well founded. But the solution of the problem in question is absolutely impossible, and impossible for every man.",[]],"jaesche-s02-250":["For the question here is whether, and how far, there is a criterion of truth that is certain, universal, and serviceable in application. This is what the question, What is truth?, must be taken to mean.",[]],"jaesche-s02-251":["In order to decide this important question, we must carefully distinguish that in our knowledge which belongs to its matter and relates to the object from that which concerns the mere form, as the condition without which a mode of knowledge would not be knowledge at all. With regard to this distinction between the objective, material relation and the subjective, formal relation in our knowledge, the above question therefore divides into these two particular questions:",[]],"jaesche-s02-252":["1) Is there a universal material criterion of truth? and",[]],"jaesche-s02-253":["2) Is there a universal formal criterion of truth?",[]],"jaesche-s02-254":["A universal material criterion of truth is not possible; it is indeed self-contradictory. For as universal, that is, as valid for all objects whatsoever, it would have to abstract completely from all distinction between them. Yet at the same time, as a material criterion, it would have to be directed precisely to this distinction, in order to be able to determine whether a mode of knowledge agrees with the very object to which it is related, and not merely with some object in general, which would be to say nothing at all. But it is in this agreement of a mode of knowledge with the determinate object to which it is related that material truth must consist. For a mode of knowledge which is true in respect of one object may be false in relation to other objects. It is therefore absurd to demand a universal material criterion of truth which is required at once to abstract and not to abstract from all distinction between objects.",[]],"jaesche-s02-255":["If, however, the question concerns universal formal criteria of truth, the decision is easy: there can certainly be such criteria. For formal truth consists solely in the agreement of knowledge with itself, in complete abstraction from all objects whatsoever and from every distinction between them. The universal formal criteria of truth are accordingly nothing but universal logical marks of the agreement of knowledge with itself, or, what is the same thing, with the universal laws of understanding and reason.",[]],"jaesche-s02-256":["These formal, universal criteria are not, indeed, sufficient for objective truth; but they are nevertheless to be regarded as its *conditio sine qua non*.",[]],"jaesche-s02-257":["For the question whether knowledge agrees with the object must be preceded by the question whether it agrees with itself (in respect of form). And this is the business of logic.",[]],"jaesche-s02-258":["The formal criteria of truth in logic are",[]],"jaesche-s02-259":["1) the principle of contradiction,",[]],"jaesche-s02-260":["2) the principle of sufficient reason [Grund].",[]],"jaesche-s02-261":["By the former the logical possibility, by the latter the logical actuality, of a mode of knowledge is determined.",[]],"jaesche-s02-262":["For to the logical truth of a mode of knowledge there belongs",[]],"jaesche-s02-263":["First: that it be logically possible, that is, that it do not contradict itself. This mark of internal logical truth is, however, only negative. For a mode of knowledge which contradicts itself is indeed false; but if it does not contradict itself, it is not always true.",[]],"jaesche-s02-264":["Secondly: that it be logically grounded, that is, that it (a) have grounds and (b) have no false consequences.",[]],"jaesche-s02-265":["This second criterion, which concerns the logical connection of a mode of knowledge with grounds and consequences, is the criterion of external logical truth, or of the rationality [Rationabilität] of knowledge, and it is positive. Here the following rules hold:",[]],"jaesche-s02-266":["1) From the truth of the consequence we may infer the truth of the mode of knowledge as its ground, but only negatively: if a single false consequence follows from a mode of knowledge, the knowledge itself is false. For if the ground were true, the consequence would also have to be true, since the consequence is determined by the ground. —",[]],"jaesche-s02-267":["But we cannot infer conversely that if no false consequence follows from a mode of knowledge, it is true. For true consequences may be drawn from a false ground.",[]],"jaesche-s02-268":["2) If all the consequences of a mode of knowledge are true, the knowledge is also true. For were there anything false in the knowledge, there would also have to be a false consequence.",[]],"jaesche-s02-269":["From the consequence, therefore, we can indeed infer a ground, but without being able to determine that ground. Only from the sum [Inbegriff] of all the consequences can we infer, of a determinate ground, that it is the true ground.",[]],"jaesche-s02-270":["The former mode of inference, according to which the consequence can be only a negatively and indirectly sufficient criterion of the truth of a mode of knowledge, is entitled in logic the apagogic mode (*modus tollens*).",[]],"jaesche-s02-271":["This procedure, of which frequent use is made in geometry, has the advantage that I need derive only a single false consequence from a mode of knowledge in order to prove its falsity. For example, to show that the earth is not flat, I need not bring forward positive and direct grounds; I need only reason apagogically and indirectly, thus: If the earth were flat, the pole star would always have to be at the same height; but this is not the case; therefore the earth is not flat.",[]],"jaesche-s02-272":["In the other, the positive and direct mode of inference (*modus ponens*), the difficulty arises that the totality [Allheit] of the consequences cannot be known apodeictically. By this mode of inference, therefore, we are led only to probable and hypothetically true knowledge (hypotheses), on the presupposition that where many consequences are true, all the rest may also be true.",[]],"jaesche-s02-273":["We may, then, here set up three principles [Grundsätze] as universal, merely formal or logical, criteria of truth. They are",[]],"jaesche-s02-274":["1) the principle of contradiction and of identity (*principium contradictionis* and *identitatis*), by which the inner pos|sibility",[]],"jaesche-s02-275":["of a mode of knowledge is determined for problematic judgments;",[]],"jaesche-s02-276":["2) the principle of sufficient reason (*principium rationis sufficientis*), on which the (logical) actuality of a mode of knowledge, namely, that it is grounded, rests, as material for assertoric judgments;",[]],"jaesche-s02-277":["3) the principle of excluded middle (*principium exclusi medii inter duo contradictoria*), on which the (logical) necessity of a mode of knowledge is based, namely, that we must necessarily judge thus and not otherwise, that is, that the opposite is false; [this holds] for apodeictic judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s02-278":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-279":["The opposite of truth is falsity, which, in so far as it is taken for truth, is entitled error. An erroneous judgment (for error, like truth, is to be found only in judgment) is therefore one which confuses the illusion [Schein] of truth with truth itself.",[]],"jaesche-s02-280":["How truth is possible is easily understood, since in it the understanding acts according to its essential laws.",[]],"jaesche-s02-281":["But how error, in the formal meaning of the word, is possible, that is, how a form of thought contrary to the understanding is possible, is hard to comprehend; just as it is in general incomprehensible how any power [Kraft] should deviate from its own essential laws. We cannot, therefore, seek the ground of errors in the understanding itself and its essential laws, any more than in the limits of the understanding. In these limits lies indeed the cause of ignorance, but by no means the cause of error. If, then, we had no other cognitive power than the understanding, we should never err. But besides the understanding there lies in us another indispensable source of knowledge. This is sensibility, which supplies us with the material for thought, and which in so doing acts according to other laws than does the understanding. Yet error cannot arise from sensibility considered in and by itself either, since the senses do not judge at all.",[]],"jaesche-s02-282":["The originating ground of all error must therefore be sought solely in the unnoticed influence of sensibility upon the understanding, or, to speak more precisely, upon judgment. For this influence causes us, in judging, to take merely subjective grounds for objective ones, and consequently to confuse the mere illusion of truth with truth itself. In this, indeed, consists the essence of illusion, which is for that reason to be regarded as a ground for holding a false mode of knowledge to be true.",[]],"jaesche-s02-283":["What makes error possible is therefore illusion, through which, in judgment, the merely subjective is confused with the objective.",[]],"jaesche-s02-284":["In a certain sense we may indeed make the understanding also the author of errors, namely, in so far as, through want of the requisite attention to that influence of sensibility, it allows itself to be misled by the illusion arising from it into taking merely subjective grounds of determination of the judgment for objective ones, or into accepting as true according to its own laws what is true only according to the laws of sensibility.",[]],"jaesche-s02-285":["Only the blame for ignorance, therefore, lies in the limits of the understanding; the blame for error we must ascribe to ourselves. Nature has indeed denied us many kinds of knowledge [Kenntnisse], and leaves us in unavoidable ignorance about a great many things; but she does not cause error. We are misled into it by our own propensity to judge and to decide, even where, because of our limitations, we are not capable of judging and deciding.",[]],"jaesche-s02-286":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-287":["All error into which the human understanding can fall is, however, only partial; and in every erroneous judgment there must always be something true. For a total error would be a complete conflict with the laws of understanding and reason. How could it, as such, in any way proceed from the understanding, and, in so far as it is nevertheless a judgment, be held to be a product of the understanding?",[]],"jaesche-s02-288":["With regard to what is true and what is erroneous in our knowledge, we distinguish exact [genau] knowledge from rough [roh] knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s02-289":["Knowledge is exact when it is adequate to its object, that is, when in respect of its object there is not the least error in it. It is rough when there may be errors in it without their hindering the purpose in view.",[]],"jaesche-s02-290":["This distinction concerns the wider or narrower determinateness of our knowledge (*cognitio late vel stricte determinata*). At the outset it is sometimes necessary to determine a mode of knowledge in a wider range (*late determinare*), especially in historical matters. In knowledge of reason [Vernunfterkenntnissen], however, everything must be determined exactly (*stricte*). In the case of wide [late] determination, a mode of knowledge is said to be determined *praeter propter* [roughly]. Whether a mode of knowledge is to be roughly or exactly determined always depends upon its purpose. Wide determination always leaves some room for error, though this room may still have its determinate limits. Error occurs especially where a wide determination is taken for a strict one, as for instance in matters of morality, where everything must be strictly determined. Those who do not so determine them are entitled by the English latitudinarians.",[]],"jaesche-s02-291":["From exactness, as an objective perfection of knowledge (since here the knowledge is completely congruent with the object), we may further distinguish subtlety, as a subjective perfection of it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-292":["Knowledge of a thing is subtle when we discover in it what usually escapes the attention of others. It therefore requires a higher degree of attention and a greater expenditure of the power of the understanding.",[]],"jaesche-s02-293":["Many censure all subtlety because they cannot attain to it. But in itself it always does honour to the understanding, and is even meritorious and necessary, in so far as it is applied to an object worthy of observation. If, however, the same end could have been attained with less attention and exertion of the understanding, and we nevertheless expend more upon it, we incur a useless expenditure and fall into subtleties which are indeed difficult but serve no purpose (*nugae difficiles*).",[]],"jaesche-s02-294":["Just as the rough is opposed to the exact, so the coarse [das Grobe] is opposed to the subtle.",[]],"jaesche-s02-295":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-296":["From the nature of error, in the concept of which, as we have remarked, there is contained, besides falsity, the illusion of truth as an essential mark, there follows this important rule for the truth of our knowledge:",[]],"jaesche-s02-297":["In order to avoid errors, we must seek to discover and explain their source, namely, illusion. (No error is unavoidable, at least not absolutely or without qualification, though an error may be relatively unavoidable in those cases where it is unavoidable for us to judge, even at the risk of erring.) Very few philosophers, however, have done this. They have sought only to refute the errors themselves, without indicating the illusion from which they arise. Yet this uncovering and dissolving of illusion is a far greater service to truth than the direct refutation of the errors themselves. For by direct refutation we cannot stop up their source, nor prevent the same illusion, since it remains unknown, from leading again to errors in other cases. Even when we have been convinced that we have erred, there still remain with us, if the illusion which underlies our error has not itself been removed, certain scruples, however little we may be able to bring forward in justification of them.",[]],"jaesche-s02-298":["By explaining the illusion we also do a kind of justice [Billigkeit] to the person who errs. For no one will admit that he has erred without some illusion of truth, which might perhaps have deceived a more acute mind as well, since what matters here are subjective grounds.",[]],"jaesche-s02-299":["An error in which the illusion is evident even to common understanding (*sensus communis*) is entitled an absurdity [Abgeschmacktheit oder Ungereimtheit]. The reproach of absurdity is always a personal censure, which must be avoided, especially in the refutation of errors.",[]],"jaesche-s02-300":["For to him who maintains an absurdity the illusion which underlies this evident falsity is not itself evident. This illusion must first be made evident to him. If even then he still persists in it, he is indeed absurd; but then nothing more can be done with him. He has thereby made himself as incapable as he is unworthy of all further correction and refutation. For strictly we cannot prove to anyone that he is absurd; all reasoning would here be in vain. When we prove the absurdity, we are no longer speaking with the one who errs, but with the man of reason. And for him the exposure of the absurdity (*deductio ad absurdum*) is not necessary.",[]],"jaesche-s02-301":["An absurd error may also be entitled one",[]],"jaesche-s02-302":["for which nothing, not even illusion, serves as excuse; just as a gross error is one which shows ignorance in common knowledge or an offence against ordinary attention.",[]],"jaesche-s02-303":["Error in principles is greater than error in their application.",[]],"jaesche-s02-304":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-305":["An external mark or external touchstone of truth is the comparison of our own judgments with those of others. For what is subjective will not be present in the same way in all others, and the illusion can thereby be explained. The incompatibility of others' judgments with our own is therefore to be regarded as an external mark of error, and as a hint to examine our procedure in judging, but not on that account to reject it forthwith. For we may perhaps be right in substance [in der Sache] and wrong only in manner, that is, in the presentation [Vortrag].",[]],"jaesche-s02-306":["The common understanding of mankind (*sensus communis*) is also in itself a touchstone for discovering the faults of the artificial employment of the understanding. To use common understanding as a test for judging the correctness of the speculative employment is what is meant by orientating oneself in thought, or in the speculative employment of reason, by means of common understanding.",[]],"jaesche-s02-307":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-308":["The universal rules and conditions for avoiding error in general are: (1) to think for oneself; (2) to think oneself into the place of another; and (3) always to think consistently with oneself. The maxim of thinking for oneself may be entitled the enlightened mode of thought; the maxim of putting oneself in thought at the standpoint of others, the enlarged mode of thought; and the maxim of always thinking consistently with oneself, the consistent or coherent [bündige] mode of thought.",[]],"jaesche-s02-309":["[Introduction.] VIII. C) Logical Perfection of Knowledge in respect of Quality.",[]],"jaesche-s02-310":["— Clearness. — Concept of a mark in general. — Different kinds of marks. — Determination of the logical essence of a thing. — Its distinction from the real essence. — Distinctness, a higher degree of clearness. — Aesthetic and logical distinctness. — Distinction between analytic and synthetic distinctness.",[]],"jaesche-s02-311":["Human knowing [Erkennen] is, on the side of the understanding, discursive. That is to say, it takes place through representations which make what is common to several things the ground of knowledge, and therefore through marks as such. We know things, then, only through marks; and this is just what is meant by knowing [Erkennen], a word derived from being acquainted [Kennen].",[]],"jaesche-s02-312":["A mark is that in a thing which constitutes part of the knowledge of it; or, what is the same, it is a partial representation in so far as it is regarded as ground of knowledge of the whole representation. — All our concepts, accordingly, are marks, and all thought is nothing but representing through marks.",[]],"jaesche-s02-313":["Every mark may be considered from two sides:",[]],"jaesche-s02-314":["First, as a representation in itself;",[]],"jaesche-s02-315":["Secondly, as belonging, as a partial concept, to the whole representation of a thing, and thereby as ground of knowledge of this thing itself.",[]],"jaesche-s02-316":["All marks, considered as grounds of knowledge, have a twofold use, either internal or external. The internal use consists in derivation, in order to know the thing itself through marks as its grounds of knowledge. The external use consists in comparison, in so far as through marks we can compare one thing with others according to the rules of identity or diversity.",[]],"jaesche-s02-317":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-318":["Among marks there are various specific differences, on which the following classification of them is based.",[]],"jaesche-s02-319":["1) Analytic or synthetic marks. The former are partial concepts of my actual concept, which I already think in it; the latter, on the other hand, are partial concepts of the merely possible whole concept, which is first to come into being through a synthesis of several parts. The former are all concepts of reason; the latter may be concepts of experience.",[]],"jaesche-s02-320":["2) Co-ordinate or subordinate marks. This division of marks concerns their connection alongside or under one another.",[]],"jaesche-s02-321":["Marks are co-ordinate in so far as each of them is represented as an immediate mark of the thing; subordinate, in so far as one mark is represented in the thing only by means of another. The combination of co-ordinate marks into the whole of the concept is entitled an aggregate; the combination of subordinate marks, a series. The former, the aggregation of co-ordinate marks, constitutes the totality of the concept. In respect of synthetic empirical concepts, however, this totality can never be completed, but resembles a straight line without limits.",[]],"jaesche-s02-322":["The series of subordinate marks comes up *a parte ante*, that is, on the side of the grounds, against unanalysable concepts, which on account of their simplicity admit of no further analysis. *A parte post*, on the other hand, that is, in respect of the consequences, it is infinite, since we have indeed a highest *genus* but no lowest *species*.",[]],"jaesche-s02-323":["With the synthesis of each new concept in the aggregation of co-ordinate marks, extensive or expanded distinctness increases; just as, with the further analysis of concepts in the series of subordinate marks, intensive or deep distinctness increases. This latter kind of distinctness, since it necessarily serves the thoroughness and coherence of knowledge, is for that reason chiefly the business of philosophy, and is carried furthest in metaphysical investigations especially.",[]],"jaesche-s02-324":["3) Affirmative or negative marks. Through the former we know what the thing is; through the latter, what it is not.",[]],"jaesche-s02-325":["Negative marks serve to keep us from errors. They are therefore unnecessary where it is impossible to err, and are necessary and of importance only in those cases where they keep us from an important error into which we may easily fall. Thus, for example, in respect of the concept of a being such as God, negative marks are very necessary and important.",[]],"jaesche-s02-326":["Through affirmative marks, then, we seek to understand something; through negative marks (into which all marks whatsoever can be converted) we seek only not to misunderstand, or not to err, even though we should learn nothing about the thing thereby.",[]],"jaesche-s02-327":["4) Important and fruitful, or empty and unimportant, marks.",[]],"jaesche-s02-328":["A mark is important and fruitful if it is a ground of knowledge of great and numerous consequences. It is so partly in respect of its internal use, the use in derivation, in so far as it suffices for knowing a great deal about the thing itself; partly in respect of its external use, the use in comparison, in so far as it serves for knowing both the similarity of a thing to many others and its difference from many others.",[]],"jaesche-s02-329":["We must, moreover, here distinguish logical importance and fruitfulness from practical importance and fruitfulness, that is, from usefulness and serviceableness.",[]],"jaesche-s02-330":["5) Sufficient and necessary, or insufficient and contingent, marks.",[]],"jaesche-s02-331":["A mark is sufficient in so far as it suffices always to distinguish the thing from all others; otherwise it is insufficient, as, for example, the mark of barking in the case of the dog. The sufficiency of marks, like their importance, can, however, be determined only in a relative sense, in relation to the ends aimed at by a mode of knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s02-332":["Necessary marks, finally, are those which must always be found in the thing represented. Such marks are also entitled essential, and are opposed to the extra-essential and contingent marks, which can be separated from the concept of the thing.",[]],"jaesche-s02-333":["Among necessary marks, however, there is a further distinction.",[]],"jaesche-s02-334":["Some of them belong to the thing as grounds of other marks of one and the same thing; others, on the contrary, only as consequences of other marks.",[]],"jaesche-s02-335":["The former are primitive and constitutive marks (*constitutiva, essentialia in sensu strictissimo*). The latter are entitled attributes (*consectaria, rationata*); they too belong to the essence of the thing, but only in so far as they must first be derived from those essential elements [Stücke] of it; as, for example, the three angles in the concept of a triangle are derived from the three sides.",[]],"jaesche-s02-336":["Extra-essential marks are again of two kinds: they concern either internal determinations of a thing (*modi*) or its external relations (*relationes*). Thus, for example, the mark of learning signifies an internal determination of a man; being master or servant, only an external relation of him.",[]],"jaesche-s02-337":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-338":["The sum of all the essential elements of a thing, or the sufficiency of its marks in respect of co-ordination or subordination, is its essence (*complexus notarum primitivarum, interne conceptui dato sufficientium; s. complexus notarum, conceptum aliquem primitive constituentium*).",[]],"jaesche-s02-339":["In this definition, however, we must not here think at all of the real or natural essence of things, into which we are wholly unable to see. For since logic abstracts from all content of knowledge, and consequently from the thing itself, there can be question in this science only of the logical essence of things. And into this we can easily see. For it requires nothing more than knowledge of all the predicates in respect of which an object is determined through its concept; whereas the real essence of the thing (*esse rei*) requires knowledge of those predicates on which, as grounds of determination, everything belonging to its existence depends. If, for example, we wish to determine the logical essence of body, we have no need to seek the data for it in nature. We need only direct our reflection to the marks which, as essential elements (*constitutiva, rationes*), originally constitute its fundamental concept. For the logical essence is itself nothing but the first fundamental concept of all the necessary marks of a thing (*esse conceptus*).",[]],"jaesche-s02-340":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-341":["The first stage of the perfection of our knowledge in respect of quality is, then, its clearness. A second stage, or a high|er",[]],"jaesche-s02-342":["degree of clearness, is distinctness. This consists in the clearness of the marks.",[]],"jaesche-s02-343":["We must here first of all distinguish logical distinctness in general from aesthetic distinctness. The logical rests on the objective, the aesthetic on the subjective, clearness of the marks. The former is a clearness through concepts, the latter a clearness through intuition. The latter kind of distinctness thus consists in mere vividness and intelligibility, that is, in a mere clearness through examples *in concreto*. (For much may be intelligible which is not distinct; and conversely much may be distinct which is yet hard to understand, because it goes back to remote marks whose connection with intuition is possible only through a long series.)",[]],"jaesche-s02-344":["Objective distinctness often causes subjective obscurity, and conversely. Logical distinctness is therefore not seldom possible only to the detriment of aesthetic distinctness; and conversely aesthetic distinctness, through examples and similes which do not fit exactly but are taken only according to an analogy, is often harmful to logical distinctness. Besides, examples in general are not marks, and do not belong as parts to the concept, but, as intuitions, belong only to the use of the concept. A distinctness through examples, mere intelligibility, is therefore of a quite different kind from distinctness through concepts as marks. In the combination of the two, of aesthetic or popular with scholastic or logical distinctness, consists lucidity [Helligkeit]. For by a lucid mind [heller Kopf] we understand the talent for a luminous presentation of abstract and thorough modes of knowledge, suited to the comprehension of common understanding.",[]],"jaesche-s02-345":["As regards, next, logical distinctness in particular, it is to be entitled complete distinctness in so far as all the marks which, taken together, make up the whole concept have been brought to clearness. A concept may in turn be completely or perfectly distinct either in respect of the totality of its co-ordinate marks or in respect of the totality of its subordinate marks. In the total clearness of the co-ordinate marks consists the extensively complete or sufficient distinctness of a concept, which is also entitled exhaustiveness [Ausführlichkeit]. The total clearness of the subordinate marks constitutes intensively complete distinctness, profundity.",[]],"jaesche-s02-346":["The former kind of logical distinctness may also be entitled the external completeness (*completudo externa*), and the latter the internal completeness (*completudo interna*), of the clearness of the marks. The latter can be attained only in pure concepts of reason and in arbitrary concepts, not in empirical concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s02-347":["The extensive magnitude of distinctness, in so far as it is not superabundant, is entitled precision [Präcision, Abgemessenheit]. Exhaustiveness (*completudo*) and precision (*praecisio*) together constitute adequacy (*cognitionem, quae rem adaequat*). And in intensively adequate knowledge, in profundity, combined with extensively adequate knowledge in exhaustiveness and precision, consists (in respect of quality) the consummate perfection of a mode of knowledge (*consummata cognitionis perfectio*).",[]],"jaesche-s02-348":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-349":["Since, as we have remarked, it is the business of logic to make clear concepts distinct, the question now arises: In what way does it make them distinct?",[]],"jaesche-s02-350":["The logicians of the Wolffian school place all making distinct of modes of knowledge in their mere analysis. But not all distinctness rests on the analysis of a given concept. Distinctness arises in that way only in respect of those marks which we already thought in the concept, and by no means in respect of the marks which are first added to the concept as parts of the whole possible concept.",[]],"jaesche-s02-351":["That kind of distinctness which arises not through analysis but through synthesis of the marks is synthetic distinctness. There is, therefore, an essential difference between the two propositions: to make a distinct concept, and to make a concept distinct.",[]],"jaesche-s02-352":["For when I make a distinct concept, I begin with the parts and proceed from them to the whole. Here there are as yet no marks present; I first obtain them through the synthesis. From this synthetic procedure, then, there results synthetic distinctness, which actually extends my concept in respect of its content through what is added to it as a mark beyond the concept in (pure or empirical) intuition. — This synthetic procedure in making concepts dis|tinct",[]],"jaesche-s02-353":["is employed by the mathematician and also by the natural philosopher. For all distinctness of strictly mathematical knowledge, as of all knowledge from experience, rests on such an extension of it through synthesis of the marks.",[]],"jaesche-s02-354":["But when I make a concept distinct, my knowledge does not in the least increase in respect of content through this mere analysis. The content remains the same; only the form is altered, in that I learn merely to distinguish better, or to know with clearer consciousness, what already lay in the given concept. Just as nothing further is added to a map by merely colouring [Illumination] it, so the mere clarifying of a given concept by means of the analysis of its marks does not in the least augment the concept itself.",[]],"jaesche-s02-355":["To synthesis belongs the making distinct of objects; to analysis, the making distinct of concepts. In the latter the whole precedes the parts; in the former the parts precede the whole. The philosopher makes only given concepts distinct. Sometimes we proceed synthetically even when the concept which we wish to make distinct in this way is already given. This often occurs with propositions of experience, when we are not yet satisfied with the marks already thought in a given concept.",[]],"jaesche-s02-356":["The analytic procedure of producing distinctness, with which alone logic can concern itself, is the first and chief requirement in making our knowledge distinct. For the more distinct our knowledge of a thing is, the stronger and more effective it can also be. Only the analysis must not go so far that the object itself in the end vanishes in the process.",[]],"jaesche-s02-357":["Were we conscious of all that we know, we should be astonished at the great quantity of our modes of knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s02-358":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-359":["In respect of the objective content of our knowledge in general, the following degrees may be thought, according to which it can in this regard be raised:",[]],"jaesche-s02-360":["The first degree of knowledge is: to represent something to oneself;",[]],"jaesche-s02-361":["The second: to represent something to oneself with consciousness, or to perceive (*percipere*);",[]],"jaesche-s02-362":["The third: to be acquainted with something [*kennen*] (*noscere*), that is, to represent something to oneself in comparison with other things, in respect both of identity and of difference;",[]],"jaesche-s02-363":["The fourth: to be acquainted with something with consciousness, that is, to know it [*erkennen*] (*cognoscere*). Animals are also acquainted with objects, but they do not know them.",[]],"jaesche-s02-364":["The fifth: to understand something [*verstehen*] (*intelligere*), that is, to know it through the understanding by means of concepts, or to conceive it [*concipiren*]. This is very different from comprehending [*Begreifen*]. Much can be conceived that cannot be comprehended, for instance a *perpetuum mobile*, the impossibility of which is shown in mechanics.",[]],"jaesche-s02-365":["The sixth: to know something through reason, or to have insight into it [*einsehen*] (*perspicere*). We attain to this in few things; and our modes of knowledge become ever fewer in number the more we seek to perfect them in content.",[]],"jaesche-s02-366":["The seventh, finally: to comprehend something [*begreifen*] (*comprehendere*), that is, to know it through reason, or *a priori*, in the degree that suffices for our purpose. For all our comprehending is merely relative, that is, sufficient for a certain purpose; absolutely we comprehend nothing whatsoever. Nothing can be more fully comprehended than what the mathematician demonstrates, for instance that all lines in the circle are proportional. And yet he does not comprehend how it comes about that so simple a figure should have these properties. The field of understanding [*Verstehen*], or of the understanding [*Verstand*], is therefore in general far wider than the field of comprehending, or of reason.",[]],"jaesche-s02-367":["[Introduction.] IX. D) Logical Perfection of Knowledge in respect of Modality.",[]],"jaesche-s02-368":["Certainty. — Concept of holding-to-be-true [*Fürwahrhalten*] in general. — Modes of holding-to-be-true: opining, believing, knowing. — Conviction and persuasion. — Withholding and suspending a judgment. — Provisional judgments. — Prejudices, their sources and chief kinds.",[]],"jaesche-s02-369":["Truth is an objective property of knowledge, [namely] the judgment through which something is represented as true. The relation to an understanding, and so to a particular subject, is, subjectively, holding-to-be-true [*Fürwahrhalten*].",[]],"jaesche-s02-370":["Holding-to-be-true is in general of two kinds, certain or uncertain. Certain holding-to-be-true, or certainty, is bound up with consciousness of necessity; uncertain holding-to-be-true, or uncertainty, on the other hand, with consciousness of contingency, that is, of the possibility of the opposite. The latter, in turn, is either insufficient both subjectively and objectively, or else objectively insufficient but subjectively sufficient. The former is entitled opinion [*Meinung*]; the latter must be entitled belief [*Glaube*].",[]],"jaesche-s02-371":["Accordingly there are three kinds or modes of holding-to-be-true: opining, believing and knowing. Opining is a problematic, believing an assertoric, and knowing an apodeictic judging. For what I merely opine I hold in judging, and with consciousness [of doing so], to be only problematic. What I believe I hold to be assertoric, though not as objectively but only as subjectively necessary (valid only for me). What, finally, I know I hold to be apodeictically certain, that is, universally and objectively necessary (valid for all); and this even if the object itself, to which this certain holding-to-be-true refers, should be a merely empirical truth. For this distinction of holding-to-be-true according to the three *modi* just named concerns only the faculty of judgment, in respect of the subjective criteria for subsuming a judgment under objective rules.",[]],"jaesche-s02-372":["Thus, for example, our holding-to-be-true of immortality would be merely problematic in so far as we only act as if we were immortal; assertoric, in so far as we believe that we are immortal; and apodeictic, finally, in so far as we all knew that there is another life after this one.",[]],"jaesche-s02-373":["Between opining, believing and knowing there is, therefore, an essential difference, which we shall here set out more exactly and in greater detail.",[]],"jaesche-s02-374":["1) Opining. Opining, or holding-to-be-true on a ground of knowledge [*Erkenntnißgrund*] which is neither subjectively nor objectively sufficient, may be regarded as a provisional judging (*sub conditione suspensiva ad interim*), which we cannot easily dispense with. We must first opine before we accept and assert; but we must also guard against taking an opinion for anything more than mere opinion. In almost all our knowing we begin from opining. At times we have an obscure presentiment of the truth: a thing seems to us to contain marks of truth, and we divine its truth even before we know it with determinate certainty.",[]],"jaesche-s02-375":["But where, then, does mere opining properly have place? In none of the sciences that contain modes of knowledge *a priori*, and so neither in mathematics, nor in metaphysics, nor in morals, but solely in empirical modes of knowledge: in physics, psychology and the like. For it is in itself absurd to opine *a priori*. Nothing, indeed, could be more ridiculous than, for example, merely to opine in mathematics. Here, as in metaphysics and morals, the rule is: either to know or not to know. Matters of opinion can therefore only ever be objects of a knowledge by experience which is in itself possible, but impossible for us, owing to the empirical limitations and conditions of our power of experience and to the degree of that power which we possess and which depends on these conditions. Thus, for example, the ether of the modern physicists is a mere matter of opinion. For in regard to it, as in regard to every opinion whatsoever, I see that the opposite might perhaps after all be proved. My holding-to-be-true is therefore here insufficient both objectively and subjectively, although, considered in itself, it can become complete.",[]],"jaesche-s02-376":["2) Believing. Believing, or holding-to-be-true on a ground which is objectively insufficient but subjectively sufficient, refers to objects in regard to which we can not only know nothing, but can also opine nothing, nor even plead probability. We can only be certain that it is not contradictory to think such objects as we think them. The rest is here a free holding-to-be-true, which is necessary only for a practical purpose given *a priori*. It is thus a holding-to-be-true of what I accept on moral grounds, and in such manner that I am certain that the opposite can never be proved.*[1]",[]],"jaesche-s02-377":["Matters of belief are therefore I) not objects of empirical knowledge. So-called historical belief can therefore not proper|ly",[]],"jaesche-s02-378":["be entitled belief either, nor as such be opposed to knowing, since it can itself be a knowing. Holding-to-be-true on testimony differs neither in degree nor in kind from holding-to-be-true through one's own experience.",[]],"jaesche-s02-379":["II) Nor are they objects of rational knowledge (knowledge *a priori*), whether theoretical, as in mathematics and metaphysics, or practical, as in morals.",[]],"jaesche-s02-380":["Mathematical truths of reason may indeed be believed on testimony, since error is here partly not easily possible and partly also easily discovered; but in this way they cannot be known. Philosophical truths of reason, however, cannot even be believed; they must simply be known, for philosophy in its nature suffers no mere persuasion. And as regards in particular the objects of practical rational knowledge in morals, namely rights and duties, here too there can just as little be a mere believing. We must be entirely certain whether something is right or wrong, in accordance with duty or contrary to it, permitted or not permitted. In moral matters we can venture nothing on uncertainty, can decide nothing at the risk of transgressing the law. Thus, for example, it is not enough for the judge merely to believe that the man accused of a crime has actually committed it. He must know it (juridically), or he acts unconscientiously.",[]],"jaesche-s02-381":["III) Only those objects are matters of belief in which the holding-to-be-true is necessarily free, that is, not determined by objective grounds of truth independent of the nature and interest of the subject.",[]],"jaesche-s02-382":["Because of its merely subjective grounds, believing therefore yields no conviction which can be communicated and which commands universal assent, such as the conviction that comes from knowing. I myself can be certain only of the validity and unchangeableness of my practical belief; and my belief in the truth of a proposition or in the actuality of a thing is what, in relation to me, merely takes the place of a mode of knowledge, without itself being a mode of knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s02-383":["He is morally unbelieving who does not accept that which, though it is impossible to know, it is morally necessary to presuppose. This kind of unbelief always rests on a lack of moral interest. The greater a man's moral disposition, the firmer and more living will be his belief in all that he feels himself constrained, from moral interest and for a practically necessary purpose, to accept and presuppose.",[]],"jaesche-s02-384":["3) Knowing. Holding-to-be-true on a ground of knowledge which is sufficient both objectively and subjectively, that is, certainty, is either empirical or rational, according as it is based either on experience (one's own as well as that of others communicated to us) or on reason. This distinction thus refers to the two sources from which the whole of our knowledge is drawn: experience and reason.",[]],"jaesche-s02-385":["Rational certainty is in turn either mathematical or philosophical certainty. The former is intuitive, the latter discursive.",[]],"jaesche-s02-386":["Mathematical certainty is also entitled evidence [*Evidenz*], since intuitive knowledge is clearer than discursive. Although, therefore, mathematical and philosophical rational knowledge are both in themselves equally certain, the kind of certainty in each is different.",[]],"jaesche-s02-387":["Empirical certainty is original (*originarie empirica*) in so far as I become certain of something through my own experience, and derivative (*derivative empirica*) in so far as I become certain of something through the experience of others. The latter is also commonly entitled historical certainty.",[]],"jaesche-s02-388":["Rational certainty is distinguished from empirical certainty by the consciousness of necessity bound up with it. It is therefore an apodeictic certainty, whereas empirical certainty is only an assertoric one. We are rationally certain of that which we should have had insight into *a priori* even apart from all experience. Our modes of knowledge may therefore concern objects of experience, and the certainty of them may yet be at once empirical and rational, namely, in so far as we know an empirically certain proposition from principles *a priori*.",[]],"jaesche-s02-389":["We cannot have rational certainty of everything; but where we can have it, we must prefer it to empirical certainty.",[]],"jaesche-s02-390":["All certainty is either unmediated or mediated, that is, it either requires a proof, or is neither capable nor in need of any proof. However much in our knowledge may be certain only mediately, that is, only through a proof, there must yet be something indemonstrable or immediately certain; and the whole of our knowledge must proceed from immediately certain propositions.",[]],"jaesche-s02-391":["The proofs on which all mediated or mediate certainty of a mode of knowledge rests are either direct or indirect, that is, apagogic proofs. When I prove a truth from its grounds, I give a direct proof of it; when I infer the truth of a proposition from the falsity of its opposite, I give an apagogic proof. But if the latter is to be valid, the propositions must be opposed contradictorily or diametrically (*diametraliter*). For two propositions opposed to one another merely as contraries (*contrarie opposita*) may both be false. A proof which is the ground of mathematical certainty is entitled demonstration; and one which is the ground of philosophical certainty, an acroamatic proof. The essential parts of any proof whatsoever are its matter and its form, or the ground of proof and the consequence.",[]],"jaesche-s02-392":["From knowing [*Wissen*] comes science [*Wissenschaft*], by which is to be understood the sum of a knowledge as a system. It is opposed to common knowledge, that is, to the sum of a knowledge as a mere aggregate. The system rests on an idea of the whole which precedes the parts; in common knowledge, on the other hand, or in the mere aggregate of modes of knowledge, the parts precede the whole. There are historical sciences and sciences of reason.",[]],"jaesche-s02-393":["In a science we often know only the modes of knowledge, but not the things represented by them. There can therefore be a science of that of which our knowledge is not a knowing [*Wissen*].",[]],"jaesche-s02-394":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-395":["From the foregoing remarks on the nature and kinds of holding-to-be-true we can now draw the general result that all our conviction is either logical or practical. When, namely, we know that we are free from all subjective grounds, and yet the holding-to-be-true is sufficient, we are convinced, and indeed logically convinced, or convinced on objective grounds (the object is certain).",[]],"jaesche-s02-396":["But complete holding-to-be-true on subjective grounds which, in a practical relation, are as valid as objective grounds, is also conviction, only not logical but practical (I am certain). And this practical conviction, or this moral belief of reason [*Vernunftglaube*], is often firmer than all knowing. In knowing we still listen to counter-arguments, but in belief we do not, since here what matters is not objective grounds but the moral interest of the subject.*[2]",[]],"jaesche-s02-397":["Opposed to conviction is persuasion [*Überredung*], a holding-to-be-true on insufficient grounds of which we do not know whether they are merely subjective or also objective.",[]],"jaesche-s02-398":["Persuasion often precedes conviction. We are conscious of many modes of knowledge only in such a way that we cannot judge whether the grounds of our holding-to-be-true are objective or subjective. In order, therefore, to pass from mere persuasion to conviction, we must first reflect [*überlegen*], that is, see to which faculty of knowledge a mode of knowledge belongs; and then investigate [*untersuchen*], that is, examine whether the grounds are sufficient or insufficient in respect of the object. With many, matters remain at persuasion; with some they come to reflection; with few, to investigation. He who knows what belongs to certainty will not easily confuse persuasion and conviction, and so will not easily allow himself to be persuaded. There is a determining ground of assent which is compounded of objective and subjective grounds; and most men do not separate out this mixed effect.",[]],"jaesche-s02-399":["Although every persuasion is false in form (*formaliter*), in so far, namely, as an uncertain mode of knowledge here seems to be certain, it may yet be true in matter (*materialiter*). And it is thus distinguished also from opinion, which is an uncertain mode of knowledge in so far as it is held to be uncertain.",[]],"jaesche-s02-400":["The sufficiency of holding-to-be-true (in belief) may be put to the test by wagering or by swearing. The former requires comparative, the latter absolute, sufficiency of objective grounds. Where these are not present, a holding-to-be-true which is absolutely sufficient subjectively is nevertheless valid in their stead.",[]],"jaesche-s02-401":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-402":["We often make use of such expressions as: to give assent to one's judgment, to withhold, suspend or give up one's judgment. These and similar phrases seem to indicate that there is something arbitrary in our judging, inasmuch as we hold something to be true because we will to hold it true. The question therefore here arises: whether willing has an influence on our judgments?",[]],"jaesche-s02-403":["The will has no immediate influence on holding-to-be-true; that would indeed be very absurd. When it is said that we readily believe",[]],"jaesche-s02-404":["what we wish, this refers only to our benevolent wishes, for example those of a father in regard to his children. Were the will to have an immediate influence on our conviction of what we wish, we should be constantly forming chimeras of a happy condition, and should then also always hold them to be true. But the will cannot contend against convincing proofs of truths which run counter to its wishes and inclinations.",[]],"jaesche-s02-405":["In so far, however, as the will either impels the understanding to the investigation of a truth or holds it back from it, we must grant it an influence on the employment of the understanding, and so also, mediately, on conviction itself, since conviction depends so greatly on the employment of the understanding.",[]],"jaesche-s02-406":["As regards, in particular, the suspension or withholding of our judgment, it consists in the resolve not to allow a merely provisional judgment to become a determining one. A provisional judgment is one through which I represent to myself that there are indeed more grounds for the truth of a thing than against it, but that these grounds do not yet suffice for a determining or definitive judgment through which I decide outright in favour of its truth. Provisional judging is therefore a judging which, with consciousness [of the fact], is merely problematic.",[]],"jaesche-s02-407":["The withholding of judgment can take place with a twofold purpose: either in order to seek out the grounds of the determining judgment, or in order never to judge. In the former case the suspension of judgment is entitled critical (*suspensio judicii indagatoria*), in the latter sceptical (*suspensio judicii sceptica*). For the sceptic renounces all judging, whereas the true philosopher merely suspends his judgment so long as he has not yet sufficient grounds for holding something to be true.",[]],"jaesche-s02-408":["To suspend one's judgment in accordance with maxims requires a practised faculty of judgment, which is found only with advancing years. In general, the withholding of our assent is a very difficult matter: partly because our understanding is so eager to extend itself through judging and to enrich itself with knowledge [*Kenntnissen*], partly because our inclination is always directed more towards certain things than towards others. But he who has often had to retract his assent, and has thereby become prudent and cautious, will not give it so quickly, for fear of having later to retract his judgment a|gain.",[]],"jaesche-s02-409":["[*zurücknehmen zu müssen*.] Such retraction is always a mortification, and a cause of distrust towards all other knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s02-410":["We may further remark here that it is one thing to leave one's judgment *in dubio*, another to leave it *in suspenso*. In the latter case I always have an interest in the matter; in the former it is not always in keeping with my purpose and interest to decide whether the thing is true or not.",[]],"jaesche-s02-411":["Provisional judgments are very necessary, indeed indispensable, for the employment of the understanding in all meditation and investigation. For they serve to guide the understanding in its inquiries and to furnish it with various means to that end.",[]],"jaesche-s02-412":["When we meditate on an object we must always already judge provisionally, and, as it were, already scent the knowledge that will fall to our share through the meditation. And when we set out upon inventions or discoveries, we must always make ourselves a provisional plan; otherwise our thoughts proceed merely at random. Provisional judgments may therefore be thought of as maxims for the investigation of a thing. They might also be entitled anticipations, since we anticipate our judgment of a thing even before we have the determining judgment. Such judgments thus have their proper use, and rules could even be given as to how we ought to judge provisionally of an object.",[]],"jaesche-s02-413":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-414":["From provisional judgments prejudices must be distinguished.",[]],"jaesche-s02-415":["Prejudices are provisional judgments in so far as they are accepted as principles [*Grundsätze*]. Every prejudice is to be regarded as a principle of erroneous judgments; and from prejudices there arise, not prejudices, but erroneous judgments. We must therefore distinguish the false knowledge that arises from prejudice from its source, the prejudice itself. Thus, for example, the significance attributed to dreams is not in itself a prejudice, but an error arising from the general rule accepted [as valid], that what happens several times always happens, or is always to be held true. And this principle, under which the significance of dreams also falls, is a prejudice.",[]],"jaesche-s02-416":["Prejudices are at times true provisional judgments; it is only their being held by us as principles or as determining judgments that is wrong. The cause of this illusion is to be sought in the fact that subjective grounds are mistakenly taken for objective, through want of the reflection which must precede all judging. For although we may accept many modes of knowledge, for instance immediately certain propositions, without investigating them, that is, without examining the conditions of their truth, we neither can nor may judge of anything without reflecting, that is, without comparing a mode of knowledge with the faculty of knowledge (sensibility or understanding) from which it is supposed to arise. If, then, we accept judgments without this reflection, which is necessary even where no investigation takes place, prejudices arise therefrom, that is, principles of judging from subjective causes which are mistakenly taken for objective grounds.",[]],"jaesche-s02-417":["The chief sources of prejudices are imitation, custom and inclination.",[]],"jaesche-s02-418":["Imitation has a universal influence on our judgments; for it is a strong ground for holding to be true what others have given out as true. Hence the prejudice: what all the world does is right. As regards prejudices that have arisen from custom, they can be uprooted only in the course of time, the understanding, gradually checked and delayed in its judging by counter-arguments, being thereby brought little by little to an opposite way of thinking. But if a prejudice of custom has at the same time arisen through imitation, the man who has it is hardly to be cured of it. A prejudice from imitation may also be entitled the propensity to the passive employment of reason, or to the mechanism of reason in place of its spontaneity under laws.",[]],"jaesche-s02-419":["Reason is indeed an active principle, which ought to borrow nothing from the mere authority of others, nor even, where its pure employment is concerned, from experience. But the indolence of very many men makes them prefer to tread in the footsteps of others rather than exert their own powers of understanding. Such men can only ever become copies of others; and were all of this kind, the world would remain for ever in one and the same place. It is therefore most necessary and important not to train the young, as commonly happens, to mere imitation.",[]],"jaesche-s02-420":["There are many things which help to accustom us to the maxim of imitation, and thereby to make reason a fertile soil for prejudices. Among such aids to imitation are",[]],"jaesche-s02-421":["1) Formulae. These are rules whose expression serves as a model for imitation. They are, however, uncommonly useful as an aid in dealing with intricate propositions, and the most enlightened mind therefore seeks to invent such.",[]],"jaesche-s02-422":["2) Sayings [*Sprüche*], whose expression has a great precision of pregnant meaning, so that it seems the sense could not be comprised in fewer words. Such dicta (*dicta*), which must always be borrowed from others to whom a certain infallibility is ascribed, serve, on account of this authority, as rule and law. The dicta of the Bible are entitled sayings κατ' ἐξοχην.",[]],"jaesche-s02-423":["3) Sentences [*Sentenzen*], that is, propositions which commend themselves and often retain their authority for centuries, as products of a mature faculty of judgment, through the force of the thoughts that lie in them.",[]],"jaesche-s02-424":["4) Canons (*Canones*). These are universal doctrinal sayings which serve as the foundation of the sciences and indicate something sublime and well thought out. They may further be expressed in a sententious manner, so as to please the more.",[]],"jaesche-s02-425":["5) Proverbs (*proverbia*). These are popular rules of the common understanding, or expressions denoting its popular judgments. Since such merely provincial propositions serve only the common rabble as sentences and canons, they are not to be met with among people of more refined education.",[]],"jaesche-s02-426":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-427":["From the three general sources of prejudice indicated above, and in particular from imitation, there now arise many particular prejudices, of which we shall here touch upon the following as the most common.",[]],"jaesche-s02-428":["1) Prejudices of authority. To these belong:",[]],"jaesche-s02-429":["a) The prejudice of the authority of the person. When, in matters that rest on experience and testimony, we build our knowledge on the authority of other persons, we are not thereby guilty of any prejudice. For in matters of this kind, since we cannot ourselves experience everything or comprehend it with our own understanding, the authority of the person must be the foundation of our judgments. But when we make the authority of others the ground of our holding-to-be-true in regard to modes of rational knowledge, we accept these modes of knowledge on mere prejudice. For truths of reason hold anonymously: the question here is not, Who said it?, but, What did he say? It matters nothing whether a mode of knowledge is of noble descent. Yet the propensity to [defer to] the authority of great men is very common, partly owing to the limitation of one's own insight, partly from the desire to imitate what is described to us as great. There is the further fact that the authority of the person serves indirectly to flatter our vanity. The subjects of a mighty despot are proud that they are all treated alike by him, since the lowest can deem himself equal to the highest in so far as both are nothing in face of the unlimited power of their ruler. In the same way the admirers of a great man judge themselves equal, in so far as whatever advantages they may have over one another are to be accounted insignificant when viewed against the merits of the great man. For more than one reason, therefore, highly extolled great men lend no small encouragement to the propensity to the prejudice of the authority of the person.",[]],"jaesche-s02-430":["b) The prejudice of the authority of the multitude. It is chiefly the rabble that is inclined to this prejudice. For since it is unable to judge of the merits, abilities and knowledge of the person, it prefers to hold to the judgment of the multitude, on the presupposition that what all say must surely be true. With the rabble, however, this prejudice refers only to historical matters; in matters of religion, in which it is itself interested, it relies on the judgment of the learned.",[]],"jaesche-s02-431":["It is in general remarkable that the ignorant man has a prejudice in favour of learning, and the learned man, on the other hand, a prejudice in favour of the common understanding.",[]],"jaesche-s02-432":["When the learned man, after having pretty well run through the circle of the sciences, finds that all his labours do not afford him due satisfaction, he comes at last to have a dis|trust",[]],"jaesche-s02-433":["of learning, especially in regard to those speculations in which concepts cannot be made sensible and whose foundations are insecure, as for instance in metaphysics. But since he nevertheless believes that the key to certainty about certain objects must be found somewhere, he now seeks it in the common understanding, after having so long sought it in vain on the path of scientific inquiry.",[]],"jaesche-s02-434":["This hope, however, is very deceptive. For if the cultivated faculty of reason can accomplish nothing in regard to the knowledge of certain things, the uncultivated will assuredly accomplish just as little. In metaphysics, appeal to the pronouncements of the common understanding is everywhere quite inadmissible, since here no case can be exhibited *in concreto*. With morals, however, the case is admittedly otherwise. Not only can all rules in morals be given *in concreto*, but practical reason in general reveals itself more clearly and more correctly through the organ of the common than through that of the speculative employment of the understanding. Hence the common understanding often judges more correctly than the speculative in matters of morality and duty.",[]],"jaesche-s02-435":["c) The prejudice of the authority of the age. Here the prejudice in favour of antiquity is one of the most significant. We have indeed ground to judge favourably of antiquity; but that is ground only for a moderate respect, the limits of which we all too often overstep, by making the ancients the treasurers of knowledge and of the sciences, by raising the relative value of their writings to an absolute value, and by blindly entrusting ourselves to their guidance. To esteem the ancients so excessively is to lead the understanding back to its childhood and to neglect the use of one's own talent. We should also greatly err were we to believe that all the ancients wrote as classically as those whose writings have come down to us. For since time sifts everything, and only that survives which has an inner worth, we may not without reason assume that we possess only the best writings of the ancients.",[]],"jaesche-s02-436":["There are several causes by which the prejudice in favour of antiquity is produced and sustained.",[]],"jaesche-s02-437":["When something exceeds the expectation [we form] according to a general rule, we are at first surprised at it, and this surprise then often passes over into admiration. This is the case with the ancients, when we find in them something which, having regard to the circumstances of the times in which they lived, we did not look for. A second cause lies in the circumstance that acquaintance with the ancients and with antiquity evidences a learning and erudition which always earns respect, however common and insignificant in themselves the things may be that have been drawn from the study of the ancients. A third cause is the gratitude we owe the ancients for having broken the path for us to much knowledge. It seems fair to show them a special esteem for this, though we often exceed the due measure of it. A fourth cause, finally, is to be sought in a certain envy towards our contemporaries. He who cannot compete with the moderns extols the ancients at their expense, so that the moderns may not raise themselves above him.",[]],"jaesche-s02-438":["The opposite of this is the prejudice in favour of novelty. At times the authority of antiquity, and the prejudice in its favour, declined, especially at the beginning of this century, when the celebrated Fontenelle took the side of the moderns. In modes of knowledge that are capable of extension, it is very natural that we should place more trust in the moderns than in the ancients. But this judgment too is well-grounded only as a merely provisional judgment. If we make it a determining judgment, it becomes prejudice.",[]],"jaesche-s02-439":["2) Prejudices from self-love or logical egoism, according to which one holds the agreement of one's own judgment with the judgments of others to be a dispensable criterion of truth. They are opposed to the prejudices of authority, since they express themselves in a certain partiality for what is a product of one's own understanding, for instance one's own system of doctrine [*Lehrgebäude*].",[]],"jaesche-s02-440":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-441":["Is it good and advisable to leave prejudices standing, or even to encourage them? It is astonishing that in our age su|ch",[]],"jaesche-s02-442":["questions, especially that regarding the encouragement of prejudices, can still be propounded. To encourage someone's prejudices is the same as to deceive him with good intent. To leave prejudices untouched might still pass; for who can occupy himself with uncovering and removing the prejudices of everyone? But whether it would not be advisable to work with all our powers for their uprooting is surely another question. Old and deep-rooted prejudices are admittedly hard to combat, since they answer for themselves and are, as it were, their own judges. The leaving of prejudices undisturbed is also excused on the ground that disadvantages would result from their uprooting. But let these disadvantages be admitted; in the sequel they will bring all the more good. Notes:",[]],"jaesche-s02-443":["*[1] Believing is no special source of knowledge. It is a kind of holding-to-be-true which is consciously incomplete; and when it is regarded as restricted to a particular kind of objects (which belong only to belief), it is distinguished from opining, not by degree, but by the relation which it has, as knowledge, to action. Thus, for example, the merchant, in order to enter upon a transaction, needs not merely to opine that there will be something to gain by it, but //IX68// to believe it, that is, his opinion must be sufficient for an undertaking whose outcome is uncertain. Now we have theoretical modes of knowledge (of the sensible) in which we can attain to certainty; and in respect of everything that we can call human knowledge, this must be possible. Just such certain modes of knowledge, and indeed wholly *a priori*, we have in practical laws; but these are grounded on a supersensible principle (that of freedom), and indeed one in ourselves, as a principle of practical reason. But this practical reason is a causality in respect of an object likewise supersensible, the highest good, which is not possible in the sensible world through our power. Nevertheless nature, as object of our theoretical reason, must harmonise with it; for the consequence or effect of this idea is to be met with in the sensible world. We ought, therefore, to act so as to make this end actual.",[]],"jaesche-s02-444":["We find in the world of sense traces also of an artistic wisdom; and we thereupon believe that the cause of the world operates also with moral wisdom towards the highest good. This is an assent which is sufficient for action, that is, a belief. Now we do not need this belief in order to act according to moral laws, for these are given by practical reason alone. But we do need to assume a supreme wisdom as [providing] the object of our moral will, the object towards which, beyond the mere rightfulness of our actions, we cannot avoid directing our ends. Although objectively this would not be a necessary relation of our will [*Willkür*], the highest good is none the less, subjectively, the necessary object of a good (even of a human) will; and belief in the attainability of that good is necessarily presupposed for this purpose.",[]],"jaesche-s02-445":["Between the acquisition of knowledge through experience (*a posteriori*) and its acquisition through reason (*a priori*) there is no middle term. But between knowledge of an object and the mere presupposition of its possibility there is a middle term, namely, an empirical ground or a ground of reason for assuming that possibility, in relation to a necessary extension of the field of possible objects beyond those of which knowledge is possible to us. This necessity arises only in respect of that case in which the object is known as practical, and as practically necessary through reason; for to assume something merely for the purpose of extending theoretical knowledge is always contingent. This practically necessary presupposition of an object is the presupposition of the possibility of the highest good as object of the will [*Willkür*], and consequently also of the condition of this possibility (God, freedom and immortality). It is a subjective necessity of assuming the reality of the object for the sake of the necessary determination of the will. This is the *casus extraordinarius* [extraordinary case] without which practical reason cannot maintain itself in respect of its necessary end; and here the *favor necessitatis* [privilege of necessity] comes to its aid in its own judgment. Reason cannot acquire any object logically; it can only resist that which hinders it in the employment of this idea, which belongs to it practically. //IX69// [The Academy pagination marker stands in the German before 'allein'.]",[]],"jaesche-s02-446":["This belief is the necessity of assuming the objective reality of a concept (of the highest good), that is, the possibility of its object, as an object of the will [*Willkür*] which is *a priori* necessary. If we look merely to actions, we have no need of this belief. But if through our actions we would extend ourselves to the possession of the end which is thereby possible, we must assume that this end is altogether possible. I can therefore only say: I see myself constrained by my end, in accordance with laws of freedom, to assume a highest good in the world as possible; but I cannot constrain anyone else by grounds [to do so] (belief is free).",[]],"jaesche-s02-447":["Rational belief can therefore never be directed to theoretical knowledge; for there an assent which is objectively insufficient is mere opinion. It is simply a presupposition of reason from a subjective, but absolutely necessary, practical point of view. A disposition in accordance with moral laws leads to an object of a will [*Willkür*] determinable by pure reason. The assumption that this object is attainable, and therefore also of the reality of its cause, is a moral belief, that is, an assent which is free, and which is necessary from the moral point of view for the completion of one's ends.",[]],"jaesche-s02-448":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-449":["*Fides* is properly fidelity in a *pactum* [compact], or the subjective confidence of the parties in one another that each will keep his promise to the other: fidelity and faith [*Treue und Glauben*]. The first [applies] when the *pactum* has been made, the second when it is to be concluded.",[]],"jaesche-s02-450":["By analogy, practical reason is, as it were, the *promittent* [one who promises], man the *promissarius* [one to whom the promise is made], and the good expected from the deed the *promissum* [thing promised].",[]],"jaesche-s02-451":["*[2] This practical conviction is, then, the moral belief of reason. It alone must be entitled belief in the strictest sense, and as such be opposed to knowing and to all theoretical or logical conviction whatsoever, since it can never rise to knowing. So-called historical belief, on the other hand, must not, as already remarked, be distinguished from knowing, since, as a species of theoretical or logical assent, it can itself be knowing. We can accept an empirical truth on the testimony of others with the same certainty as if we had arrived at it through facts of our own experience. In the former kind of empirical knowing there is something deceptive; but so is there also in the latter.",[]],"jaesche-s02-452":["Historical, or mediate empirical, knowing rests on the reliability of testimony. Among the requirements of an unimpeachable witness are authenticity (competence) and integrity.",[]],"jaesche-s02-453":["[Einleitung.] X. Probability.",[]],"jaesche-s02-454":["— Definition of the probable. — Distinction between probability and plausibility. — Mathematical and philosophical probability. — Doubt, subjective and objective. — Sceptical, dogmatic and critical modes of thought, or methods of philosophising. — Hypotheses.",[]],"jaesche-s02-455":["To the doctrine of the certainty of our knowledge belongs also the doctrine of knowledge of the probable, which is to be regarded as an approximation to certainty.",[]],"jaesche-s02-456":["By probability is to be understood an assent on insufficient grounds, which, however, stand in a greater ratio to sufficient grounds than do the grounds of the opposite. By this definition we distinguish probability (*probabilitas*) from mere plausibility (*verisimilitudo*), which is an assent on insufficient grounds in so far as these are greater than the grounds of the opposite.",[]],"jaesche-s02-457":["For the ground of assent can be greater than that of the opposite either objectively or subjectively. Which of the two it is can be discovered only by comparing the grounds of assent with sufficient grounds; for in that case the grounds of assent are greater than the grounds of the opposite can be. In probability, therefore, the ground of assent is objectively valid; in mere plausibility, on the other hand, it is only subjectively valid. Plausibility is merely a magnitude of persuasion; probability is an approximation to certainty. In probability there must always be a standard by which I can estimate it. This standard is certainty. For since I am to compare the insufficient grounds with the sufficient, I must know how much is required for certainty. Such a standard is lacking, however, in mere plausibility, since there I compare the insufficient grounds, not with the sufficient, but only with the grounds of the opposite.",[]],"jaesche-s02-458":["The factors [*Momente*] of probability may be either homogeneous or heterogeneous. If they are homogeneous, as in mathematical knowledge, they must be counted; if they are heterogeneous, as in philosophical knowledge, they must be weighed, that is, estimated by their effect, and this effect, again, by the overcoming of obstacles in the mind. The latter yield no ratio to certainty, but only a ratio of one plausibility to another. It follows that only the mathematician can determine the ratio of insufficient grounds to the sufficient ground; the philosopher must content himself with plausibility, an assent which is merely subjectively and practically sufficient. For in philosophical knowledge, owing to the heterogeneity of the grounds, probability cannot be estimated; here the weights are not, so to speak, all stamped. Strictly, therefore, it can be said only of mathematical probability that it is more than half of certainty.",[]],"jaesche-s02-459":["There has been much talk of a logic of probability (*logica probabilium*). But such a logic is not possible; for if the ratio of the insufficient grounds to the sufficient cannot be weighed mathematically, no rules are of any help. Nor indeed can any universal rules of probability be given, except [these]: that error will not fall all on one side, but that there must be a ground of agreement in the object; and likewise that, when errors are made on two opposite sides in equal number and in equal degree, the truth lies in the mean.",[]],"jaesche-s02-460":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-461":["Doubt is a counter-ground to assent, or a mere obstacle to it, and may be regarded either subjectively or objectively. Subjectively, doubt is sometimes taken as the state of an irresolute mind; objectively, as the knowledge that the grounds of assent are insufficient. In the latter respect it is entitled an objection, that is, an objective ground for holding to be false a mode of knowledge [hitherto] held to be true.",[]],"jaesche-s02-462":["A counter-ground to assent which is valid only subjectively is a scruple. In a scruple one does not know whether the obstacle to assent is grounded objectively or only subjectively, for instance only in inclination, habit and the like. One doubts without being able to give a distinct and determinate account of the ground of the doubt, and without being able to see whether this ground lies in the object itself or only in the subject. If such scruples are to be removed, they must be raised to the distinctness and determinateness of an objection. For it is through objections that certainty is brought to distinctness and completeness; and no one can be certain of a matter unless counter-grounds have been stirred up, by which it can be determined how far one still is from certainty, or how near to it. Nor is it enough that every doubt should merely be answered; it must also be resolved, that is, it must be made comprehensible how the scruple arose. If this is not done, the doubt is only dismissed, not removed; the seed of doubt then still remains. In many cases, indeed, we cannot know whether the obstacle to assent in us has only subjective or objective grounds, and so cannot remove the scruple by exposing the illusion; for we cannot always compare our modes of knowledge with the object, but often only with one another. It is therefore modesty to put forward one's objections only as doubts.",[]],"jaesche-s02-463":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-464":["There is a principle of doubting which consists in the maxim of treating modes of knowledge with the aim of rendering them uncertain and of showing the impossibility of attaining to certainty. This method of philosophising is the sceptical mode of thought, or scepticism. It is opposed to the dogmatic mode of thought, or dogmatism,|",[]],"jaesche-s02-465":["which is a blind confidence in the power of reason to extend itself *a priori*, without criticism, through mere concepts, merely on account of its apparent success.",[]],"jaesche-s02-466":["Both methods, when they become universal, are faulty. For there are many items of knowledge [*Kenntnisse*] in respect of which we cannot proceed dogmatically; and, on the other side, scepticism, by renouncing all assertoric knowledge, destroys all our efforts to attain to the possession of knowledge of what is certain.",[]],"jaesche-s02-467":["But however harmful this scepticism may be, the sceptical method is none the less useful and purposive, provided that by it we understand nothing more than the manner of treating something as uncertain and bringing it to the highest uncertainty, in the hope of thereby finding the track of truth. This method, then, is properly a mere suspension of judgment. It is very useful to the critical procedure, by which is to be understood that method of philosophising in which one investigates the sources of one's assertions or objections and the grounds on which they rest, a method which gives hope of attaining to certainty.",[]],"jaesche-s02-468":["In mathematics and physics scepticism has no place. Only that knowledge which is neither mathematical nor empirical, namely, the purely philosophical, can have given occasion to it. Absolute scepticism declares everything to be illusion. It therefore distinguishes illusion from truth, and must accordingly possess a criterion of the difference; consequently it presupposes a knowledge of truth, and thereby contradicts itself.",[]],"jaesche-s02-469":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-470":["We have remarked above of probability that it is a mere approximation to certainty. This is now especially the case with hypotheses, through which we can never attain to apodeictic certainty in our knowledge, but always only to a degree of probability, now greater, now less.",[]],"jaesche-s02-471":["A hypothesis is an assent to the judgment that a ground is true, on account of the sufficiency of its consequences; or, more briefly, the holding of a presupposition to be true as a ground.",[]],"jaesche-s02-472":["All assent in hypotheses is therefore based on the fact that the presupposition, as ground, suffices to explain other modes of knowledge from it, as its consequences. For here we infer from the truth of the consequence to the truth of the ground. But, as has already been remarked above, this mode of inference yields a sufficient criterion of truth, and can lead to apodeictic certainty, only if all the possible consequences of an assumed ground are true. Since we can never determine all the possible consequences, it is evident that hypotheses always remain hypotheses, that is, presuppositions of whose complete certainty we can never be assured. Nevertheless the probability of a hypothesis may increase, and rise to an analogue of certainty, namely, when all the consequences which have so far come before us can be explained from the presupposed ground. For in such a case there is no reason why we should not assume that all possible consequences will be explicable from it. In this case, therefore, we give ourselves up to the hypothesis as if it were completely certain, although it is so only by induction.",[]],"jaesche-s02-473":["And yet something in every hypothesis must be apodeictically certain, namely:",[]],"jaesche-s02-474":["1) The possibility of the presupposition itself. If, for instance, we assume a subterranean fire to explain earthquakes and volcanoes, such a fire must at least be possible, if not as a flaming body, yet as a heated one. But to make the earth into an animal, in which the circulation of the internal fluids produces heat, for the sake of explaining certain other appearances, is to put forward a mere fiction and not a hypothesis. For actualities may indeed be invented, but not possibilities; these must be certain.",[]],"jaesche-s02-475":["2) The consequence. The consequences must follow correctly from the assumed ground; otherwise the hypothesis becomes a mere chimera.",[]],"jaesche-s02-476":["3) Unity. It is an essential requirement of a hypothesis that it should be one only, and should need no auxiliary hypotheses to support it. If in a hypothesis we have to call several others to our aid, it thereby loses much of its probability. For the more consequences can be derived from a hypothesis, the more probable it is; the fewer, the less probable. Thus, for instance, the hypothesis of Tycho Brahe did not suffice to explain many appearances; he therefore assumed several new hypo|theses to supplement it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-477":["Here it may already be surmised that the assumed hypothesis cannot be the true ground. The Copernican system, on the other hand, is a hypothesis from which everything that is to be explained by it, so far as it has hitherto come before us, can be explained. Here we need no auxiliary hypotheses (*hypotheses subsidiarias*). [The German begins with 'an', completing the verb 'nahm … an' of the preceding passage.]",[]],"jaesche-s02-478":["There are sciences which permit no hypotheses, such as mathematics and metaphysics. But in natural science they are useful and indispensable.",[]],"jaesche-s02-479":["[Einleitung.] Appendix.",[]],"jaesche-s02-480":["Of the Distinction between Theoretical and Practical Knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s02-481":["A mode of knowledge is called practical in opposition to theoretical knowledge, but also in opposition to speculative knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s02-482":["Practical modes of knowledge are, namely, either:",[]],"jaesche-s02-483":["1) imperatives, and in so far opposed to theoretical modes of knowledge; or they contain",[]],"jaesche-s02-484":["2) the grounds of possible imperatives, and are in so far opposed to speculative modes of knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s02-485":["By an imperative in general is to be understood any proposition which states a possible free action whereby a certain end is to be made actual. Every mode of knowledge, therefore, which contains imperatives is practical, and is to be called practical in opposition to theoretical knowledge. For theoretical modes of knowledge are those which state, not what ought to be, but what is, and which therefore have as their object not an acting but a being.",[]],"jaesche-s02-486":["If, on the other hand, we oppose practical modes of knowledge to speculative, they may also be theoretical, provided only that imperatives can be derived from them. Regarded in this respect, they are then practical in content (*in potentia*) or objectively. For by speculative modes of knowledge we understand those from which no rules of conduct can be derived, or which contain no grounds of possible imperatives. Of such merely speculative propositions there are, for instance, a great many in theology. Speculative modes of knowledge of this kind are therefore always theoretical; but it does not follow conversely|",[]],"jaesche-s02-487":["that every theoretical mode of knowledge is speculative; regarded in another respect, it may at the same time be practical as well.",[]],"jaesche-s02-488":["Everything in the end comes down to the practical; and in this tendency of everything theoretical and of all speculation in respect of its use consists the practical value of our knowledge. This value, however, is unconditioned only if the end to which the practical use of knowledge is directed is an unconditioned end. The one unconditioned and ultimate end (final end), to which all practical use of our knowledge must ultimately be related, is morality, which on that account we also entitle the practical without qualification, or absolutely practical. And that part of philosophy which has morality for its object would accordingly have to be entitled practical philosophy *κατ' ἐξοχην* [par excellence]; although every other philosophical science may always have its practical part as well, that is, may contain, in respect of the theories it sets forth, directions for their practical use in the realisation of certain ends.",[]],"jaesche-s02-489":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-001":["I. General Doctrine of Elements.",[]],"jaesche-s03-002":["First Section. Of Concepts. [§§ 1 – 16]",[]],"jaesche-s03-003":["§ 1. Concept in general, and its distinction from intuition.",[]],"jaesche-s03-004":["All modes of knowledge, that is, all representations related with consciousness to an object, are either intuitions or concepts. An intuition is a singular representation (*repraesentatio singularis*); a concept is a universal representation (*repraesentatio per notas communes*) or reflected representation (*repraesentatio discursiva*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-005":["Knowledge through concepts is entitled thought (*cognitio discursiva*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-006":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-007":["Note 1. The concept is opposed to the intuition; for it is a universal representation, or a representation of what is common to several objects, and therefore a representation in so far as it can be contained in different [objects].",[]],"jaesche-s03-008":["2. It is mere tautology to speak of universal or common concepts. The error rests on an incorrect division of concepts into universal, particular and singular. Not concepts themselves, but only their use, can be so divided.",[]],"jaesche-s03-009":["§ 2. Matter and form of concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s03-010":["In every concept we must distinguish matter and form. The matter of concepts is the object; their form is universality.",[]],"jaesche-s03-011":["§ 3. Empirical and pure concept.",[]],"jaesche-s03-012":["A concept is either an empirical or a pure concept (*vel empiricus vel intellectualis*). A pure concept is one which is not abstracted from experience, but which originates from the understanding even as regards its content.",[]],"jaesche-s03-013":["An idea is a concept of reason, whose object cannot be met with in experience at all.",[]],"jaesche-s03-014":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-015":["Note 1. The empirical concept originates from the senses through comparison of the objects of experience, and receives from the understanding merely the form of universality. The reality of these concepts rests on actual experience, from which, as regards their content, they are drawn. But whether there are pure concepts of understanding (*conceptus puri*) which, as such, originate solely from the understanding, independently of all experience, is a question which metaphysics must investigate.",[]],"jaesche-s03-016":["2. Concepts of reason, or ideas, can never lead to actual objects, since all such objects must be contained in a possible experience. They serve, however, to guide the understanding through reason, in respect of experience and of the employment of its rules in the greatest perfection. They serve also to show that not all possible things are objects of experience, and that the principles of the possibility of experience do not hold of things in themselves, nor of objects of experience regarded as things in themselves.",[]],"jaesche-s03-017":["The idea contains the archetype of the use of the understanding. An example is the idea of the world-whole, which must be necessary, not as a constitutive principle for the empirical use of understanding, but only as a regulative principle for the sake of the thoroughgoing connection of our empirical use of understanding. It is therefore to be regarded as a necessary fundamental concept, [serving] either to complete objectively the acts of subordination performed by the understanding or to regard them as unlimited. — Nor can the idea be obtained by composition, for the whole is prior to the part. There are, however, ideas to which an approximation is possible. This is the case with the mathematical ideas, or ideas of the mathematical generation of a whole. These differ essentially from the dynamical ideas, which are wholly heterogeneous from all concrete concepts, since the whole differs from the concrete concepts not in magnitude (as with the mathematical ideas) but in kind.",[]],"jaesche-s03-018":["Objective reality cannot be secured for, or proved of, any theoretical idea, save only the idea of freedom; and this because freedom is the condition of the moral law, whose reality is an axiom. The reality of the idea of God can be proved only by means of this [idea of freedom], and therefore only from a practical point of view, that is, [as a ground for] acting as if there were a God; it can thus be proved only for this purpose.",[]],"jaesche-s03-019":["In all sciences, especially those of reason, the idea of the science is its general sketch or outline, and therefore the extent of all the items of knowledge [*Kenntnisse*] that belong to it. Such an idea of the whole, the first thing to be looked to and sought for in a science, is architectonic: for instance, the idea of jurisprudence.",[]],"jaesche-s03-020":["The idea of humanity, the idea of a perfect republic, of a happy life, and the like, is lacking in most men. Many men have no idea of what they want, and therefore proceed according to instinct and authority.",[]],"jaesche-s03-021":["§ 4. Given (*a priori* or *a posteriori*) and made concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s03-022":["All concepts are, as regards their matter, either given (*conceptus dati*) or made (*conceptus factitii*). The former are given either *a priori* or *a posteriori*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-023":["All concepts given empirically or *a posteriori* are entitled concepts of experience; those given *a priori*, notions.",[]],"jaesche-s03-024":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-025":["Note. The form of a concept, as a discursive representation, is always made.",[]],"jaesche-s03-026":["§ 5. Logical origin of concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s03-027":["The origin of concepts as regards their mere form rests on reflection, and on abstraction from the difference of the things designated by a certain representation. The question therefore arises: What acts of the understanding constitute a concept, or, what is the same thing, are required for generating a concept from given representations?",[]],"jaesche-s03-028":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-029":["Note 1. Since general logic abstracts from all content of knowledge through concepts, that is, from all matter of thought, it can consider the concept only in respect of its form, that is, only subjectively. It considers, not how the concept determines an object through a mark, but only how it can be related to several objects. General logic has not, therefore, to investigate the source of concepts. It has to consider, not how concepts originate as representations, but solely how given representations become concepts in thought, whether these concepts contain something derived from experience, or something fictitious, or something borrowed from the nature of the understanding. — This logical origin of concepts, their origin as regards their mere form, consists in reflection, whereby a representation common to several objects (*conceptus communis*) arises, as that form which is required for judgment [*Urteilskraft*]. In logic, therefore, only the difference of reflection in concepts is considered.",[]],"jaesche-s03-030":["2. The origin of concepts in respect of their matter, according to which a concept is either empirical, or arbitrary, or intellectual, is considered in metaphysics.",[]],"jaesche-s03-031":["§ 6. Logical acts of comparison, reflection and abstraction.",[]],"jaesche-s03-032":["The logical acts of understanding by which concepts are generated as regards their form are:",[]],"jaesche-s03-033":["1) comparison, that is, the comparing of representations with one another in relation to the unity of consciousness;",[]],"jaesche-s03-034":["2) reflection, that is, the consideration of how different representations can be comprehended in one consciousness; and finally",[]],"jaesche-s03-035":["3) abstraction, or the setting aside of everything else in which the given representations differ.",[]],"jaesche-s03-036":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-037":["Note 1. In order to make concepts out of representations we must therefore be able to compare, to reflect and to abstract; for these three logical operations of the understanding are the essential and universal conditions of the generation of every concept whatsoever. I see, for instance, a spruce, a willow and a lime. By first comparing these objects with one another, I note that they differ from one another in respect of trunk, branches, leaves and the like. Next, however, I reflect only on what they have in common, the trunk, the branches, the leaves themselves, and abstract from their size, shape and so forth. In this way I obtain a concept of tree.",[]],"jaesche-s03-038":["2. The expression 'abstraction' is not always correctly used in logic. We must not say: to abstract something (*abstrahere aliquid*), but: to abstract from something (*abstrahere ab aliquo*). If, for instance, in thinking of scarlet cloth I think only of the red colour, I abstract from the cloth. If I abstract from this also, and think of scarlet as a material stuff in general, I abstract from still more determinations, and my concept has thereby become still more abstract. For the more differences of things are left out of a concept, or the more determinations in it are abstracted from, the more abstract is the concept. Abstract concepts should therefore properly be called abstracting concepts (*conceptus abstrahentes*), that is, concepts in which several abstractions occur. The concept of body, for instance, is not properly an abstract concept; for from body itself I cannot abstract, else I should not have the concept of it. But I must indeed abstract from size, colour, hardness or fluidity, in short from all the special determinations of particular bodies. The most abstract concept is that which has nothing in common with any concept differing from it. This is the concept of something; for what differs from it is nothing, and therefore has nothing in common with something.",[]],"jaesche-s03-039":["3. Abstraction is only the negative condition under which universally valid representations can be generated; the positive condition is comparison and reflection. For no concept comes into being through abstraction; abstraction only completes it and encloses it within its determinate limits.",[]],"jaesche-s03-040":["§ 7. Content and extension of concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s03-041":["Every concept, as a partial concept, is contained in the representation of things; as a ground of knowledge, that is, as a mark, these things are contained under it. In the former respect every concept has a content; in the latter, an extension.",[]],"jaesche-s03-042":["The content and the extension of a concept stand in inverse ratio to one another. The more a concept contains under it, the less it contains in it, and conversely.",[]],"jaesche-s03-043":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-044":["Note. The universality or universal validity of a concept rests, not on its being a partial concept, but on its being a ground of knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s03-045":["§ 8. Magnitude of the extension of concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s03-046":["The extension or sphere of a concept is the greater, the more things stand under it and can be thought through it.",[]],"jaesche-s03-047":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-048":["Note. Just as we say of a ground in general that it contains the consequence under it, so we may also say of a concept that, as a ground of knowledge, it contains under it all those things from which it has been abstracted; the concept of metal, for instance, contains gold, silver, copper and so forth. For since every concept, as a universally valid representation, contains what is common to several representations of different things, all these things, which are in so far contained under it, can be represented through it. It is precisely this that constitutes the usefulness of a concept. The more things can be represented through a concept, the greater is its sphere. Thus the concept of body, for instance, has a greater extension than the concept of metal.",[]],"jaesche-s03-049":["§ 9. Higher and lower concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s03-050":["Concepts are entitled higher (*conceptus superiores*) in so far as they have other concepts under them, which in relation to them are entitled lower concepts. A mark of a mark, a remote mark, is a higher concept; the concept in relation to a remote mark is a lower concept.",[]],"jaesche-s03-051":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-052":["Note. Since higher and lower concepts are so entitled only relatively (*respective*), one and the same concept can, in different relations, be at once higher and lower. Thus, for instance, the concept man is a higher concept in relation to the concept Negro, but a lower concept in relation to the concept animal.",[]],"jaesche-s03-053":["§ 10. Genus and species.",[]],"jaesche-s03-054":["The higher concept is entitled, in respect of its lower concept, genus (*genus*); the lower concept, in respect of its higher, species (*species*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-055":["Like higher and lower concepts, therefore, concepts of genus and of species are distinguished, not by their nature, but only in respect of their relation to one another (*termini a quo* or *ad quod*) in logical subordination.",[]],"jaesche-s03-056":["§ 11. Highest genus and lowest species.",[]],"jaesche-s03-057":["The highest genus is that which is not a species (*genus summum non est species*), just as the lowest species is that which is not a genus (*species, quae non est genus, est infima*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-058":["In accordance with the law of continuity, however, there can be neither a lowest nor a next species.",[]],"jaesche-s03-059":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-060":["Note. Let us think a series of several concepts subordinated to one another, for instance iron, metal, body, substance, thing. Here we can always obtain higher genera (for every species is always to be regarded at the same time as a genus in respect of its lower concept, as for instance the concept scholar in respect of the concept philosopher), until at last we come to a genus which cannot in turn be a species. And we must ultimately be able to arrive at such a genus, since there must in the end be a highest concept (*conceptum summum*) from which, as such, nothing further can be abstracted without the whole concept vanishing. — But a lowest concept (*conceptum infimum*), or a lowest species, under which no other would be contained, is not to be found in the series of species and genera, since such a concept cannot possibly be determined. For even if we have a concept which we apply immediately to individuals, there may still be specific differences in respect of it, which we either do not notice or disregard. Only comparatively, for [practical] use, are there lowest concepts, which have acquired this significance as it were by convention, in so far as it has been agreed not to go deeper in the matter.",[]],"jaesche-s03-061":["In regard to the determination of concepts of species and of genus the following universal law therefore holds: There is a genus which can no longer be a species; but there is no species which should not in turn be capable of being a genus.",[]],"jaesche-s03-062":["§ 12. Wider and narrower concept. Reciprocal concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s03-063":["The higher concept is also entitled a wider concept; the lower, a narrower concept.",[]],"jaesche-s03-064":["Concepts which have the same sphere are entitled reciprocal concepts (*conceptus reciproci*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-065":["§ 13. Relation of the lower to the higher, and of the wider to the narrower, concept.",[]],"jaesche-s03-066":["The lower concept is not contained in the higher, for it contains more in it than does the higher; but it is nevertheless contained under the higher, because the higher contains the ground of knowledge of the lower.",[]],"jaesche-s03-067":["Further, one concept is wider than another, not because it contains more under it (for that cannot be known), but in so far as it contains under it the other concept, and more besides.",[]],"jaesche-s03-068":["§ 14. Universal rules in regard to the subordination of concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s03-069":["In respect of the logical extension of concepts the following universal rules hold:",[]],"jaesche-s03-070":["1) What belongs to, or contradicts, the higher concepts belongs also to, or contradicts, all the lower concepts which are contained under those higher concepts; and",[]],"jaesche-s03-071":["2) conversely: what belongs to, or contradicts, all the lower concepts belongs also to, or contradicts, their higher concept.",[]],"jaesche-s03-072":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-073":["Note. That in which things agree follows from their universal properties, and that in which they differ from one another follows from their particular properties. We cannot therefore infer that what belongs to, or contradicts, one lower concept belongs also to, or contradicts, other lower concepts which, together with it, belong to one higher concept. We cannot, for instance, infer that what does not belong to man does not belong to the angels either.",[]],"jaesche-s03-074":["§ 15. Conditions of the origin of higher and lower concepts: logical abstraction and logical determination.",[]],"jaesche-s03-075":["Through continued logical abstraction ever higher concepts arise, just as, on the other hand, through continued logical determination ever lower concepts arise. The greatest possible abstraction yields the highest or most abstract concept, that is, the concept from which no further determination can be thought away. The highest completed determination would yield a thoroughly determined concept (*conceptum omnimode determinatum*), that is, a concept to which no further determination could be added in thought.",[]],"jaesche-s03-076":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-077":["Note. Since only single things or individuals are thoroughly determined, there can be thoroughly determined modes of knowledge only as intuitions, not as concepts. In respect of concepts, logical determination can never be regarded as complete (§ 11, Note).",[]],"jaesche-s03-078":["§ 16. Use of Concepts *in abstracto* and *in concreto*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-079":["Every concept can be used universally and particularly (*in abstracto* and *in concreto*). The lower concept is used *in abstracto* in respect of its higher concept; the higher concept is used *in concreto* in respect of its lower concept.",[]],"jaesche-s03-080":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-081":["Note 1. The expressions ‘abstract’ and ‘concrete’ thus refer not so much to concepts in themselves (for every concept is an abstract concept) as only to their use. This use, in turn, can have different degrees, according as we treat a concept now more, now less abstractly or concretely, that is, according as we now omit or add more, now fewer, determinations. Through the ab|stract",[]],"jaesche-s03-082":["use a concept comes nearer to the highest genus; through the concrete use, on the other hand, [it comes] nearer to the individual.",[]],"jaesche-s03-083":["2. Which use of concepts, the abstract or the concrete, has the advantage over the other? Nothing can be decided on this point. The value of the one is not to be esteemed less than the value of the other. Through very abstract concepts we know little in many things; through very concrete concepts we know much in few things. What we gain on the one side, therefore, we lose again on the other. A concept which has a large sphere is in so far very serviceable, in that it can be applied to many things; but there is, in return, all the less contained in it. In the concept ‘substance’, for instance, I do not think as much as in the concept ‘chalk’.",[]],"jaesche-s03-084":["3. The art of popularity consists in striking the right proportion, within the same knowledge, between the representation *in abstracto* and *in concreto*, that is, between concepts and their presentation [*Darstellung*]. By this proportion the maximum of knowledge, in extension as well as in content, is attained.",[]],"jaesche-s03-085":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-086":["Section II. Of Judgments. [§§ 17 — 40]",[]],"jaesche-s03-087":["§ 17. Definition of a Judgment in General.",[]],"jaesche-s03-088":["A judgment is the representation of the unity of the consciousness of various representations, or the representation of their relation in so far as they constitute a concept.",[]],"jaesche-s03-089":["§ 18. Matter and Form of Judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-090":["To every judgment there belong, as its essential constituents, matter and form. The matter of the judgment consists in the given modes of knowledge which are combined in the judgment into the unity of consciousness; its form consists in the determination of the manner in which the various representations, as such, belong to one consciousness.",[]],"jaesche-s03-091":["§ 19. The Object of Logical Reflection is the Mere Form of Judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-092":["Since logic abstracts from all real or objective difference of knowledge, it can concern itself as little with the matter of judgments as with the content of concepts. It has, therefore, to take into consideration solely the difference of judgments in respect of their mere form.",[]],"jaesche-s03-093":["§ 20. Logical Forms of Judgments: Quantity, Quality, Relation and Modality.",[]],"jaesche-s03-094":["The differences of judgments in respect of their form can be traced back to the four chief moments of quantity, quality, relation and modality. In respect of these moments just as many different kinds of judgment are determined.",[]],"jaesche-s03-095":["§ 21. Quantity of Judgments: Universal, Particular, Singular.",[]],"jaesche-s03-096":["In respect of quantity, judgments are either universal or particular or singular, according as the subject in the judgment is either wholly included in or excluded from the notion of the predicate, or is only in part included in it and in part excluded from it. In the universal judgment the sphere of one concept is enclosed wholly within the sphere of another. In the particular judgment a part of the former is enclosed under the sphere of the other. Finally, in the singular judgment a concept which has no sphere at all is enclosed, and therefore merely as a part, under the sphere of another.",[]],"jaesche-s03-097":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-098":["Note 1. In respect of logical form, singular judgments are to be esteemed equal in use to universal judgments; for in both the predicate holds of the subject without exception. In the singular proposition ‘Caius is mortal’, for instance, there can be no more an exception than in the universal proposition ‘All men are mortal’. For there is only one Caius.",[]],"jaesche-s03-099":["2. In regard to the universality of a mode of knowledge, there is a real difference between general and universal propositions, though this difference is admittedly no concern of logic. General propositions are those which contain merely something of the universal [character] of certain objects, and which consequently do not contain sufficient conditions of subsumption; for instance, the proposition ‘Proofs must be made thorough’. Universal propositions are those which assert something universally of an object.",[]],"jaesche-s03-100":["3. Universal rules are universal either analytically or synthetically. The former abstract from the differences; the latter attend to the distinctions, and consequently determine in respect of them also. The more simply an object is thought, the more readily is analytic universality possible in consequence of a concept.",[]],"jaesche-s03-101":["4. If universal propositions cannot be understood in their universality without being known *in concreto*, they cannot serve as a standard, and therefore cannot be valid heuristically in their application. They are only problems [*Aufgaben*] for the investigation of the universal grounds of what has first become known in particular cases. Take, for example, the proposition: ‘He who has no interest in lying and knows the truth, speaks the truth.’ This proposition cannot be understood in its universality, because we know the restriction to the condition of disinterestedness only through experience, namely, [we know only from experience] that men can lie from interest. This comes from their not adhering firmly to morality. It is an observation which teaches us to know the weakness of human nature.",[]],"jaesche-s03-102":["5. Of particular judgments it is to be observed that, if they are to be capable of being understood through reason, and so are to have a rational and not merely an intellectual (abstracted) form, the subject must be a wider concept (*conceptus latior*) than the predicate. Let the predicate always be = O, the subject , then",[]],"jaesche-s03-103":["{}",[]],"jaesche-s03-104":["is a particular judgment; for some of what belongs under a is b, some is not b. This follows from reason. But let it be",[]],"jaesche-s03-105":["{}",[]],"jaesche-s03-106":["then at least all a can be contained under b, if it is smaller, but not if it is larger; it is therefore particular only contingently.",[]],"jaesche-s03-107":["§ 22. Quality of Judgments: Affirmative, Negative, Infinite.",[]],"jaesche-s03-108":["In respect of quality, judgments are either affirmative or negative or infinite. In the affirmative judgment the subject is thought under the sphere of a predicate; in the negative it is posited outside the sphere of the latter; and in the infinite it is posited in the sphere of a concept which lies outside the sphere of another.",[]],"jaesche-s03-109":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-110":["Note 1. The infinite judgment indicates not merely that a subject is not contained under the sphere of a predicate, but that it lies somewhere in the infinite sphere outside the sphere of that predicate. This judgment consequently represents the sphere of the predicate as limited.",[]],"jaesche-s03-111":["Everything possible is either A or *non* A. If, then, I say that something is *non* A, for instance, ‘The human soul is non-mortal’, ‘Some men are non-learned’, and the like, this is an infinite judgment. For it does not determine, beyond the finite sphere A, under what concept the object belongs, but solely that it belongs in the sphere outside A. This is properly no sphere at all, but only the bordering of a sphere on the infinite, or the limitation itself. Now although exclusion is a negation, the limitation of a concept is nevertheless a positive act. Limits are therefore positive concepts of limited objects.",[]],"jaesche-s03-112":["2. According to the principle of the exclusion of any third (*exclusi tertii*), the sphere of a concept, relatively to another, is either exclusive or inclusive. Now since logic has to do merely with the form of the judgment, not with concepts in respect of their content, the distinction of infinite from negative judgments does not belong to this science.",[]],"jaesche-s03-113":["3. In negative judgments the negation always affects the copula; in infinite judgments it is not the copula but the predicate that is affected by the negation. This can best be expressed in Latin.",[]],"jaesche-s03-114":["§ 23. Relation of Judgments: Categorical, Hypothetical, Disjunctive.",[]],"jaesche-s03-115":["In respect of relation, judgments are either categorical or hypothetical or disjunctive. For the given representations in the judgment are subordinated one to another for the unity of consciousness either as predicate to subject, or as consequence to ground, or as member of the division to the divided concept. Categorical judgments are determined by the first relation, hypothetical by the second, and disjunctive by the third.",[]],"jaesche-s03-116":["§ 24. Categorical Judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-117":["In categorical judgments subject and predicate constitute the matter. The form, through which the relation (of agreement or of conflict) between subject and predicate is determined and expressed, is entitled the copula.",[]],"jaesche-s03-118":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-119":["Note. The categorical judgments do indeed constitute the matter of the other judgments. We must not on that account believe, as several logicians do, that hypothetical as well as disjunctive judgments are nothing more than different guises [*Einkleidungen*] of categorical judgments, and can therefore all be reduced to the latter. All three kinds of judgment rest on essentially different logical functions of the understanding, and must therefore be considered according to their specific difference.",[]],"jaesche-s03-120":["§ 25. Hypothetical Judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-121":["The matter of hypothetical judgments consists of two judgments which are connected with each other as ground and consequence. The one of these judgments, which contains the ground, is the antecedent (*antecedens, prius*); the other, which stands to it in the relation of consequence, is the consequent (*consequens, posterius*). The representation of this kind of connection of the two judgments with one another for the unity of consciousness is entitled the consequence [*Consequenz*]; it constitutes the form of hypothetical judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-122":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-123":["Note 1. What the *copula* is for categorical judgments, the consequence is, therefore, for hypothetical judgments, namely, their form.",[]],"jaesche-s03-124":["2. Some believe that it is easy to transform a hypothetical proposition into a categorical one. But this cannot be done, since the two are by their nature entirely different from each other. In categorical judgments nothing is problematic; everything is assertoric. In hypothetical judgments, on the other hand, only the consequence is assertoric. In the latter I can therefore connect two false judgments with each other; for what matters here is only the correctness of the connection, that is, the form of the consequence, and on this the logical truth of these judgments rests. There is an essential difference between the two propositions ‘All bodies are divisible’ and ‘If all bodies are composite, they are divisible’. In the former proposition I assert the matter directly; in the latter, only under a condition expressed problematically.",[]],"jaesche-s03-125":["§ 26. Modes of Connection in Hypothetical Judgments: *modus ponens* and *modus tollens*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-126":["The form of connection in hypothetical judgments is twofold: the positing (*modus ponens*) or the removing (*modus tollens*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-127":["1) If the ground (*antecedens*) is true, the consequence (*consequens*) determined by it is also true. This is entitled the *modus ponens*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-128":["2) If the consequence (*consequens*) is false, the ground (*antecedens*) is also false. [This is the] *modus tollens*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-129":["§ 27. Disjunctive Judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-130":["A judgment is disjunctive if the parts of the sphere of a given concept determine one another in the whole, or towards a whole, as complements (*complementa*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-131":["§ 28. Matter and Form of Disjunctive Judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-132":["The several given judgments of which the disjunctive judgment is composed constitute its matter, and are entitled the members of the disjunction or opposition. The form of these judgments consists in the disjunction itself, that is, in the determination of the relation of the various judgments as members of the whole sphere of the divided knowledge, members which mutually exclude and complement one another.",[]],"jaesche-s03-133":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-134":["Note. All disjunctive judgments, therefore, represent different judgments as standing in the community of one sphere, and produce each judgment only through",[]],"jaesche-s03-135":["the limitation of the others in respect of the whole sphere. They thus determine the relation of each judgment to the whole sphere, and thereby at the same time the relation which these different members of the division (*membra disjuncta*) have to one another. Here, then, one member determines every other only in so far as they all stand in community as parts of a whole sphere of knowledge, outside of which, in a certain respect, nothing can be thought.",[]],"jaesche-s03-136":["§ 29. Peculiar Character of Disjunctive Judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-137":["The peculiar character of all disjunctive judgments, by which their specific difference, in respect of the moment of relation, from the other judgments, and especially from categorical judgments, is determined, consists in this: the members of the disjunction are all problematic judgments. Nothing else is thought of them than that, as parts of the sphere of a mode of knowledge, each the complement of the other towards the whole (*complementum ad totum*), they are, taken together, equal to the sphere of the first [i.e. of the divided knowledge]. From this it follows that the truth must be contained in one of these problematic judgments, or, what is the same thing, that one of them must hold assertorically. For outside them the sphere of the knowledge, under the given conditions, comprises nothing further, and each is opposed to the other. Consequently neither can anything else outside them be true, nor can more than one among them be true.",[]],"jaesche-s03-138":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-139":["Note. In a categorical judgment the thing whose representation is regarded as a part of the sphere of another, subordinated representation is regarded as contained under this, its higher concept. Here, therefore, in the subordination of the spheres, the part of the part is compared with the whole. But in disjunctive judgments I go from the whole to all the parts taken together. What is contained under the sphere of a concept is also contained under one of the parts of this sphere. Accordingly the sphere must first be divided. If, for instance, I make the disjunctive judgment ‘A learned man is either a historical scholar or a scholar of reason’, I thereby determine that these concepts are, in respect of their sphere, parts of the sphere of the learned, but by no means parts of one another, and that taken all together they are complete.",[]],"jaesche-s03-140":["That in disjunctive judgments it is not the sphere of the divided concept that is regarded as contained in the sphere of the divisions, but rather what is contained under the divided concept that is regarded as contained under one of the members of the division, may be made more evident by the following schema of comparison between categorical and disjunctive judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-141":["In categorical judgments, x, which is contained under b, is also under a:",[]],"jaesche-s03-142":["{}",[]],"jaesche-s03-143":["In disjunctive judgments, x, which is contained under a, is contained either under b or under c, etc.:",[]],"jaesche-s03-144":["{}",[]],"jaesche-s03-145":["The division in disjunctive judgments therefore indicates the co-ordination not of the parts of the whole concept, but of all the parts of its sphere. Here I think many things through one concept; there, one thing through many concepts, for instance the *definitum* through all the marks of co-ordination.",[]],"jaesche-s03-146":["§ 30. Modality of Judgments: Problematic, Assertoric, Apodeictic.",[]],"jaesche-s03-147":["In respect of modality, the moment by which the relation of the whole judgment to the faculty of knowledge is determined, judgments are either problematic or assertoric or apodeictic. The problematic are accompanied by consciousness of the mere possibility of the judging, the assertoric by consciousness of its actuality, and the apodeictic, finally, by consciousness of its necessity.",[]],"jaesche-s03-148":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-149":["Note 1. This moment of modality therefore indicates only the manner in which something is affirmed or denied in the judgment: whether we settle nothing as to the truth or untruth of a judgment, as in the proble|matic",[]],"jaesche-s03-150":["judgment ‘The soul of man may be immortal’; or whether we determine something about it, as in the assertoric judgment ‘The human soul is immortal’; or, finally, whether we express the truth of a judgment even with the dignity of necessity, as in the apodeictic judgment ‘The soul of man must be immortal’. This determination of merely possible or actual or necessary truth therefore concerns only the judgment itself, and in no way the thing about which judgment is passed.",[]],"jaesche-s03-151":["2. In problematic judgments, which may also be defined as those whose matter is given together with the possible relation between predicate and subject, the subject must always have a smaller sphere than the predicate.",[]],"jaesche-s03-152":["3. On the difference between problematic and assertoric judging rests the true difference between judgments and propositions [*Sätze*]. This difference is commonly, but wrongly, placed in mere expression through words, without which, after all, we could not judge at all. In the judgment the relation of different representations to the unity of consciousness is thought merely as problematic; in a proposition, on the other hand, as assertoric. A problematic proposition is a *contradictio in adjecto*. Before I have a proposition, I must surely first judge; and I judge about much that I do not settle, which I must, however, do as soon as I determine a judgment as a proposition. It is, moreover, well to judge problematically first, before accepting the judgment as assertoric, so as to test it in this way. Nor is it always necessary for our purpose to have assertoric judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-153":["§ 31. Exponible Judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-154":["Judgments in which an affirmation and a negation are contained at the same time, but in a concealed manner, so that the affirmation is made distinctly but the negation covertly, are exponible propositions.",[]],"jaesche-s03-155":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-156":["Note. In the exponible judgment ‘Few men are learned’, for instance, there lie, 1) though in a concealed manner, the negative judgment ‘Many men are not learned’, and 2) the affirmative judgment ‘Some men are learned’. Since the nature of exponible propositions depends solely on conditions of language, according to which two judgments can be expressed at once and briefly, the observation that there can be judgments in our language which must be expounded belongs not to logic but to grammar.",[]],"jaesche-s03-157":["§ 32. Theoretical and Practical Propositions.",[]],"jaesche-s03-158":["Those propositions are entitled theoretical which refer to the object and determine what does or does not belong to it. Practical propositions, on the other hand, are those which state the action whereby, as its necessary condition, an object becomes possible.",[]],"jaesche-s03-159":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-160":["Note. Logic has to treat only of practical propositions in respect of their form, and in so far as they are opposed to theoretical propositions. Practical propositions in respect of their content, and in so far as they are distinguished from speculative propositions, belong to morals.",[]],"jaesche-s03-161":["§ 33. Indemonstrable and Demonstrable Propositions.",[]],"jaesche-s03-162":["Demonstrable propositions are those which are capable of proof; those which are not capable of proof are entitled indemonstrable.",[]],"jaesche-s03-163":["Immediately certain judgments are indemonstrable, and are therefore to be regarded as elementary propositions.",[]],"jaesche-s03-164":["§ 34. Fundamental Propositions [*Grundsätze*].",[]],"jaesche-s03-165":["Immediately certain judgments *a priori* may be entitled fundamental propositions, in so far as other judgments can be proved from them, while they themselves cannot be subordinated to any other. For this reason they are also entitled principles (beginnings).",[]],"jaesche-s03-166":["§ 35. Intuitive and Discursive Fundamental Propositions: Axioms and Acroams.",[]],"jaesche-s03-167":["Fundamental propositions are either intuitive or discursive. The former can be presented in intuition and are entitled axioms (*axiomata*); the latter can be expressed only through concepts, and may be entitled acroams (*acroamata*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-168":["§ 36. Analytic and Synthetic Propositions.",[]],"jaesche-s03-169":["Those propositions are entitled analytic whose certainty rests on identity of the concepts (of the predicate with the notion of the subject). Propositions whose truth is not grounded on identity of the concepts must be entitled synthetic.",[]],"jaesche-s03-170":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-171":["Note 1. ‘To everything x to which the concept of body (a+b) belongs, extension (b) also belongs’ is an example of an analytic proposition.",[]],"jaesche-s03-172":["‘To everything x to which the concept of body (a+b) belongs, attraction (c) also belongs’ is an example of a synthetic proposition. Synthetic propositions increase knowledge *materialiter*, analytic propositions merely *formaliter*. The former contain determinations (*determinationes*), the latter only logical predicates.",[]],"jaesche-s03-173":["2. Analytic principles are not axioms, for they are discursive. And synthetic principles, too, are axioms only when they are intuitive.",[]],"jaesche-s03-174":["§ 37. Tautological Propositions.",[]],"jaesche-s03-175":["The identity of the concepts in analytic judgments can be either explicit (*explicita*) or [*der*, read *oder*] non-explicit (*implicita*). In the former case the analytic propositions are tautological.",[]],"jaesche-s03-176":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-177":["Note 1. Tautological propositions are *virtualiter* empty or void of consequences, for they are without utility or use. Such, for instance, is the tautological proposition ‘Man is man’. For if I know nothing further to say of man than that he is a man, I know nothing whatsoever further about him.",[]],"jaesche-s03-178":["Implicitly identical propositions, on the other hand, are not void of consequences or fruitless; for they make clear, through development (*explicatio*), the predicate which lay undeveloped (*implicite*) in the concept of the subject.",[]],"jaesche-s03-179":["2. Propositions void of consequences must be distinguished from propositions void of sense. The latter are empty of meaning [*leer an Verstand*] because they concern the determination of so-called hidden properties (*qualitates occultae*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-180":["§ 38. Postulate and Problem.",[]],"jaesche-s03-181":["A postulate is a practical, immediately certain proposition, or a fundamental proposition which determines a possible action, in which it is presupposed that the manner of carrying it out is immediately certain.",[]],"jaesche-s03-182":["Problems (*problemata*) are demonstrable propositions which require direction, or propositions which state an action whose manner of execution is not immediately certain.",[]],"jaesche-s03-183":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-184":["Note 1. There can also be theoretical postulates on behalf of practical reason. These are theoretical hypotheses necessary for the purposes of practical reason, such as those of the existence of God, of freedom, and of another world.",[]],"jaesche-s03-185":["2. To a problem there belong 1) the question, which contains what is to be accomplished; 2) the resolution, which contains the manner in which what is to be accomplished can be carried out; and 3) the demonstration that, when I shall have proceeded in this way, what is required will come about.",[]],"jaesche-s03-186":["§ 39. Theorems, Corollaries, Lemmas and Scholia.",[]],"jaesche-s03-187":["Theorems are theoretical propositions which are capable of proof and in need of it. Corollaries are immediate consequences of one of the preceding propositions. Lemmas (*lemmata*) are entitled those propositions which are not native to the science in which they are presupposed as proved, but are borrowed from other sciences. Scholia, finally, are mere explanatory propositions, which therefore do not belong as members to the whole of the system.",[]],"jaesche-s03-188":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-189":["Note. The essential and universal moments of every theorem are the thesis and the demonstration. The difference between theorems and corollaries may, moreover, also be placed in this, that the latter are inferred immediately, whereas the former are drawn through a series of consequences from immediately certain propositions.",[]],"jaesche-s03-190":["§ 40. Judgments of Perception and Judgments of Experience.",[]],"jaesche-s03-191":["A judgment of perception is merely subjective; an objective judgment from perceptions is a judgment of experience.",[]],"jaesche-s03-192":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-193":["Note. A judgment from mere perceptions is hardly possible except through my stating my representation as a perception: ‘I, who perceive a tower, perceive in it the red colour.’ But I cannot say ‘It is red’. For this would be not merely an empirical judgment but also a judgment of experience, that is, an empirical judgment through which I obtain a concept of the object. For instance, ‘On touching the stone I sense warmth’ is a judgment of perception; ‘The stone is warm’, on the other hand, is a judgment of experience. It belongs to the latter that I do not reckon to the object what is merely in my subject; for a judgment of experience is the perception from which a concept of the object arises. [An example is the question] whether luminous points move in the moon, or in the air, or in my eye.",[]],"jaesche-s03-194":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-195":["Section III. Of Inferences. [§§ 41 — 43]",[]],"jaesche-s03-196":["§ 41. Inference in General.",[]],"jaesche-s03-197":["By inferring is to be understood that function of thought whereby one judgment is derived from another. An inference in general is therefore the derivation of one judgment from another.",[]],"jaesche-s03-198":["§ 42. Immediate and Mediate Inferences.",[]],"jaesche-s03-199":["All inferences are either immediate or mediate.",[]],"jaesche-s03-200":["An immediate inference (*consequentia immediata*) is the derivation (*deductio*) of one judgment from another without a mediating judgment (*judicium intermedium*). An inference is mediate when, besides the concept which a judgment contains in itself, we require others in order to derive a mode of knowledge from it.",[]],"jaesche-s03-201":["§ 43. Inferences of the Understanding, Inferences of Reason, and Inferences of Judgment.",[]],"jaesche-s03-202":["Immediate inferences are also entitled inferences of the understanding. All mediate inferences, on the other hand, are either inferences of reason or inferences of judgment. We treat here first of immediate inferences, or inferences of the understanding.",[]],"jaesche-s03-203":["[Section III. Of Inferences.] I. Inferences of the Understanding.[§§ 44 — 55]",[]],"jaesche-s03-204":["§ 44. Peculiar Nature of Inferences of the Understanding.",[]],"jaesche-s03-205":["The essential character of all immediate inferences, and the principle of their possibility, consists solely in an alteration of the mere form of the judgments, while the matter of the judgments, the subject and predicate, remains unaltered and the same.",[]],"jaesche-s03-206":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-207":["Note 1. Because in immediate inferences only the form and in no way the matter of the judgments is altered, these inferences are essentially distinguished from all mediate inferences. In the latter the judgments differ in respect of their matter also, since here a new concept must be added, as mediating judgment or as middle term (*terminus medius*), in order to infer the one judgment from the other. If, for instance, I infer ‘All men are mortal, therefore Caius also is mortal’, this is not an immediate inference. For here I require for the conclusion the further mediating judgment ‘Caius is a man’; and through this new concept the matter of the judgments is altered.",[]],"jaesche-s03-208":["2. A *judicium intermedium* can indeed be made in inferences of the understanding also, but this mediating judgment is then merely tautological. Thus, for instance, in the immediate inference ‘All men are mortal. Some men are men. Therefore some men are mortal’, the middle term is a tautological proposition.",[]],"jaesche-s03-209":["§ 45. *Modi* of the Inferences of the Understanding.",[]],"jaesche-s03-210":["The inferences of the understanding run through all the classes of the logical functions of judging, and are consequently determined, in their chief kinds, by the moments of quantity, quality, relation and modality. On this rests the following division of these inferences.",[]],"jaesche-s03-211":["§ 46. 1. Inferences of the Understanding (in Relation to the Quantity of Judgments) *per judicia subalternata*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-212":["In inferences of the understanding *per judicia subalternata* the two judgments differ in respect of quantity, and the particular judgment is here derived from the universal, in accordance with the fundamental proposition: From the universal the inference to the particular is valid (*ab universali ad particulare valet consequentia*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-213":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-214":["Note. A *judicium* is entitled *subalternatum* in so far as it is contained under another, as, for instance, particular judgments under universal.",[]],"jaesche-s03-215":["§ 47. 2. Inferences of the Understanding (in Relation to the Quality of Judgments) *per judicia opposita*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-216":["In inferences of the understanding of this kind the alteration concerns the quality of the judgments, considered in relation to opposition. Now since this opposition can be threefold, the following special division of immediate inference results: through contradictorily opposed, through contrary, and through subcontrary judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-217":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-218":["Note. Inferences of the understanding through equipollent judgments (*judicia aequipollentia*) cannot properly be entitled inferences, for here no consequence takes place. They are rather to be regarded as a mere substitution of words which designate one and the same concept, the judgments themselves remaining unaltered even in respect of form. For instance, ‘Not all men are virtuous’ and ‘Some men are not virtuous’. Both judgments say one and the same thing.",[]],"jaesche-s03-219":["§ 48. a. Inferences of the Understanding *per judicia contradictorie opposita*./",[]],"jaesche-s03-220":["In inferences of the understanding through judgments which are contradictorily opposed to each other, and which as such constitute genuine, pure opposition, the truth of the one of the contradictorily opposed judgments is inferred from the falsity of the other, and conversely. For the genuine opposition which takes place here contains neither more nor less than what belongs to opposition. In accordance with the principle of excluded middle, therefore, the two contradictory judgments cannot both be true; but [*auch*] just as little can they both be false. If, therefore, the one is true, the other is false, and conversely.",[]],"jaesche-s03-221":["§ 49. b. Inferences of the Understanding *per judicia contrarie opposita*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-222":["Contrary or conflicting judgments (*judicia contrarie opposita*) are judgments of which the one is universally affirmative, the other universally negative. Now since one of them states more than the other, and since the falsity may lie in the surplus which, beyond the mere negation of the other, it further states, they cannot indeed both be true, but they can both be false. In respect of these judgments, therefore, only the inference from the truth of the one to the falsity of the other is valid, but not conversely.",[]],"jaesche-s03-223":["§ 50. c. Inferences of the Understanding *per judicia subcontrarie opposita*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-224":["Subcontrary judgments are those of which the one particularly (*particulariter*) affirms or denies what the other particularly denies or affirms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-225":["Since they can both be true, but cannot both be false, only the following inference is valid in respect of them: If the one of these propositions is false, the other is true; but not conversely.",[]],"jaesche-s03-226":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-227":["Note. In subcontrary judgments no pure, strict opposition takes place; for what is affirmed or denied in the one is not denied or affirmed of the same objects in the other. In the inference, for instance, ‘Some men are learned, therefore some men are not learned’, what is asserted in the first judgment is not asserted of the same men of whom it is denied in the other.",[]],"jaesche-s03-228":["§ 51. 3. Inferences of the Understanding (in Respect of the Relation of Judgments) *per judicia conversa sive per conversionem*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-229":["Immediate inferences through conversion concern the relation of judgments. They consist in the transposition of subjects and predicates in the two judgments, so that the subject of the one judgment is made the predicate of the other, and conversely.",[]],"jaesche-s03-230":["§ 52. Pure and Altered Conversion.",[]],"jaesche-s03-231":["In conversion the quantity of the judgments is either altered or it remains unaltered. In the former case the converted judgment (*conversum*) differs in quantity from the converting judgment (*convertente*), and the conversion is entitled an altered conversion (*conversio per accidens*). In the latter case the conversion is entitled a pure conversion (*conversio simpliciter talis*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-232":["§ 53. Universal Rules of Conversion.",[]],"jaesche-s03-233":["In regard to inferences of the understanding through conversion the following rules hold:",[]],"jaesche-s03-234":["1) Universal affirmative judgments can be converted only *per accidens*. For the predicate in these judgments is a wider concept, and only some of what falls under it is therefore contained in the concept of the subject.",[]],"jaesche-s03-235":["2) But all universal negative judgments can be converted *simpliciter*. For here the subject is removed from the sphere of the predicate. Likewise, finally,",[]],"jaesche-s03-236":["3) all particular affirmative propositions can be converted *simpliciter*. For in these judgments a part of the sphere of the subject has been subsumed under the predicate; therefore a part of the sphere of the predicate can also be subsumed under the subject.",[]],"jaesche-s03-237":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-238":["Note 1. In universal affirmative judgments the subject is regarded as a *contentum* of the predicate, since it is con|tained",[]],"jaesche-s03-239":["under the sphere of the predicate. I may therefore only infer, for instance: ‘All men are mortal, therefore some of those who are contained under the concept “mortal” are men.’ The reason why universal negative judgments can be converted *simpliciter* is this: two concepts which contradict each other universally contradict each other in equal extension.",[]],"jaesche-s03-240":["2. Some universal affirmative judgments can indeed also be converted *simpliciter*. But the ground of this lies not in their form but in the particular character of their matter, as, for instance, in the two judgments ‘Everything unchangeable is necessary’ and ‘Everything necessary is unchangeable’.",[]],"jaesche-s03-241":["§ 54. 4. Inferences of the Understanding (in Relation to the Modality of Judgments) *per judicia contraposita*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-242":["The immediate mode of inference through contraposition consists in that transposition (*metathesis*) of the judgments in which only the quantity remains the same, while the quality is altered. [Such inferences] concern only the modality of judgments, in that they transform an assertoric into an apodeictic judgment.",[]],"jaesche-s03-243":["§ 55. Universal Rule of Contraposition.",[]],"jaesche-s03-244":["In regard to contraposition the universal rule holds:",[]],"jaesche-s03-245":["All universal affirmative judgments can be contraposed *simpliciter*. For if the predicate is denied, as that which contains the subject under it, and therefore its whole sphere is denied, then a part of that sphere, that is, the subject, must also be denied.",[]],"jaesche-s03-246":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-247":["Note 1. The metathesis of judgments by conversion and that by contraposition are therefore opposed to each other in so far as the former alters only the quantity, the latter only the quality.",[]],"jaesche-s03-248":["2. The above-mentioned modes of immediate inference relate only to categorical judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-249":["[Dritter Abschnitt. Von den Schlüssen.] II. Syllogisms [Vernunftschlüsse]. [§§ 56 — 80]",[]],"jaesche-s03-250":["§ 56. Syllogism in general.",[]],"jaesche-s03-251":["A syllogism is the knowledge of the necessity of a proposition through the subsumption of its condition under a given universal rule.",[]],"jaesche-s03-252":["§ 57. Universal principle of all syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-253":["The universal principle upon which the validity of all inference by reason rests can be expressed determinately in the following formula:",[]],"jaesche-s03-254":["What stands under the condition of a rule stands also under the rule itself.",[]],"jaesche-s03-255":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-256":["Note. The syllogism premises a universal rule and a subsumption under its condition. We thereby know the conclusion *a priori*, not in the individual case, but as contained in the universal and as necessary under a certain condition. And this, that everything stands under the universal and is determinable in universal rules, is precisely the principle of rationality or of necessity (*principium rationalitatis sive necessitatis*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-257":["§ 58. Essential constituents of the syllogism.",[]],"jaesche-s03-258":["To every syllogism belong the following three essential parts:",[]],"jaesche-s03-259":["1) a universal rule, which is entitled the major premiss (*propositio major*);",[]],"jaesche-s03-260":["2) the proposition which subsumes a mode of knowledge under the condition of the universal rule, and which is entitled the minor premiss (*propositio minor*); and finally",[]],"jaesche-s03-261":["3) the proposition which affirms or denies the predicate of the rule of the subsumed knowledge: the conclusion (*conclusio*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-262":["The first two propositions, in their combination with each other, are entitled the antecedent propositions or premisses.",[]],"jaesche-s03-263":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-264":["Note. A rule is an assertion under a universal condition. The relation of the condition to the assertion, namely, how the latter stands under the former, is the exponent of the rule.",[]],"jaesche-s03-265":["The knowledge that the condition is found (somewhere) is the subsumption.",[]],"jaesche-s03-266":["The combination of that which has been subsumed under the condition with the assertion of the rule is the inference.",[]],"jaesche-s03-267":["§ 59. Matter and form of syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-268":["The matter of syllogisms consists in the antecedent propositions or premisses; their form consists in the conclusion, in so far as it contains the consequence.",[]],"jaesche-s03-269":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-270":["Note 1. In every syllogism, therefore, we must examine first the truth of the premisses and then the correctness of the consequence. In rejecting a syllogism we must never reject the conclusion first, but always first either the premisses or the consequence.",[]],"jaesche-s03-271":["2. In every syllogism the conclusion is given at once, as soon as the premisses and the consequence are given.",[]],"jaesche-s03-272":["§ 60. Division of syllogisms (according to relation) into categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive.",[]],"jaesche-s03-273":["All rules (judgments) contain objective unity of consciousness of the manifold of knowledge, and therefore a condition under which one mode of knowledge belongs with another to one consciousness. Now only three conditions of this unity can be thought: [the condition may serve] as subject of the inherence of marks, or as ground of the dependence of one mode of knowledge on another, or finally as combination of the parts in a whole (logical division). Consequently there can also be only as many kinds of universal rules (*propositiones majores*) through which the consequence of one judgment from another is mediated.",[]],"jaesche-s03-274":["And upon this is based the division of all syllogisms into categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive.",[]],"jaesche-s03-275":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-276":["Note 1. Syllogisms cannot be divided according to quantity, since every *major* is a rule, and therefore something universal; nor in respect of quality, since it is a matter of indifference whether the conclusion is affirmative or negative; nor finally with regard to modality, since the conclusion is always accompanied by consciousness of necessity, and consequently has the dignity of an apodeictic proposition. Relation alone, therefore, remains as the only possible ground of division of syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-277":["2. Many logicians hold only categorical syllogisms to be ordinary, and the others to be extraordinary. But this is groundless and false. For all three of these kinds are products of functions of reason which are equally correct, and equally essentially distinct from one another.",[]],"jaesche-s03-278":["§ 61. Distinctive difference between categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-279":["What distinguishes the three kinds of syllogism lies in the major premiss. In categorical syllogisms the *major* is a categorical proposition, in hypothetical it is a hypothetical or problematic, and in disjunctive a disjunctive proposition.",[]],"jaesche-s03-280":["§ 62. 1. Categorical syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-281":["In every categorical syllogism there are three principal concepts (*termini*), namely:",[]],"jaesche-s03-282":["1) the predicate in the conclusion, which concept is entitled the major term (*terminus major*), because it has a greater sphere than the subject;",[]],"jaesche-s03-283":["2) the subject (in the conclusion), the concept of which is entitled the minor term (*terminus minor*); and",[]],"jaesche-s03-284":["3) a mediating mark (*nota intermedia*), which is entitled the middle term (*terminus medius*), because through it a mode of knowledge is subsumed under the condition of the rule.",[]],"jaesche-s03-285":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-286":["Note. This distinction of *termini* is found only in categorical syllogisms, because they alone infer through a *terminus medius*. The others infer only through the subsumption of a proposition represented problematically in the *major* and assertorically in the *minor*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-287":["§ 63. Principle of categorical syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-288":["The principle upon which the possibility and validity of all categorical syllogisms rests is this:",[]],"jaesche-s03-289":["What belongs to the mark of a thing belongs also to the thing itself; and what contradicts the mark of a thing contradicts also the thing itself (*nota notae est nota rei ipsius; repugnans notae, repugnat rei ipsi*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-290":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-291":["Note. From the principle just laid down the so-called *dictum de omni et nullo* can easily be deduced; and for that reason it cannot count as the supreme principle either of syllogisms in general or of categorical syllogisms in particular.",[]],"jaesche-s03-292":["Concepts of genus and species are, that is to say, universal marks of all the things which stand under these concepts. The rule therefore holds here: What belongs to or contradicts the genus or species belongs to or contradicts all the objects contained under that genus or species. And this rule is what is entitled the *dictum de omni et nullo*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-293":["§ 64. Rules for categorical syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-294":["From the nature and the principle of categorical syllogisms there follow these rules for them:",[]],"jaesche-s03-295":["1) In every categorical syllogism neither more nor fewer than three principal concepts (*termini*) can be contained; for I have here to combine two concepts (subject and predicate) through a mediating mark.",[]],"jaesche-s03-296":["2) The antecedent propositions or premisses must not both be negative (*ex puris negativis nihil sequitur*); for the subsumption in the minor premiss, as asserting that a mode of knowledge stands under the condition of the rule, must be affirmative.",[]],"jaesche-s03-297":["3) Nor may the premisses both be particular (*particulare*) propositions (*ex puris particularibus nihil sequitur*); for there would then be no rule, that is, no universal proposition, from which a particular mode of knowledge could be inferred.",[]],"jaesche-s03-298":["4) The conclusion always follows the weaker part of the inference, that is, the negative and particular proposition in the premisses, which is entitled the weaker part of the categorical syllogism (*conclusio sequitur partem debiliorem*). Hence, if",[]],"jaesche-s03-299":["5) one of the premisses is a negative proposition, the conclusion must also be negative; and",[]],"jaesche-s03-300":["6) if one premiss is a particular proposition, the conclusion must also be particular.",[]],"jaesche-s03-301":["7) In all categorical syllogisms the *major* must be a universal (*universalis*), the *minor* an affirmative proposition (*affirmans*); and from this it finally follows",[]],"jaesche-s03-302":["8) that the conclusion must follow the major premiss in respect of quality, but the minor premiss in respect of quantity.",[]],"jaesche-s03-303":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-304":["Note. That the conclusion must always follow the negative and particular proposition in the premisses is easily seen.",[]],"jaesche-s03-305":["If I make the minor premiss only particular, and say, ‘Something is contained under the rule’, then in the conclusion I can also say only that the predicate of the rule belongs to something; for I have subsumed no more than this under the rule. And if I have a negative proposition as rule (major premiss), I must make the conclusion negative also. For if the major premiss says, ‘Of everything that stands under the condition of the rule this or that predicate must be denied’, then the conclusion must also deny the predicate of that (subject) which has been subsumed under the condition of the rule.",[]],"jaesche-s03-306":["§ 65. Pure and mixed categorical syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-307":["A categorical syllogism is pure (*purus*) when no immediate inference is mixed in with it, and the regular order of the premisses has not been altered. Otherwise it is entitled impure or mixed (*ratiocinium impurum* or *hybridum*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-308":["§ 66. Mixed syllogisms through conversion of propositions. Figures.",[]],"jaesche-s03-309":["Among mixed inferences are to be reckoned those which arise through the conversion of propositions, and in which, therefore, the position of these propositions is not the regular one. This is the case in the last three so-called figures of the categorical syllogism.",[]],"jaesche-s03-310":["§ 67. Four figures of inference.",[]],"jaesche-s03-311":["By figures are to be understood those four modes of inference whose difference is determined by the particular position of the premisses and of their concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s03-312":["§ 68. Determining ground of their difference: the different position of the middle term.",[]],"jaesche-s03-313":["The middle term, upon whose position everything here properly depends, can occupy either 1) the place of the subject in the major premiss and the place of the predicate in the minor; or 2) the place of the predicate in both premisses; or 3) the place of the subject in both; or finally 4) the place of the predicate in the major premiss and the place of the subject in the minor. By these four cases the difference of the four figures is determined. If S stands for the subject of the conclusion, P for its predicate, and M for the *terminus medius*, the schema for the four figures can be presented in the following table:",[]],"jaesche-s03-314":["{}",[]],"jaesche-s03-315":["§ 69. Rule for the first figure, as the only regular one.",[]],"jaesche-s03-316":["The rule of the first figure is that the *major* be a universal, and the *minor* an affirmative proposition. And since this must be the universal rule of all categorical syllogisms whatsoever, it follows that the first figure is the only regular one. It underlies all the others, and all the others, in so far as they are to have validity, must be reduced to it through conversion of the premisses (*metathesin praemissorum*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-317":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-318":["Note. The first figure can have a conclusion of every quantity and quality. In the other figures there are only conclusions of a certain kind; some of their *modi* are here excluded. This of itself indicates that these figures are not perfect, but that certain restrictions are present in them which prevent the conclusion from holding in all *modi*, as it does in the first figure.",[]],"jaesche-s03-319":["§ 70. Condition of the reduction of the last three figures to the first.",[]],"jaesche-s03-320":["The condition of the validity of the last three figures, under which a correct mode of inference is possible in each of them, comes to this: that the *medius terminus* obtain such a place in the propositions that from it, through immediate inferences (*consequentias immediatas*), its place according to the rules of the first figure can arise. From this the following rules for the last three figures result.",[]],"jaesche-s03-321":["§ 71. Rule of the second figure.",[]],"jaesche-s03-322":["In the second figure the *minor* stands rightly; the *major* must therefore be converted, and in such a way that it remains universal (*universalis*). This is possible only if it is universal negative; if, however, it is affirmative, it must be contraposed. In both cases the conclusion becomes negative (*sequitur partem debiliorem*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-323":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-324":["Note. The rule of the second figure is: What a mark of a thing contradicts, contradicts the thing itself. Here I must first convert, and say: What a mark contradicts, contradicts this mark; or I must convert the conclusion: What a mark of a thing contradicts, the thing itself contradicts; consequently it contradicts the thing.",[]],"jaesche-s03-325":["§ 72. Rule of the third figure.",[]],"jaesche-s03-326":["In the third figure the *major* stands rightly; the *minor* must therefore be converted, yet in such a way that an affirmative proposition results. This, however, is possible only if the affirmative proposition is particular; consequently the conclusion is particular.",[]],"jaesche-s03-327":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-328":["Note. The rule of the third figure is: What belongs to or contradicts a mark belongs to or contradicts some of those under which this mark is contained. Here I must first say: It belongs to or contradicts all that are contained under this mark.",[]],"jaesche-s03-329":["§ 73. Rule of the fourth figure.",[]],"jaesche-s03-330":["If in the fourth figure the *major* is universal negative, it can be converted purely (*simpliciter*), and so likewise the *minor*, as particular; the conclusion is therefore negative. If, on the other hand, the *major* is universal affirmative, it can either only be converted *per accidens* or be contraposed; the conclusion is therefore either particular or negative. If the conclusion is not to be converted (PS changed into SP), there must be a transposition of the premisses (*metathesis praemissorum*) or a conversion (*conversio*) of both.",[]],"jaesche-s03-331":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-332":["Note. In the fourth figure the inference runs: the predicate depends on the *medius terminus*, the *medius terminus* on the subject (of the conclusion), consequently the subject on the predicate. This, however, does not follow at all; at most its converse follows. To make this possible, the *major* must be made the *minor* and *vice versa*, and the conclusion must be converted, since by the first change the *terminus minor* is transformed into the *major*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-333":["§ 74. General results concerning the last three figures.",[]],"jaesche-s03-334":["From the rules given for the last three figures it is evident",[]],"jaesche-s03-335":["1) that in none of them is there a universal affirmative conclusion, but that the conclusion is always either negative or particular;",[]],"jaesche-s03-336":["2) that in each of them an immediate inference (*consequentia immediata*) is mixed in, which, though not expressly indicated, must yet be tacitly understood along with it; and that therefore, for this reason also,",[]],"jaesche-s03-337":["3) all these last three *modi* of inference must be entitled not pure but impure inferences (*ratiocinia hybrida, impura*), since every pure inference can have no more than three principal propositions [Hauptsätze] (*termini*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-338":["§ 75. 2. Hypothetical syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-339":["A hypothetical inference is one which has a hypothetical proposition as its *major*. It therefore consists of two propositions, 1) an antecedent (*antecedens*) and 2) a consequent (*consequens*); and the inference here proceeds either by the *modus ponens* or by the *modus tollens*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-340":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-341":["Note 1. Hypothetical syllogisms therefore have no *terminus medius*; in them the consequence of one proposition from another is merely indicated. In their *major*, that is to say, the consequence of two propositions from each other is expressed, of which the first is a premiss, the second a conclusion. The *minor* is a transformation of the problematic condition into a categorical proposition.",[]],"jaesche-s03-342":["2. From the fact that the hypothetical inference consists of only two propositions, without having a middle term, it can be seen that it is not properly a syllogism, but rather only an immediate inference, to be demonstrated from an antecedent and a consequent, either as to matter or as to form (*consequentia immediata demonstrabilis [ex antecedente et consequente] vel quoad materiam vel quoad formam*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-343":["Every syllogism ought to be a proof. But the hypothetical inference carries with it only the ground of proof. Consequently it is clear from this also that it cannot be a syllogism.",[]],"jaesche-s03-344":["§ 76. Principle of hypothetical inferences.",[]],"jaesche-s03-345":["The principle of hypothetical inferences is the principle of ground: *A ratione ad rationatum; a negatione rationati ad negationem rationis valet consequentia.*",[]],"jaesche-s03-346":["§ 77. 3. Disjunctive syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-347":["In disjunctive inferences the *major* is a disjunctive proposition, and must therefore, as such, have members of division or disjunction.",[]],"jaesche-s03-348":["The inference here proceeds either 1) from the truth of one member of the disjunction to the falsity of the others, or 2) from the falsity of all the members but one to the truth of this one. The former takes place through the *modus ponens* (or *ponendo tollens*), the latter through the *modus tollens* (*tollendo ponens*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-349":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-350":["Note 1. All the members of the disjunction but one, taken together, constitute the contradictory opposite of this one. There is therefore a dichotomy here, according to which, if one of the two is true, the other must be false, and conversely.",[]],"jaesche-s03-351":["2. All disjunctive syllogisms with more than two members of the disjunction are therefore properly polysyllogistic. For all true disjunction can only be *bimembris*, and logical division is also *bimembris*; but for the sake of brevity the *membra subdividentia* are placed among the *membra dividentia*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-352":["§ 78. Principle of disjunctive syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-353":["The principle of disjunctive inferences is the principle of excluded middle:",[]],"jaesche-s03-354":["*A contradictorie oppositorum negatione unius ad affirmationem alterius, a positione unius ad negationem alterius valet consequentia.*",[]],"jaesche-s03-355":["§ 79. Dilemma.",[]],"jaesche-s03-356":["A dilemma is a hypothetico-disjunctive syllogism, or a hypothetical inference whose *consequens* is a disjunctive judgment. The hypothetical proposition whose *consequens* is disjunctive is the major premiss; the minor premiss affirms that the *consequens* is false (*per omnia membra*); and the conclusion affirms that the *antecedens* is false. (*A remotione consequentis ad negationem antecedentis valet consequentia.*)",[]],"jaesche-s03-357":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-358":["Note. The ancients made a great deal of the dilemma, and called this inference *cornutus*. They knew how to drive an opponent into a corner by enumerating every position to which he could turn, and then refuting him on each. They showed him many difficulties in every opinion he adopted. But it is a sophistical artifice not to refute propositions directly, but only to point out difficulties; and this can indeed be done with many things, nay, with most.",[]],"jaesche-s03-359":["If, now, we are prepared at once to declare false everything in which difficulties are found, it is an easy game to reject everything. It is good, certainly, to show the impossibility of the opposite; but there is nevertheless something deceptive in this, if we take the inconceivability of the opposite for its impossibility. Dilemmas, therefore, have much that is captious about them, although they infer correctly. They can be used to defend true propositions, but also to attack true propositions through difficulties raised against them.",[]],"jaesche-s03-360":["§ 80. Formal and concealed syllogisms (*ratiocinia formalia* and *cryptica*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-361":["A formal syllogism is one which not only contains everything required as regards its matter, but is also correctly and completely expressed as regards its form. Opposed to formal syllogisms are the concealed (*cryptica*). Among these may be reckoned all those in which either the premisses are transposed, or one of the premisses is omitted, or finally the middle term alone is combined with the conclusion. A concealed syllogism of the second kind, in which one premiss is not expressed but only thought along with it, is entitled a mutilated syllogism or an enthymeme. Those of the third kind are entitled contracted inferences.",[]],"jaesche-s03-362":["[Dritter Abschnitt. Von den Schlüssen.] III. Inferences of judgment [Schlüsse der Urtheilskraft]. [§§ 81 — 93]",[]],"jaesche-s03-363":["§ 81. Determinant and reflective judgment.",[]],"jaesche-s03-364":["The faculty of judgment is twofold: determinant or reflective judgment. The former proceeds from the universal to the particular, the latter from the particular to the universal. The latter has only subjective validity; for the universal to which it advances from the particular is only empirical universality, a mere analogue of the logical.",[]],"jaesche-s03-365":["§ 82. Inferences of (reflective) judgment.",[]],"jaesche-s03-366":["Inferences of judgment are certain modes of inference by which we pass from particular concepts to universal ones. They are therefore functions not of determinant but of reflective judgment; and consequently they do not determine the object, but only the mode of reflection upon it, in order to arrive at acquaintance [Kenntniß] with it.",[]],"jaesche-s03-367":["§ 83. Principle of these inferences.",[]],"jaesche-s03-368":["The principle underlying inferences of judgment is this: that the many will not agree in one without a common ground, but that what belongs to many in this way will be necessary from a common ground.",[]],"jaesche-s03-369":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-370":["Note. Since such a principle underlies inferences of judgment, they cannot for that reason be regarded as immediate inferences.",[]],"jaesche-s03-371":["§ 84. Induction and analogy: the two modes of inference of judgment.",[]],"jaesche-s03-372":["Judgment, in advancing from the particular to the universal in order to draw universal judgments from experience, and therefore not *a priori* (empirically), infers either from many to all things of a kind, or from many determinations and properties in which things of the same kind agree to the remaining ones, in so far as they belong to the same principle. The former mode of inference is entitled inference by induction, the other inference by analogy.",[]],"jaesche-s03-373":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-374":["Note 1. Induction thus infers from the particular to the universal (*a particulari ad universale*) according to the principle of generalisation: What belongs to many things of a genus belongs also to the rest. Analogy infers from the partial similarity of two things to their total similarity, according to the principle of specification: Things of one genus, of which we know many points of agreement, agree also in the remainder, which we know in some of this genus but do not perceive in others. Induction extends what is empirically given from the particular to the universal in respect of many objects; analogy, on the other hand, extends the given properties of a thing to more properties of the very same thing. One in many, therefore in all: induction. Many in one (which are also in others), therefore also the rest in the same: analogy. Thus, for example, the argument for immortality from the complete development of the natural predispositions of every creature is an inference by analogy.",[]],"jaesche-s03-375":["In inference by analogy, however, identity of the ground (*par ratio*) is not required. By analogy we infer only rational inhabitants of the moon, not men. Nor can we, by analogy, infer beyond the *tertium comparationis*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-376":["2. Every syllogism must yield necessity. Induction and analogy are therefore not syllogisms, but only logical presumptions, or empirical inferences; and through induction we obtain general, but not universal, propositions.",[]],"jaesche-s03-377":["3. The above-mentioned inferences of judgment are useful and indispensable for the extension of our empirical knowledge. But since they yield only empirical certainty, we must employ them with care and caution.",[]],"jaesche-s03-378":["§ 85. Simple and composite syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-379":["A syllogism is entitled simple when it consists of only one syllogism, composite when it consists of several.",[]],"jaesche-s03-380":["§ 86. *Ratiocinatio polysyllogistica.*",[]],"jaesche-s03-381":["A composite inference in which the several syllogisms are combined with one another not by mere co-ordination but by subordination,",[]],"jaesche-s03-382":["that is, as grounds and consequences, is entitled a chain of syllogisms (*ratiocinatio polysyllogistica*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-383":["§ 87. Prosyllogisms and episyllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-384":["In the series of composite inferences we can infer in two ways: either downwards from grounds to consequences, or upwards from consequences to grounds. The former takes place through episyllogisms, the latter through prosyllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-385":["An episyllogism, that is to say, is that inference in the series of inferences whose premiss becomes the conclusion of a prosyllogism, that is, of an inference which has the premiss of the former as its conclusion.",[]],"jaesche-s03-386":["§ 88. Sorites or chain-inference.",[]],"jaesche-s03-387":["An inference consisting of several abbreviated inferences combined with one another into one conclusion is entitled a sorites or chain-inference. It may be either progressive or regressive, according as we ascend from nearer grounds to more remote ones, or descend from more remote grounds to nearer ones.",[]],"jaesche-s03-388":["§ 89. Categorical and hypothetical sorites.",[]],"jaesche-s03-389":["Progressive as well as regressive chain-inferences can in turn be either categorical or hypothetical. The former consist of categorical propositions, as a series of predicates; the latter of hypothetical propositions, as a series of consequences.",[]],"jaesche-s03-390":["§ 90. Fallacy. Paralogism. Sophism.",[]],"jaesche-s03-391":["A syllogism which is false in form, although it has the semblance of a correct inference, is entitled a fallacy (*fallacia*). Such an inference is a paralogism in so far as we thereby deceive ourselves, a sophism in so far as we thereby intentionally seek to deceive others.",[]],"jaesche-s03-392":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-393":["Note. The ancients occupied themselves greatly with the art of devising such sophisms. Many of this kind have therefore arisen; for example, the *sophisma figurae dictionis*, in which the *medius terminus* is taken in different senses; *fallacia a dicto secundum quid ad dictum simpliciter*; *sophisma heterozeteseos*, *elenchi*, *ignorationis*, and the like.",[]],"jaesche-s03-394":["§ 91. Leap in inference.",[]],"jaesche-s03-395":["A leap (*saltus*) in inferring or proving is the combination of one premiss with the conclusion in such a way that the other premiss is omitted. Such a leap is legitimate (*legitimus*) when everyone can easily supply the missing premiss in thought; illegitimate (*illegitimus*) when the subsumption is not clear. In the latter case a remote mark is connected with a thing without an intermediate mark (*nota intermedia*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-396":["§ 92. *Petitio principii*. *Circulus in probando.*",[]],"jaesche-s03-397":["By a *petitio principii* is understood the adoption of a proposition as ground of proof, as being immediately certain, although it still requires proof. And we commit a circle in proof when we make the very proposition which we wished to prove the basis of its own proof.",[]],"jaesche-s03-398":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-399":["Note. The circle in proof is often hard to detect; and this error is usually committed most frequently precisely where proofs are difficult.",[]],"jaesche-s03-400":["§ 93. *Probatio plus* and *minus probans*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-401":["A proof can prove too much, but also too little. In the latter case it proves only a part of what is to be proved; in the former it extends also to what is false.",[]],"jaesche-s03-402":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-403":["Note. A proof which proves too little can be true, and is therefore not to be rejected. But if it proves too much, it proves more than is true, and that is then false. Thus, for example, the argument against suicide, that he who has not given himself life cannot take it from himself either, proves too much; for on this ground we ought not to kill animals either. It is therefore false.",[]],"jaesche-s03-404":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-001":["II. General Doctrine of Method. [Allgemeine Methodenlehre.] [§§ 94 — 98]",[]],"jaesche-s04-002":["§ 94. Manner and method.",[]],"jaesche-s04-003":["All knowledge, and every whole of knowledge, must conform to a rule. (Absence of rule is at the same time unreason.) But this rule is either that of manner (free) or that of method (constraint).",[]],"jaesche-s04-004":["§ 95. Form of science. Method.",[]],"jaesche-s04-005":["Knowledge, as science, must be ordered according to a method. For science is a whole of knowledge as a system, and not merely as an aggregate. It therefore requires systematic knowledge, that is, knowledge framed according to rules deliberately considered.",[]],"jaesche-s04-006":["§ 96. Doctrine of method. Its object and end.",[]],"jaesche-s04-007":["Just as the doctrine of elements in logic has for its content the elements and conditions of the perfection of knowledge, so the general doctrine of method, as the other part of logic, treats of the form of a science in general, or of the manner of proceeding in order to connect the manifold of knowledge into a science.",[]],"jaesche-s04-008":["§ 97. Means for furthering the logical perfection of knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s04-009":["The doctrine of method has to set forth the way in which we attain to the perfection of knowledge. Now one of the most essential logi|cal",[]],"jaesche-s04-010":["perfections of knowledge consists in its distinctness, its thoroughness, and its systematic arrangement into the whole of a science. The doctrine of method will therefore chiefly have to state the means by which these perfections of knowledge are furthered.",[]],"jaesche-s04-011":["§ 98. Conditions of the distinctness of knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s04-012":["The distinctness of modes of knowledge, and their combination into a systematic whole, depends on the distinctness of concepts, both in respect of what is contained in them and with regard to what is contained under them.",[]],"jaesche-s04-013":["Distinct consciousness of the content of concepts is furthered by their exposition and definition; distinct consciousness of their extension, on the other hand, by their logical division. First, then, of the means for furthering the distinctness of concepts in respect of their content.",[]],"jaesche-s04-014":["I. Furtherance of the logical perfection of knowledge through definition, exposition and description of concepts. [§§ 99 — 109]",[]],"jaesche-s04-015":["§ 99. Definition.",[]],"jaesche-s04-016":["A definition is a sufficiently distinct and precisely limited [abgemessener] concept (*conceptus rei adaequatus in minimis terminis, complete determinatus*).",[]],"jaesche-s04-017":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-018":["Note. Definition alone is to be regarded as a logically perfect concept; for in it are united the two most essential perfections of a concept: distinctness, and completeness and precision in distinctness (quantity of distinctness).",[]],"jaesche-s04-019":["§ 100. Analytic and synthetic definition.",[]],"jaesche-s04-020":["All definitions are either analytic or synthetic. The former are definitions of a given concept, the latter definitions of a made concept.",[]],"jaesche-s04-021":["§ 101. Given and made concepts, *a priori* and *a posteriori*.",[]],"jaesche-s04-022":["The given concepts of an analytic definition are given either *a priori* or *a posteriori*; just as the made concepts of a synthetic definition are made either *a priori* or *a posteriori*.",[]],"jaesche-s04-023":["§ 102. Synthetic definitions through exposition or construction.",[]],"jaesche-s04-024":["The synthesis of made concepts, from which synthetic definitions arise, is either that of exposition (of appearances) or that of construction. The latter is the synthesis of concepts made arbitrarily; the former the synthesis of concepts made empirically, that is, from given appearances as their matter (*conceptus factitii vel a priori vel per synthesin empiricam*). Mathematical concepts are arbitrarily made concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s04-025":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-026":["Note. All definitions of mathematical concepts, and also of concepts of experience (if indeed definitions could take place at all in the case of empirical concepts), must therefore be made synthetically. For with concepts of the latter kind also, for example the empirical concepts water, fire, air and the like, I have not to analyse what lies in them, but to learn through experience what belongs to them. All empirical concepts must therefore be regarded as made concepts, whose synthesis, however, is not arbitrary but empirical.",[]],"jaesche-s04-027":["§ 103. Impossibility of empirically synthetic definitions.",[]],"jaesche-s04-028":["Since the synthesis of empirical concepts is not arbitrary but empirical, and as such can never be complete (because in experience we can always discover still more marks of the concept), empirical concepts cannot be defined.",[]],"jaesche-s04-029":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-030":["Note. Only arbitrary concepts, therefore, can be defined synthetically. Such definitions of arbitrary concepts are not only always possible but also necessary, and must precede everything that is said by means of an arbitrary concept. They might also be called declarations, in so far as we thereby declare our thoughts, or give an account of what we understand by a word. This is the case with mathematicians.",[]],"jaesche-s04-031":["§ 104. Analytic definitions through analysis of concepts given *a priori* or *a posteriori*.",[]],"jaesche-s04-032":["All given concepts, whether given *a priori* or *a posteriori*, can be defined only through analysis. For given concepts can be made distinct only by successively making their marks clear. If all the marks of a given concept are made clear, the concept becomes completely distinct; if, further, it contains no more marks than it should, it is at the same time precise, and from this there arises a definition of the concept.",[]],"jaesche-s04-033":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-034":["Note. Since by no test can we become certain whether we have exhausted all the marks of a given concept through complete analysis, all analytic definitions are to be held as uncertain.",[]],"jaesche-s04-035":["§ 105. Expositions and descriptions.",[]],"jaesche-s04-036":["Not all concepts, then, can be defined; nor need they all be defined.",[]],"jaesche-s04-037":["There are approximations to the definition of certain concepts; these are partly expositions (*expositiones*), partly descriptions (*descriptiones*).",[]],"jaesche-s04-038":["The exposition of a concept consists in the connected (successive) representation of its marks, so far as these have been found through analysis.",[]],"jaesche-s04-039":["Description is the exposition of a concept in so far as it is not precise.",[]],"jaesche-s04-040":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-041":["Note 1. We can expound either a concept or experience. The former is done through analysis, the latter through synthesis.",[]],"jaesche-s04-042":["2. Exposition therefore takes place only with given concepts, which are thereby made distinct. It is thereby distinguished from declaration, which is a distinct representation of made concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s04-043":["Since it is not always possible to make the analysis complete, and since, in general, an analysis [Zergliederung] must be incomplete before it becomes complete, an incomplete exposition, as part of a definition, is also a true and serviceable presentation of a concept. Definition here remains always only the idea of a logical perfection which we must seek to attain.",[]],"jaesche-s04-044":["3. Description can find place only in the case of empirically given concepts. It has no determinate rules, and contains only the materials for definition.",[]],"jaesche-s04-045":["§ 106. Nominal and Real Definitions.",[]],"jaesche-s04-046":["By mere explanations of names, or nominal definitions, are to be understood those which contain the meaning that it has been chosen arbitrarily to give to a certain name. They therefore signify only the logical essence of their object, or serve merely to distinguish it from other objects. Explanations of things, or real definitions, on the other hand, are those which suffice for knowledge of the object in respect of its inner determinations, since they set forth the possibility of the object from inner marks [Merkmale].",[]],"jaesche-s04-047":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-048":["Note 1. If a concept is inwardly sufficient to distinguish the thing, it is certainly also outwardly sufficient. If, however, it is not inwardly sufficient, it can still be outwardly sufficient, but only in a certain respect, namely, in the comparison of the *definitum* with other [things]. Unrestricted outer sufficiency is not, however, possible without inner sufficiency.",[]],"jaesche-s04-049":["2. Objects of experience allow only of nominal explanations. Logical nominal definitions of given concepts of understanding are derived from an attribute; real definitions, on the other hand, from the essence of the thing, from the first ground of its possibility. The latter therefore contain that which at all times belongs to the thing, namely, its real essence. Merely negative definitions cannot be entitled real definitions either. For though negative marks may serve quite as well as affirmative ones to distinguish a thing from others, they cannot serve for knowledge of the thing in respect of its inner possibility.",[]],"jaesche-s04-050":["In matters of morals real definitions must always be sought; towards this all our endeavour must be directed. There are real definitions in mathematics, for the definition of an arbitrary [willkürlich] concept is always real.",[]],"jaesche-s04-051":["3. A definition is genetic when it yields a concept through which the object can be presented *a priori in concreto*. Of this kind are all mathematical definitions.",[]],"jaesche-s04-052":["§ 107. Chief Requirements of Definition.",[]],"jaesche-s04-053":["The essential and universal requirements which belong to the perfection of a definition in general may be considered under the four chief moments of quantity, quality, relation and modality:",[]],"jaesche-s04-054":["1) as regards quantity, that is, as concerns the sphere of the definition, the definition and the *definitum* must be reciprocal concepts (*conceptus reciproci*), and the definition must therefore be neither wider nor narrower than its *definitum*;",[]],"jaesche-s04-055":["2) as regards quality, the definition must be a concept that is exhaustive [ausführlich] and at the same time precise;",[]],"jaesche-s04-056":["3) as regards relation, it must not be tautological; that is to say, the marks of the *definitum*, as grounds of knowledge of it, must be distinct from the *definitum* itself; and finally",[]],"jaesche-s04-057":["4) as regards modality, the marks must be necessary, and therefore not such as are added through experience.",[]],"jaesche-s04-058":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-059":["Note. The condition that the generic concept and the concept of the specific difference (*genus* and *differentia specifica*) should together con|stitute the definition",[]],"jaesche-s04-060":["holds only in respect of nominal definitions, [arrived at] in comparison, and not for real definitions, [arrived at] in derivation.",[]],"jaesche-s04-061":["§ 108. Rules for Testing Definitions.",[]],"jaesche-s04-062":["In testing definitions four operations have to be performed. We have, that is, to inquire whether the definition",[]],"jaesche-s04-063":["1) considered as a proposition, is true; whether it",[]],"jaesche-s04-064":["2) considered as a concept, is distinct;",[]],"jaesche-s04-065":["3) whether, as a distinct concept, it is also exhaustive; and finally",[]],"jaesche-s04-066":["4) whether, as an exhaustive concept, it is at the same time determinate, that is, adequate to the thing itself.",[]],"jaesche-s04-067":["§ 109. Rules for Framing Definitions.",[]],"jaesche-s04-068":["The very same operations which belong to the testing of a definition have also to be performed in framing it. To this end, then, seek: 1) true propositions; 2) such propositions as have a predicate which does not already presuppose the concept of the thing; 3) collect a number of these and compare them with the concept of the thing itself, [to see] whether [the definition] is adequate; and finally 4) see to it that one mark does not lie in another, or is not subordinate to it.",[]],"jaesche-s04-069":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-070":["Note 1. These rules hold, as indeed goes without saying, only of analytic definitions. Now since we can never here be certain whether the analysis has been complete, we may put forward the definition only as an attempt, and must employ it only as if it were not a definition. Under this restriction we can still use it as a distinct and true concept, and draw corollaries from its marks. I shall, that is to say, be able to assert that whatever the concept of the *definitum* belongs to, the definition also belongs to; but not, of course, conversely, since the definition does not exhaust the whole *definitum*.",[]],"jaesche-s04-071":["2. To make use of the concept of the *definitum* in the explanation, or to take the *definitum* as basis in the definition, is entitled explaining in a circle (*circulus in definiendo*).",[]],"jaesche-s04-072":["II. Furtherance of the Perfection of Knowledge through the Logical Division of Concepts.[§§ 110 — 120]",[]],"jaesche-s04-073":["§ 110. Concept of Logical Division.",[]],"jaesche-s04-074":["Every concept contains a manifold under it, in so far as this manifold agrees, but also in so far as it differs. The determination of a concept in respect of everything possible that is contained under it, so far as these are opposed to one another, that is, are distinct from one another, is entitled the logical division of the concept. The higher concept is entitled the divided concept (*divisum*), and the lower concepts the members of the division (*membra dividentia*).",[]],"jaesche-s04-075":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-076":["Note 1. To analyse [theilen] a concept and to divide [eintheilen] it are thus very different. In analysing the concept I see what is contained in it (through analysis); in dividing it I consider what is contained under it. Here I divide the sphere of the concept, not the concept itself. So far, then, from the division being an analysis of the concept, the members of the division contain more in them than does the divided concept.",[]],"jaesche-s04-077":["2. We ascend from lower to higher concepts, and afterwards we can descend again from these to lower ones, namely, through division.",[]],"jaesche-s04-078":["§ 111. Universal Rules of Logical Division.",[]],"jaesche-s04-079":["In every division of a concept we have to see to it:",[]],"jaesche-s04-080":["1) that the members of the division exclude one another, or are opposed to one another; further, that they",[]],"jaesche-s04-081":["2) belong under one higher concept (*conceptum communem*); and finally, that they",[]],"jaesche-s04-082":["3) taken all together, make up the sphere of the divided concept, or are equal to it.",[]],"jaesche-s04-083":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-084":["Note. The members of the division must be separated from one another by contradictory opposition, not by mere contrariety (*contrarium*).",[]],"jaesche-s04-085":["§ 112. Codivision and Subdivision.",[]],"jaesche-s04-086":["Different divisions of a concept, made with different purposes in view, are entitled codivisions [Nebeneintheilungen]; and the division of the members of a division is entitled a subdivision (*subdivisio*).",[]],"jaesche-s04-087":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-088":["Note 1. Subdivision can be continued to infinity, but comparatively it may be finite. Codivision also proceeds to infinity, especially in the case of empirical concepts; for who can exhaust all the relations of concepts?",[]],"jaesche-s04-089":["2. Codivision may also be entitled a division according to the diversity of the concepts of the same object (points of view), just as subdivision may be entitled a division of the points of view themselves.",[]],"jaesche-s04-090":["§ 113. Dichotomy and Polytomy.",[]],"jaesche-s04-091":["A division into two members is entitled dichotomy; but if it has more than two members, it is entitled polytomy.",[]],"jaesche-s04-092":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-093":["Note 1. All polytomy is empirical; dichotomy is the only division from principles *a priori*, and therefore the only primary [primitive] division. For the members of the division are to be opposed to one another, and the opposite of any A is nothing more than non-A.",[]],"jaesche-s04-094":["2. Polytomy cannot be taught in logic, for it requires knowledge of the object. Dichotomy, however, requires only the principle of contradiction, without our needing to know the content of the concept which we propose to divide. Polytomy requires intuition, either *a priori*, as in mathematics (for instance, the division of the conic sections), or empirical intuition, as in the description of nature. Division from the principle of *a priori* synthesis does, however, have trichotomy, namely: 1) the concept as the condition, 2) the conditioned, and 3) the derivation of the latter from the former.",[]],"jaesche-s04-095":["§ 114. Different Divisions of Method.",[]],"jaesche-s04-096":["As regards, now, method itself, more especially in the elaboration and treatment of the modes of scientific knowledge, there are various chief kinds of it, which we may here specify according to the following division.",[]],"jaesche-s04-097":["§ 115. 1. Scientific or Popular Method.",[]],"jaesche-s04-098":["The scientific or scholastic method is distinguished from the popular in that the former starts from fundamental and elementary propositions, the latter, on the other hand, from what is familiar and interesting. The former aims at thoroughness, and therefore excludes everything heterogeneous; the latter aims at entertainment.",[]],"jaesche-s04-099":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-100":["Note. These two methods, therefore, differ in kind and not merely in exposition [Vortrag]; and popularity in method is consequently something other than popularity in exposition.",[]],"jaesche-s04-101":["§ 116. 2. Systematic or Fragmentary Method.",[]],"jaesche-s04-102":["The systematic method is opposed to the fragmentary or rhapsodic. If we have thought according to a method, and have then also expressed this method in the exposition, clearly indicating the transition from one proposition to another, we have treated a [body of] knowledge systematically. If, on the other hand, we have indeed thought according to a method, but have not arranged the exposition methodically, such a method is to be entitled rhapsodic.",[]],"jaesche-s04-103":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-104":["Note. Systematic exposition is opposed to fragmentary, just as methodical is opposed to tumultuary. He who thinks metho|dically",[]],"jaesche-s04-105":["can, that is to say, expound either systematically or fragmentarily. Exposition which is outwardly fragmentary but in itself methodical is aphoristic.",[]],"jaesche-s04-106":["§ 117. 3. Analytic or Synthetic Method.",[]],"jaesche-s04-107":["The analytic method is opposed to the synthetic. The former begins from the conditioned and grounded, and proceeds to principles (*a principiatis ad principia*); the latter, on the other hand, proceeds from principles to consequences, or from the simple to the composite. The former might also be entitled the regressive, and the latter the progressive, method.",[]],"jaesche-s04-108":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-109":["Note. The analytic method is also otherwise entitled the method of discovery. For purposes of popularity the analytic method is the more suitable; for the scientific and systematic elaboration of knowledge, the synthetic.",[]],"jaesche-s04-110":["§ 118. 4. Syllogistic and Tabular Method.",[]],"jaesche-s04-111":["The syllogistic method is that according to which a science is expounded in a chain of syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s04-112":["That method is entitled tabular according to which an already completed doctrinal system [Lehrgebäude] is presented in its entire connection.",[]],"jaesche-s04-113":["§ 119. 5. Acroamatic or Erotematic Method.",[]],"jaesche-s04-114":["Method is acroamatic in so far as someone teaches alone; erotematic in so far as he also asks questions. The latter method can in turn be divided into the dialogic or Socratic and the catechetical, according as the questions are directed either to the understanding or merely to the memory.",[]],"jaesche-s04-115":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-116":["Note. We cannot teach erotematically otherwise than through the Socratic dialogue, in which both parties must question each other and also an|swer in turn,",[]],"jaesche-s04-117":["so that it seems as if the pupil were himself also teacher. The Socratic dialogue, that is to say, teaches through questions, by making the learner acquainted with his own principles of reason and sharpening his attention to them. Through ordinary catechizing, however, we cannot teach, but only examine upon what has been taught acroamatically. The catechetical method is therefore valid only for empirical and historical modes of knowledge, the dialogic, on the other hand, for rational modes of knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s04-118":["§ 120. Meditating.",[]],"jaesche-s04-119":["By meditating is to be understood reflection, or methodical thinking. Meditating must accompany all reading and learning; and for this it is required, first, that we institute preliminary inquiries, and then that we bring our thoughts into order, or connect them according to a method.",[]],"jaesche-s04-120":["Logic (1800)",[]]};
